\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US added 172,000 jobs in May as labor market shows signs of resilience\b0\par\par\uc2 Gaya Gupta \par \uc2 489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2026\par 20:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Government figures show unemployment rate at 4.3% amid rising inflation and economic uncertainty from Iran war\par \par \uc2 US employers added 172,000 jobs in May while the country\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment rate held steady at 4.3%, a sign of a resilient labor market despite rising inflation and economic uncertainty brought on by continued conflict in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Despite the positive update on the labor market, US stocks fell sharply by Friday afternoon after a big sell-off of AI chip stocks. The tech-heavy Nasdaq index closed 4% down, the largest single-day drop in over a year. The S&P 500 and and Dow were also down 2.6% and 1.3%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Economists initially predicted there would be about 80,000 new jobs and a steady unemployment rate of 4.3%. Job figures for March and April were also revised up 29,000 and 64,000, respectively, a 93,000 boost compared with initial figures.\par \par \uc2 Job growth was seen in leisure and hospitality, which was boosted by 70,000 jobs in May, including 48,000 jobs in food services and drinking places. Employment in local government and healthcare also rose last month.\par \par \uc2 The new data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  is the latest in a number of reports that have pointed to strong hiring in recent months, despite a strained economy and an increase in inflation.\par \par \uc2 The labor department announced earlier this week that the number of job openings in April increased to 7.6m, while the number of people quitting, laid off and discharged changed little.\par \par \uc2 Private employers added 122,000 jobs in May, according to payroll firm ADP, which found that employers of all sizes and most industries \u8211\'20\'13 with the exception of the information and natural resource sectors \u8211\'20\'13 were hiring.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHiring was more broad-based in May than we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in the last few years,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , ADP\u8217\'20\'19s chief economist, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CThe labor market continues to show sustained momentum going into the summer hiring season.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Economists are predicting that the Fed will hold rates steady at its meeting 16-17 June, but Trump and his advisers have made it clear they expect Warsh to be receptive to their continued calls for rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve got a Warsh Fed now,\u8221\'20\'1D the US treasury secretary, Scott Bessent, said at a news conference last week. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a new day at the Fed \u8230\'20\'26 I had my first breakfast with Chair Warsh this morning, and I believe that he will do the right thing to balance inflation and growth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Economists say even if the chair supports a rate cut, it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely that a majority of the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s 12 voting members would agree. At the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s last meeting in April, just one member voted for lowering the target range for rates.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260605em650035x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
