\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US job creation smashes forecasts in May, fuelling rate hike bets; \u8216\'20\'18no jet fuel shortage in Europe\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 2,825 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2026\par 15:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rolling coverage of the latest economic and financial newsUK house prices fall for third successive month amid Iran war uncertaintyUK shoppers return to high street as warm weather brings respite from shadow of war\par \par \uc2 4.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 US employers added 172,000 jobs in May while the country\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment rate held steady at 4.3%, a sign of a resilient labor market despite rising inflation and economic uncertainty brought on by continued conflict in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Economists initially predicted there would be about 80,000 new jobs and a steady unemployment rate of 4.3%. Job figures for March and April were also revised up 29,000 and 64,000, respectively, a 93,000 boost compared to initial figures.\par \par \uc2 The new data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  is the latest in a number of reports that have pointed to strong hiring in recent months, despite a strained economy and an increase in inflation.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street fell as investors ramped up their bets that US interest rates could be hiked by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 In other news\u8230\'20\'26\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2026/jun/05/jet-fuel-europe-transport-chief-us-jobs-report-uk-house-prices-stock-markets-oil-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-6a2266508f08119910f4939d#block-6a2266508f08119910f4939d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 European Union\u8217\'20\'19s transport chief has insisted}}}\uc2  there are no signs of jet fuel shortages in Europe in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Transport commissioner ApostolosTzitzikostas has told Reuters:\par \par \uc2 4.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 London\u8217\'20\'19s stock market has closed for the week, with the FTSE 100 blue-chip index little chagned at 10,368 points, up 7 points today.\par \par \uc2 Mining stocks fell, while London\u8217\'20\'19s defensive stocks such as tobacco firms and consumer goods makers rallied.\par \par \uc2 The smaller FTSE250 share index fell 1%, despite a roaring performance from computer maker RaspberryPi. Its share surged 27% after lifting its profit forecast this morning.\par \par \uc2 4.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 The sell-off on Wall Street is continuing, with the Nasdaq Composite index now down 2% or -535 points at 26,295 points.\par \par \uc2 3.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Hospitality jobs boom as US prepares for World Cup\par \par \uc2 The men\u8217\'20\'19s World Cup, which kicks off later this month, may have helped drive up hiring in May.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cdxpx4gel1yo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The BBC reckons}}}\uc2  that pubs, bars and restaurants ramped up hiring ahead of the World Cup, contributing to the 70,000 new jobs at hospitality firms last month.\par \par \uc2 3.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump: Market should be up, not down\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump is unhappy that Wall Street is falling.\par \par \uc2 Posting on Truth Social, the US president claims the market should be going up:\par \par \uc2 New York traders aren\u8217\'20\'19t convinced, though. The S&P 500 is now down 0.9%, with the NasdaqComposite down by 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 3.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Precious metal prices are also falling, as the US dollar strengthens.\par \par \uc2 Silver is down 5.4% at $69.87 an ounce, with gold down 2.1% at $4,377 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 3.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks on the slide today include storage firm WesternDigital (-8.7%), SuperMicroComputer (-8.1%), \uc2 Sandisk\uc2  (-7.5%) and Micron (-7.7%).\par \par \uc2 2.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s latest dilemma is whether to celebrate a strong economy or fear higher interest rates, says Rich McDonald, market analyst at investing and trading platform IG:\par \par \uc2 2.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street falls after strong jobs report\par \par \uc2 The US stock market has opened in the red, as traders react to today\u8217\'20\'19s better-than-expected jobs report.\par \par \uc2 With hopes of interest rate cuts this year fading fast, the S&P 500 share index has dropped by 47 points, or 0.6%, in early trading to 7537 points.\par \par \uc2 Technology stocks are among the fallers, as concern grows that the boom in AI-related stocks may be faltering.\par \par \uc2 This has pulled the NasdaqComposite index down by 301 points, or 1.13%, to 26,529 points.\par \par \uc2 2.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Traders fully price in Fed rate hike this year after jobs data\par \par \uc2 Financial traders are now fully pricing in a Federal Reserve interest-rate hike by the end of this year, after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2026/jun/05/jet-fuel-europe-transport-chief-us-jobs-report-uk-house-prices-stock-markets-oil-live-news-updates?page=with%3Ablock-6a22c13a8f08eeba35d81ace#block-6a22c13a8f08eeba35d81ace"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May\u8217\'20\'19s surprisingly strong jobs report}}}\uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 according to \uc2 Bloomberg\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That will not please president Trump, who repeatedly attacked former Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  for not cutting rates faster.\par \par \uc2 Isaac Stell, investment manager at Wealth Club, thinks the markets are right, though. With strong job creation, and inflationary pressure from the Iran war, a rise in US interest rates does look more likely\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 Stell explains:\par \par \uc2 1.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds are under pressure as investors conclude that early rate cuts are highly unlikely.\par \par \uc2 This has pushed up the yield, or interest rate, on US two-year Treasury bills to 4.147%, the highest since February 2025, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 1.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Such strong jobs growth will make it tricky for America\u8217\'20\'19s new top central banker to push for interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at \uc2 Principal Asset Management\uc2 , says KevinWarsh may find himself considering raising interest rates later this year, despite DonaldTrump\u8217\'20\'19s demands for lower borrowing costs:\par \par \uc2 1.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 "This is a hawkish jobs report"\par \par \uc2 This strong US jobs report makes interest rate cuts less likely, explains WaleedSaid, technical analyst from GivTrade:\par \par \uc2 1.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Where US jobs were created last month\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a breakdown of the sectors that hired more US workers last month:\par \par \uc2 * Leisure and hospitality added 70,000 jobs in May, including 48,000 new roles at food services and drinking places.\par \par \uc2 * Employment in local government rose by 55,000.\par \par \uc2 * Health care added 35,000 jobs in May, including 26,000 in ambulatory health care.\par \par \uc2 * Social assistance employment rose by 12,000 in May\par \par \uc2 * Employment in mining, quarrying, and oil and gas extraction increased by 5,000\par \par \uc2 But\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 * Financial activities employment declined by 22,000 in May and is down by 107,000 since a recent peak in May 2025. Over the month, job losses occurred in insurance carriers and related activities (-11,000) and commercial banking (-3,000).\par \par \uc2 Employment levels were broadly unchanged in transportationandwarehousing, construction, manufacturing, wholesaletrade, retailtrade, information, professionalandbusinessservices, and other services.\par \par \uc2 1.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US unemployment rate held at 4.3 percent in May.\par \par \uc2 1.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 US economy adds more jobs than expected in May\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: the US economy added more jobs than forecast last month.\par \par \uc2 Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 172,000 in May, the US Bureau of Labor Statistics has reported, with gains in leisure and hospitality, local government, and health care.\par \par \uc2 Employment in financial activities declined, though, the BLS reports.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s much stronger than the 85,000 new jobs which economists had predicted for May.\par \par \uc2 Jobs data for the previous two months has been revised higher too, the BLS adds:\par \par \uc2 Those revisions mean employment in March and April combined was 93,000 higher than previously reported \u8211\'20\'13 encouraging news for US workers, and the White House, but something which may make US interest rate cuts less likely\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 12.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 EU's trade deficit with China is 'clearly unsustainable', says \u352\'01\'60ef\u269\'01\'0Dovi\u269\'01\'0D\par \par \uc2 The EU trade commissioner has said the growing deficit with China is unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 Speaking in Brussels, Maro\u353\'01\'61 \u352\'01\'60ef\u269\'01\'0Dovi\u269\'01\'0D described the mood among commissioners who met for a China debate last Friday ahead of a leaders summit on 18 June.\par \par \uc2 \u352\'01\'60ef\u269\'01\'0Dovi\u269\'01\'0D told the Brussels and European Policy Centre conference on economy security:\par \par \uc2 But \u352\'01\'60ef\u269\'01\'0Dovi\u269\'01\'0D said they would look at re-engaging with China.\par \par \uc2 \u352\'01\'60ef\u269\'01\'0Dovi\u269\'01\'0D also pointed out that \u8220\'20\'1COpenness has to be reciprocated by your partner\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 11.34am BST\par \par \uc2 The contraction in the eurozone\u8217\'20\'19s economy in the first quarter of this year is awkward timing for the European Central Bank, which is expected to raise interest rates next week.\par \par \uc2 Economists predict a rate hike from the ECB, to fight inflationary pressures from the Middle East conflict.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Unicredit explain:\par \par \uc2 11.32am BST\par \par \uc2 Despite the inflationary pressures from the Iran war, Kazakhstan\u8217\'20\'19s central bank has cut the country\u8217\'20\'19s key interest rate to 17% from 18%.\par \par \uc2 The NationalBankofKazakhstan eased monetary policy after its forecast for inflation this year was revised down to 9-11%, from 9.5-11.5%.\par \par \uc2 10.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Eurozone on brink of technical recession after Ireland's GDP plunges\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: the eurozone is on the brink of a technical recession, due to a slump in activity among multinationals based in Ireland.\par \par \uc2 Statistics body eurostat has reported that eurozone GDP fell by 0.2% in the first quarter of this year, following a rise of 0.2% in the fourth quarter of 2025. A technical recession is two quarterly falls in GDP in a row.\par \par \uc2 The decline was due to a 12.1% slump in Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s GDP, according to official data released yesterday. However, that was caused by a 27.1% slump across Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s multinational-dominated sectors, as a surge of demand for pharmaceutical products during 2025 unwound.\par \par \uc2 Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s modified domestic demand (MDD), a better measure of its domestic economy rose by 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 Eurostat, though, uses GDP, so here we are.\par \par \uc2 It also reports that Denmark (+1.9%) recorded the fastest quarterly, followed by Estonia and Malta (both +1.1%). At the bottom of the growth table, Ireland were followed by Lithuania (-0.3%), Sweden (-0.2%) and France (-0.1%).\par \par \uc2 Eurostat had initially estimated last month that eurozone GDP rose by 0.1% in Q1 2026, before Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s GDP slump hit\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.49am BST\par \par \uc2 10.02am BST\par \par \uc2 India is set to launch a new high-ethanol fuel blend in the market, its latest effort to diversify supplies and reduce the country\u8217\'20\'19s reliance on imported oil, Bloomberg reports.\par \par \uc2 Oil minister HardeepPuri is due to introduce the fuel today at an Indian Oil Corp.\u8217\'20\'19s outlet in New Delhi. It will initially sold at 50 pumps in the country.\par \par \uc2 The South Asian nation currently sells 20% ethanol-blended gasoline, or E20, across its retail fuel network. The new blend, called E85, will contain about 85% ethanol and pollute less than its conventional counterparts. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-06-05/india-challenges-its-oil-dependence-with-new-ethanol-heavy-fuel?srnd=homepage-uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 9.40am BST\par \par \uc2 A majority of UK firms are expecting to hike their prices to pass on the cost of higher energy, but profits are still expected to be squeezed.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\u8217\'20\'19s latest poll of chief financial officers at small, medium and large UK businesses has found that 57% of firms expected to increase their prices, while 68% of firms expected their profit margins to be lower.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/decision-maker-panel/2026/may-2026"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Decision Makers Panel }}}\uc2 has also found that bosses expect to hand out smaller pay rises this year. They reported that annual wage growth was running at 4.2%, while expected year-ahead wage growth is only forecast to be 3.4%.\par \par \uc2 9.26am BST\par \par \uc2 UN: food prices stable in May\par \par \uc2 After rising in recent months, global food commodity prices stabilised in May.\par \par \uc2 The UN\u8217\'20\'19s Food and Agriculture Organisation has reported that its Food Price Index was stable last month, showing that the cost of a basket of food commodities dipped by 0.2% in May.\par \par \uc2 There were some notable price rises, though. The Sugar Price Index rose by 7.5% in the month, blamed on fears that supplies will tighten in coming months. Cereal prices rose by 2.6%, on expectations of weak harvests in major exporters, including the US, and higher fuel and fertilizer costs.\par \par \uc2 Meat prices were little changed, while vegetableoil and dairyprices fell in the month.\par \par \uc2 8.57am BST\par \par \uc2 The jump in jet fuel costs has prompted AmericanAirlines to join the list of carriers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/american-airlines-california-route-cuts-jet-fuel-iran-war/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 temporarily cutting routes}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/american-airlines-california-route-cuts-jet-fuel-iran-war/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 On Wednesday, }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/american-airlines-california-route-cuts-jet-fuel-iran-war/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 American}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/american-airlines-california-route-cuts-jet-fuel-iran-war/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  told CBS News}}}\uc2  that it has adjusted schedules for \u8220\'20\'1Cselect routes\u8221\'20\'1D in August and September, due to elevated fuel costs.\par \par \uc2 The routes affected include flights from LosAngeles to Cleveland, Columbus, Pittsburgh, and WashingtonDulles, and from Charlotte to Ontario, and to Sacramento.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.57am BST\par \par \uc2 8.18am BST\par \par \uc2 Raspberry Pi shares hit record high after profit upgrade\par \par \uc2 Shares in UK low-cost computer firm Raspberry Pi have hit a record high, after it lifted its profit forecasts this morning.\par \par \uc2 Raspberry Pi\u8217\'20\'19s shares are up 14% to 937p, their highest level since the Cambridge-based company floated on the London stock market in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Raspberry Pi told the City that trading in the first half of this year has been strong, with profitability \u8220\'20\'1Cmaterially ahead\u8221\'20\'1D of the first half of 2025, and that it has been running down stockpiles of memory chips (DRAMs) before prices surged this year.\par \par \uc2 It told shareholders:\par \par \uc2 It added that earnings this year are on track to be \u8220\'20\'1Csignificantly ahead of current market expectations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Demand for RaspberryPi\u8217\'20\'19s computers jumped this year after technology experts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/aleabitoreddit/status/2024108923658084577?lang=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found }}}\uc2 that they were a good choice to run {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/02/openclaw-viral-ai-agent-personal-assistant-artificial-intelligence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the OpenClaw AI chatbot}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 8.06am BST\par \par \uc2 The oil price is little changed this morning, with Brent crude trading around $95 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Susannah Streeter, chief investment strategist at Wealth Club, says:\par \par \uc2 7.59am BST\par \par \uc2 South Korea's KOSPI index tumbles 5%\par \par \uc2 Sentiment in risk markets across Asia has soured today, as investors worry that the rally in technology stocks may be stalling.\par \par \uc2 South Korea\u8217\'20\'19s KOSPI share index has dropped by 5% today (trimming its gains in 2026 to around 90%!), as a drop in chip shares on Wall Street yesterday ripples across markets.\par \par \uc2 Chris Weston, head of research at Pepperstone, says:\par \par \uc2 7.47am BST\par \par \uc2 A second takeover battle has ended without success for the pursuer.\par \par \uc2 US-based ApolloGlobalManagement has said it does not intend to make a firm offer for British thermal processing services company Bodycote, following talks between the two sides.\par \par \uc2 7.44am BST\par \par \uc2 William Hill owner to be taken over\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a little takeover drama in the City of London this morning: the company behind bookmaker William Hill is being acquired.\par \par \uc2 Evoke has agreed to be taken over by US casino operator Bally\u8217\'20\'19sIntralot, in a deal which values Evoke at \u163\'00\'A3243.1m.\par \par \uc2 Bally\u8217\'20\'19s\par \par \uc2 Intralot says the deal will create a \u8220\'20\'1Cglobal gaming and lottery champion\u8221\'20\'1D, with \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant scale and relevance in Europe\u8217\'20\'19s largest and most attractive gaming jurisdictions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The two sides have been talking for weeks, and Bally\u8217\'20\'19sIntralot had been given a deadline of 5pm next Monday to reach a deal or walk away.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.46am BST\par \par \uc2 7.35am BST\par \par \uc2 UK house prices dip for third month running\par \par \uc2 UK house prices have dipped again, as the jump in borrowing costs since the Middle East conflict began hits demand.\par \par \uc2 Lender Halifax has reported that house prices edged down -0.1% in May, the third monthly drop in a row after a 0.5% fall in March, and a 0.1% drop in April.\par \par \uc2 That pulled the average price, on Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s index, down to \u163\'00\'A3298,806, from \u163\'00\'A3299,251 in April.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis, prices were 0.5% higher than in May 2025.\par \par \uc2 Amanda\par \par \uc2 Bryden, head of mortgages at Halifax, says:\par \par \uc2 7.24am BST\par \par \uc2 UK shoppers return to high street\par \par \uc2 Greater numbers of UK consumers went shopping last month as spring sunshine brought welcome relief to retailers, which have faced a squeeze on spending since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/us-israel-war-on-iran"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US-Israel war on Iran}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Figures from the British Retail Consortium (BRC) and a separate survey by the accountancy firm BDO showed a bounce-back in footfall during May, reversing a sharp decline in April.\par \par \uc2 The recovery coincided with consumer confidence surveys showing a rise in May as shoppers began to get over the sharp rise in petrol and diesel prices linked to the Middle East conflict, which began in late February.\par \par \uc2 7.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: EU sees no jet fuel shortage amid price surge, transport chief says\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 With summer approaching, holidaymakers have been growing nervous that the Middle East crisis might leave airlines short of fuel.\par \par \uc2 But despite the lack of progress to reopen the strait of Hormuz, the European Union\u8217\'20\'19s transport chief has insisted there are no signs of jet fuel shortages in Europe in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Transport commissioner ApostolosTzitzikostas has told Reuters:\par \par \uc2 Tzitzikostas pointed out that current high jet fuel prices have prompted airlines to cut uneconomic routes (an example of the demand destruction created by high energy costs), saying:\par \par \uc2 In May, airlines cut two million airline seats from their schedules, or less than 2% of global aviation capacity.\par \par \uc2 Looking further ahead, Tzitzikostas suggested that the situation would be \u8220\'20\'1Cvery difficult\u8221\'20\'1D by the end of the year if Middle Eastern supplies remained disrupted.\par \par \uc2 Back in April, the head of the International Energy Agency said Europe has only six weeks of jet fuel left before shortages will hit.\par \par \uc2 Seven weeks on, flights continue \u8211\'20\'13 as Tzitzikostas points out \u8211\'20\'13 but airlines have been raising ticket prices to pass on the cost of higher fuel, and also to dampen demand.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 7am BST: Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s UK house price index for May\par \par \uc2 * 8.30am BST: UN\u8217\'20\'19s FAO Food Price Index\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm BST: US non-farm payroll jobs report for May\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260605em65001bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
