\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump signs order to make it easier to fire 8,000 highly paid federal workers\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato and agencies \par \uc2 602 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2026\par 10:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Order strips job protections from workers earning up to about $200,000 a year and deemed to be \u8216\'20\'18influencing\u8217\'20\'19 policy\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  has signed an executive order making it easier to fire thousands of the best-paid workers in the \uc2 US government\uc2  aspart of a broader drive by his administration to overhaul the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 The order, released by the White House and the office of personnel management (OPM) on Wednesday, strips job protections from a mostly senior group of federal workers \u8211\'20\'13 about 8,000 employees \u8211\'20\'13 earning up to almost $200,000 a year, and who are deemed to be \u8220\'20\'1Cinfluencing\u8221\'20\'1D government policy.\par \par \uc2 In a call previewing the move, Scott Kupor, director of the OPM, which oversees the government\u8217\'20\'19s human resources policies, said the administration needs to employ people willing and able to carry out orders to achieve the administration\u8217\'20\'19s policy priorities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can have any political views, but if you allow those views to basically interfere with your willingness to actually carry out lawful orders and policy directives with the administration, then this provides a mechanism obviously for people in those agencies to be able to be removed effectively at will,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 About 348,000 employees, over 11% of its overall workforce, have left the federal government since October 2024.\par \par \uc2 The latest order shows Trump is persisting in his efforts to discipline and fire career employees whom he sees as undermining his political goals, a year after billionaire {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/elon-musk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elon Musk}}}\uc2  left his post overseeing an effort to slash government spending and payrolls.\par \par \uc2 Trump believes his agenda was hampered by career federal workers who opposed his policies during his first term.\par \par \uc2 During Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first term, his administration attempted to reclassify federal employees to \u8220\'20\'1Cat will\u8221\'20\'1D under schedule F, but the rule was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nteu.org/schedulef"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rescinded}}}\uc2  by the Biden administration before it took full effect.\par \par \uc2 Labor union leaders have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/21/labor-union-lawsuit-trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued }}}\uc2 the reclassification serves as a step backward to the 19th-century spoils system, where jobs were given to loyalists rather than based on merit.\par \par \uc2 The number of workers affected by the order is well below a ceiling estimate of up to 50,000 who could have been subject to new rules. Senior administration officials on the call said Trump could expand the grouping but had no immediate plans to do so.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Trump-Vance administration\u8217\'20\'19s attempts to dismantle civil service protections would make it easier to purge experienced public servants,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Skye Perryman\uc2 , president and CEO of Democracy Forward, in a statement on the executive order. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen government experts can be fired without cause, it\u8217\'20\'19s not just federal workers who are harmed \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s the people across the country who rely on these essential services every day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democracy Forward is representing several federal worker unions and their allies in a lawsuit, filed in January, to strip civil service protection from thousands more workers. Federal judges paused the litigation while the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  finalized changes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a blatant attempt to corrupt the federal government by eliminating employees\u8217\'20\'19 due process rights so they can be fired for political reasons,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , president of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , in a statement on the executive order.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThousands of employees who were hired under the nonpartisan, professional civil service will be converted to a new hiring schedule where they can be fired \u8216\'20\'18at will\u8217\'20\'19 by political appointees or other overseers with essentially no procedural or appeal safeguards that have long protected the integrity of government operations.\u8221\'20\'1DReuters contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260604em640015u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
