\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We were attacked as bad Jews\u8217\'20\'19: Columbia faculty who supported Gaza protests file claims with Trump\u8217\'20\'19s antisemitism fund\b0\par\par\uc2 Alice Speri \par \uc2 1,198 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2026\par 08:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Faculty who support Palestinian rights are applying for compensation, claiming they faced harassment as Jews for their positions\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  reached a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/23/columbia-university-220m-trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 settlement}}}\uc2  with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  last year, the deal included a $21m fund to compensate Jewish employees for an allegedly hostile work environment due to heated protests against Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 This week, as the window to file claims with the \uc2 Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  came to a close, several Jewish faculty members filed claims to say they had experienced harassment as Jews on campus \u8211\'20\'13 but probably not on grounds Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 EEOC\uc2  intended.\par \par \uc2 Inhalf a dozen filings shared with the Guardian, excerpts of which were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sites.google.com/view/columbiauniversityantisemitism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 also made public}}}\uc2 , faculty alleged that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/columbia-university"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Columbia}}}\uc2  indeed enabled a hostile climate for Jews. But they argued that the hostility they faced stemmed from their support for Palestinian rights and student protests. They criticized what they view as Columbia\u8217\'20\'19s implication that all Jews identify with the state of Israel as \u8220\'20\'1Ctextbook antisemitism\u8221\'20\'1D, and argued that by repressing pro-Palestinian speech in the name of Jewish safety, the university turned Jews into \u8220\'20\'1Cscapegoats\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI no longer consider \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  a safe place to work for Jews who dare to dissent from the political agenda of its most ardently pro-Israel and anti-Palestinian donors and trustees,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Joseph Howley, a classics professor, in his claim.\par \par \uc2 In the complaints and in interviews with the Guardian, Jewish faculty described being doxed, followed, spat on, screamed at in meetings, accused of professional misconduct and, in some cases, investigated by the university because of their advocacy. They wrote that they had been targeted in person, by phone and online, and received death threats.\par \par \uc2 The class settlement was described by the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eeoc.gov/newsroom/largest-eeoc-public-settlement-almost-20-years-columbia-university-agrees-pay-21-million"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the largest}}}\uc2  public settlement in nearly two decades, to be disbursed to \u8220\'20\'1Cemployees who may have experienced antisemitism on Columbia\u8217\'20\'19s campus\u8221\'20\'1D following \uc2 Hamas\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attack on Israel on 7 October 2023, and Israel\u8217\'20\'19s enduring war in Gaza. It came in addition to a $200m fine and several measures the university agreed to in order to end its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/29/columbia-middle-east-department-trump-edward-said"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 months-long dispute}}}\uc2  with the administration. Columbia drew {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/24/columbia-university-deal-white-house-reactions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 widespread criticism}}}\uc2  for \u8220\'20\'1Ccapitulating\u8221\'20\'1D to the president in the deal.\par \par \uc2 Howley wrote in his complaint that since speaking in defense of his students in the early days of the protests, he had been subjected to endless harassment, as well as disciplinary charges over his role mediating between student protesters and the administration. \u8220\'20\'1CWe spoke as Jews about the University and how we thought it should protect its interests; we were attacked as Jews, or as bad Jews, or as fake Jews, by people who carried the University\u8217\'20\'19s authority or knew they had its implicit support,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis singling Jews out for special treatment is dangerous and encourages antisemitism,\u8221\'20\'1D said Howley. He said he felt he had an obligation to draw attention to the climate at Columbia because Muslim and Arab colleagues were not given the same chance.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 EEOC\uc2  fund invokes Title VII of the 1964 Civil Rights Act, which prohibits employment discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex and national origin. But as with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/11/campus-investigations-professors-gaza-antisemitism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other civil rights provisions}}}\uc2 , the administration has sought to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/01/trump-administration-civil-rights-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exploit the legislation}}}\uc2  to fit its political agenda.\par \par \uc2 Columbia did not respond to a request for comment. A notice provided by the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  last year to potentially eligible employees claimed that Columbia had engaged in unlawful employment practices by subjecting employees to harassment based on \u8220\'20\'1Ctheir religion (Jewish), race (Jewish) and/or national origin (Israeli)\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 People who filed claims were asked questions about their experiences on campus, including whether they had been exposed to \u8220\'20\'1Cantisemitic or anti-Israeli protests, gatherings, or demonstrations that made you feel threatened, harassed, or were otherwise disruptive to your working environment\u8221\'20\'1D, according to screengrabs of the online questionnaire. A spokesperson for the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  did not say what criteria it would employ to evaluate the complaints but said that the response was \u8220\'20\'1Crobust\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CEligible claimants will start to receive notifications as the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  and claims administrator work through the submissions,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 Some of the people who filed claims on Tuesday said the university did nothing to protect them and in some cases contributed to the hostility. Several noted that after two dozen Jewish faculty members signed an April 2024 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://academeblog.org/2024/04/15/open-letter-from-jewish-faculty-to-columbia-university-president-minouche-shafik/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter}}}\uc2  condemning the university\u8217\'20\'19s response to student protests, Columbia\u8217\'20\'19s antisemitism taskforce falsely claimed in a report that they had called \u8220\'20\'1Cfor an end to the state of Israel\u8221\'20\'1D, listing the faculty\u8217\'20\'19s names in a footnote and prompting a torrent of abuse against them. (The taskforce later edited the report but issued no apology or retraction, and the old version continues to circulate.)\par \par \uc2 Many of the employees who filed claims described being called \u8220\'20\'1Ckapos\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1CNazis\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1CHamas sympathizers\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cself-hating Jews\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy Jewishness demands that I honor my ancestors and the only way I know to do that is to oppose war, violence and injustice and fight for \u8216\'20\'18never again for anyone\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D Marianne Hirsch, a scholar of antisemitism and the daughter of Holocaust survivors, wrote in her claim. She cited the Jewish principle of tikkun olam, or repairing the world, as a reason for her speaking out. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the only acceptable way to be Jewish is to support Israel unconditionally, there is no longer a way for me to be Jewish on Columbia\u8217\'20\'19s campus.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 EY Zipris, an adjunct professor at Columbia\u8217\'20\'19s Teachers College who was raised in a Hasidic ultra-Orthodox community, wrote in her claim that her parents, including her Israeli mother, opposed Zionism on religious grounds. \u8220\'20\'1CHad my profoundly observant parents voiced those same perspectives on Columbia\u8217\'20\'19s campus today they would have been called antisemitic and pilloried for their beliefs, just as I have been for mine,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CI was on numerous occasions insulted, belittled, and verbally attacked for standing publicly and unapologetically as a Jewish American, often while holding signs in both Hebrew and English calling for the safety of students and colleagues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The people who spoke with the Guardian expressed doubts the commission would recognize their perspective; some noted they saw the claim process as an opportunity to make a statement.\par \par \uc2 Faculty said there were real incidents of antisemitism stemming from the protests, but categorically rejected accusations that the protests were antisemitic across the board. Alisa Solomon, a journalism professor, said she filed her claim in an effort to \u8220\'20\'1Cintervene in a false narrative\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 James Schamus, a film producer and professor at Columbia\u8217\'20\'19s school of the arts, last year wrote a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lithub.com/where-is-my-antisemitism-money-a-columbia-professors-letter-to-the-university-president/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cheeky critique}}}\uc2  of the fund titled \u8220\'20\'1CWhere is my antisemitism money?\u8221\'20\'1D He said in his filing with the EEOC that he would donate the money should he receive a payout.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome of us may take our settlement money and, say, donate it to the Friends of the [Israel Defense Forces], and others may use it towards a Florida timeshare,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CI have pledged to donate mine to the fight for a just peace in Israel/Palestine and the protection of those fighting for that peace here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260604em64000it\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
