\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Colorado governor vetoes block on surveillance pricing as other states push for bans\b0\par\par\uc2 Sanya Mansoor \par \uc2 871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2026\par 08:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Consumer advocates decry Democrat \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  for \u8216\'20\'18choosing to side with dominant corporations\u8217\'20\'19 over workers\par \par \uc2 Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s governor vetoed a bill on Tuesday that would have banned companies from using surveillance pricing to set workers\u8217\'20\'19 wages and prices for consumer goods.\par \par \uc2 The measure would have been the strongest in the nation against algorithmic pricing. While Maryland became the first state to approve a law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/29/maryland-grocery-stores-ban-surveillance-pricing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning}}}\uc2  surveillance pricing in grocery stores in April, Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s proposed measure was more expansive.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  wrote in a public letter explaining his veto that he found the legislation to be overly broad, and said it would \u8220\'20\'1Cinadvertently capture innocuous uses of technology that in no way harms \u8211\'20\'13 and indeed benefits \u8211\'20\'13 consumers and workers\u8221\'20\'1D, echoing business owners\u8217\'20\'19 major concern with the bill, which was supported by progressive groups. He said the bill would \u8220\'20\'1Cpunish differentially lower prices, not just higher prices\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Consumer advocates are unhappy with the veto. \u8220\'20\'1CGovernor Polis had an opportunity to stand with working Coloradans, but instead chose to side with the dominant corporations using invasive surveillance data to pick their pockets,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pat Garofalo, director of state and local policy at the American Economic Liberties Project.\par \par \uc2 Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s bill proposed banning companies from using algorithms, powered by artificial intelligence or otherdata-processing techniques, to set custom prices or wages based on the collection of an individual\u8217\'20\'19s information. This data could include everything from where an individual lives and what they have bought in the past, to their financial status, travel habits and affiliations.\par \par \uc2 Critics of surveillance pricing say that companies exploit this data to charge buyers the most that they are willing to pay, and give workers the lowest amount they are willing to accept. Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s measure also included exemptions for certain discounts tied to loyalty programs and transparent markdowns for students and senior citizens.\par \par \uc2 This is the second time in 12 months that Polis has blocked a bill focused on surveillance pricing; in 2025, he vetoed a measure that would have{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://coloradosun.com/2025/05/29/jared-polis-vetoes-bill-rent-setting-software-algorithms-realpage/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  banned landlords}}}\uc2  from using rent-setting algorithms.\par \par \uc2 Surveillance pricing bans grow in popularity across US\par \par \uc2 Many states, including Illinois, California, Massachusetts and New Jersey, are also considering bills that would regulate surveillance pricing. Connecticut\u8217\'20\'19s legislature {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cga.ct.gov/asp/cgabillstatus/cgabillstatus.asp?selBillType=Bill&which_year=2026&bill_num=4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approved}}}\uc2  a sweeping consumer privacy bill that included new rules for surveillance pricing in May. The measure bans companies setting individualized prices for their goods based on consumer data.\par \par \uc2 In New York, the state attorney general is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ag.ny.gov/press-release/2026/attorney-general-james-calls-passage-legislation-protect-new-yorkers-predatory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rallying}}}\uc2  support for a ban on surveillance pricing, and a bill that would do so has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/02/nyregion/data-centers-redistricting-ny.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed the state senate}}}\uc2 , but not the assembly; last year, New York enacted a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/protecting-new-yorkers-secret-online-price-hikes-governor-hochul-announces-nation-leading"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 transparency-focused law}}}\uc2  that forces companies to disclose when they use personal data to set individualized prices determined by an algorithm.\par \par \uc2 Maryland became the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/29/maryland-grocery-stores-ban-surveillance-pricing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first state}}}\uc2  to ban surveillance pricing in April, though that measure was limited to prices for grocery store items and was criticized by many consumer advocates for being riddled with industry carveouts.\par \par \uc2 Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s surveillance pricing bill was larger in scope, as it applied to all sorts of companies across industries, and covered wages, too. It would have prevented ride-share firms such as \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  from setting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketplace.org/story/2023/04/10/how-ride-hail-companies-use-data-to-pay-drivers-less"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 individualized wages}}}\uc2  for drivers based on data they collect about them, as documented in a 2023 study.\par \par \uc2 Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s measure had also won over many critics of Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s law, who feared that latter\u8217\'20\'19s legislation was watered down by lobbying efforts.\par \par \uc2 Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s measure, unlike Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s proposal, did not crack down on other ways companies may try to achieve the same effect as surveillance pricing, says McBrien, with the \uc2 Electronic Privacy Information Center\uc2  (Epic). Under Maryland\u8217\'20\'19s law, a company could raise its prices for everyone, and then offer individualized discounts \u8211\'20\'13 but Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s law addressed this loophole, McBrien says.\par \par \uc2 Critics of Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s bill agreed with the governor in characterizing the rules as overly broad; they argued it would disrupt competitive markets and open the door to unnecessary litigation. The Travel Technology Association, which represents online travel agencies and short-term rental platforms, called for a narrower definition of \u8220\'20\'1Csurveillance data\u8221\'20\'1D and testified through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leg.colorado.gov/summary_attachments/44670/download"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 written comment}}}\uc2  that the measure would \u8220\'20\'1Cprohibit pricing practices that are transparent, pro-competitive, and beneficial to consumers \u8211\'20\'13 while exposing travel platforms to litigation exposure that bears no relationship to the harms the bill identifies\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  (\uc2 FTC\uc2 ) has documented examples of surveillance pricing in stores selling clothing, beauty products, home goods and hardware. Under the Biden administration, the \uc2 FTC\uc2  released an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/01/ftc-surveillance-pricing-study-indicates-wide-range-personal-data-used-set-individualized-consumer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 initial study}}}\uc2  that indicates companies use a wide range of personal data when setting individualized prices for consumers.\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely the current administration will crack down on surveillance pricing, given that the current FTC chair, Andrew Ferguson, characterized the previous administration\u8217\'20\'19s report as a rush job. Consumer advocates say the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s inaction adds to the urgency of states needing to regulate surveillance pricing.\par \par \uc2 On 18 May, a bipartisan group of 16 state attorneys general {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ncdoj.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/State-AG-Comment-Letter-re-FTC-ANPRM.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote to the FTC}}}\uc2  about online food delivery fees, asking the agency to \u8220\'20\'1Caddress unfair and deceptive pricing practices across the economy\u8221\'20\'1D, including surveillance pricing.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260604em64000ih\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
