\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 White House proposes NDAs for federal workers to crack down on leaks to journalists\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato and agencies \par \uc2 623 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 May 2026\par 13:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 OPM releases draft NDA designed for federal agencies to use with new and existing employees\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s administration on Tuesday proposed asking federal employees to sign nondisclosure agreements with the goal of preventing them from sharing confidential information with journalists.\par \par \uc2 The office of personnel management (OPM), the human resources office for the \uc2 US government\uc2 , released a draft nondisclosure agreement designed for federal agencies to use with new and existing employees. Under the draft agreement, the administration could pursue civil and criminal penalties against employees who violate it. The \uc2 US government\uc2  would be entitled to all \u8220\'20\'1Croyalties\u8221\'20\'1D that employees receive from disclosing information that violates the agreement, according to the draft.\par \par \uc2 The OPM did not immediately offer further explanation.\par \par \uc2 The draft form is the latest step in the president\u8217\'20\'19s effort to exert more control over \uc2 US government\uc2  workers and the flow of information to the public.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis move is rooted in concerns that unauthorized disclosures of sensitive government information are disrupting agency operations and eroding trust across government,\u8221\'20\'1D an OPM spokesperson, McLaurine Pinover, said in an email to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Former government employees would need \u8220\'20\'1Cwritten permission from an authorized agency official\u8221\'20\'1D to speak to journalists about information the Trump administration deems \u8220\'20\'1Cconfidential\u8221\'20\'1D after leaving their jobs, according to the draft. Former employees who violate that rule could be subject to civil and criminal penalties.\par \par \uc2 Federal law prohibits government retaliation against federal workers who disclose fraud, abuse and misconduct in their workplaces to internal government watchdogs and Congress. The NDA would not apply to those disclosures, according to the draft agreement. Since taking office for the second time, Trump has waged an aggressive campaign against news outlets and media figures he sees as too critical of him. He has filed lawsuits against news outlets, dismissed coverage as \u8220\'20\'1Cfake news\u8221\'20\'1D and personally attacked journalists. His administration banned the Associated Press from the White House press pool and restricted reporters\u8217\'20\'19 access at the Pentagon, the headquarters of the US military, among other moves.\par \par \uc2 Steve Lenkart, executive director of the National Federation of Federal Employees (NFFE), said the Trump administration has long used nondisclosures and other similar agreements to pressure federal workers into silence.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the Pentagon {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.spokesman.com/stories/2025/oct/01/pentagon-plans-widespread-random-polygraphs-ndas-t/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  planned to use NDAs and polygraph tests to stop leaks to the press and internal dissent. It also enacted a new media policy in September that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2025/09/20/g-s1-89713/pentagon-new-strict-guidelines-for-media"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 required}}}\uc2  reporters to sign a pledge to only report information that has been officially released.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is part of the same broader effort to weaken federal employee unions, which often serve as the eyes and ears of accountability within government agencies,\u8221\'20\'1D Lenkart said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the push to force federal workers to sign NDAs, federal workers still have protected rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNDAs do not supersede federal law or the constitution. Federal employees retain protected rights to communicate with Congress, inspectors general and, in many circumstances, the press regarding government misconduct and matters related to their employment,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lenkart. \u8220\'20\'1CAgreements intended to suppress lawful disclosures or shield wrongdoing serve no legitimate governmental purpose and are legally suspect.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , national president of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 , said in statement that federal agencies already have extensive policies and procedures that prevent the release of classified or privileged information.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis proposed NDA is another attempt by the administration to purge the civil service of nonpartisan career employees and replace them with loyalists who won\u8217\'20\'19t speak out against waste, fraud and abuse,\u8221\'20\'1D Kelley said. \u8220\'20\'1CFederal employees do not surrender their first amendment rights when they accept federal employment, and the public has a right to know about this administration\u8217\'20\'19s abuses.\u8221\'20\'1DReuters contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260527em5r0028s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
