\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 The home affairs minister, the private prison contractor and the secret meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 Christopher Knaus and Ariel Bogle \par \uc2 2,526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 May 2026\par 01:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Exclusive: ICE-linked private prison operator blamed for catastrophic security failures in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s immigration detention networkGet our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 A series of catastrophic security failures involving the US private prison company running Australia\u8217\'20\'19s immigration detention centres has allowed the escape of high-risk detainees, caused ill-equipped staff to be stabbed and hospitalised, and triggered multiple investigations, one of which warned its \u8220\'20\'1Cminimalist staffing model\u8221\'20\'1D was putting workers and detainees at risk.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia can reveal that in September 2025, just six months after \uc2 Management and Training Corporation\uc2  assumed control of onshore detention, the home affairs minister, Tony Burke, was forced to haul in the company\u8217\'20\'19s president from the US to dress him down in a secret face-to-face meeting.\par \par \uc2 MTC won a $2.3bn contract to run onshore detention from last year under its local subsidiary Secure Journeys, a deal struck despite serious concerns about MTC\u8217\'20\'19s track record in the US and at Parklea prison in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Using internal documents obtained through freedom of information and interviews with MTC staff, detainees and government sources, Guardian Australia has established that:\par \par \uc2 * Seriously ill detainees are missing medical appointments because MTC lacks the staff to escort them to health centres, a situation that has infuriated the home affairs department.\par \par \uc2 * Two MTC staff members were admitted to hospital with smoke inhalation after trying to rescue an unconscious detainee from a fire. Investigators found MTC had not given the staff basic respiratory equipment and fire-response training six months after assuming control of the centre.\par \par \uc2 * More than 12 escapes or attempted escapes have occurred in the 14 months MTC has had control of the system. A significant number took place during transport and escort operations to hospitals, airports or detention centres.\par \par \uc2 * A child sexual abuse offender deemed high-risk escaped MTC custody during an escort to Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s Bankstown hospital despite being handcuffed and supposedly under close watch.\par \par \uc2 * In September a detainee absconded by shimmying up a light pole next to a boundary fence at Brisbane immigration detention centre. His disappearance was not discovered for 12 hours.\par \par \uc2 * Late last year two detainees were able to flee a guarded MTC vehicle travelling less than 500 metres in Melbourne. One managed to evade capture for four days.\par \par \uc2 * The risk assessment system MTC uses to classify detainees is so broken that \uc2 Comcare\uc2 , the federal work safety regulator, has warned the home affairs department it is putting staff at serious risk of violence.\par \par \uc2 Comcare\uc2  has now set up a dedicated inspection program to scrutinise immigration detention, citing an \u8220\'20\'1Cincrease in notifiable incidents, the severity and the profile of the reported incidents involving the [contractor]\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The regulator said it had received numerous reports of \u8220\'20\'1Cserious injury or illness to both workers and others (detainees)\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Cself-harm ideations and death involving detainees\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cdetainee on detainee violence\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It has since privately issued a damning report to the home affairs department, finding that it was contravening work health and safety laws at at least one centre. The report says the risk was foreseeable and the department \u8220\'20\'1Cfailed to ensure \u8230\'20\'26 the health and safety of workers and other persons\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The government has already charged MTC hundreds of thousands of dollars in \u8220\'20\'1Cabatements\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 fines for performance failures.\par \par \uc2 But any action to remove MTC from the centres would be hugely expensive, particularly in the context of a home affairs budget so stretched that redundancies are being pursued to cut costs, and the number of potential replacements is limited.\par \par \uc2 Serco\uc2 , which ran the system for years, is thought to be the only other option but it would take the company months to retool and rehire a workforce.\par \par \uc2 One departmental source, speaking on condition of anonymity, described the situation as a \u8220\'20\'1Csinking ship\u8221\'20\'1D that is no longer salvageable.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSecure Journeys\u8217\'20\'19 14 months delivering Facilities and Detainee Services in Immigration Detention has been marked by chaos, incompetence and a lack of capability,\u8221\'20\'1D the source said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow no Secure Journeys staff member or detainee hasn\u8217\'20\'19t died yet from systemic incompetence is sheer luck.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Australian Border Force\uc2  (\uc2 ABF\uc2 ) and MTC did not respond to specific questions, but a spokesperson for the government agency said immigration detention was \u8220\'20\'1Ca complex operating environment\u8221\'20\'1D, and that the \u8220\'20\'1Cwelfare, safety and dignity of people in immigration detention, as well as the safety of staff\u8221\'20\'1D were its highest priorities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere issues are identified, providers are required to implement immediate corrective actions and longer-term improvements,\u8221\'20\'1D an \uc2 ABF\uc2  spokesperson said. \u8220\'20\'1CPerformance is closely monitored.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 An MTC Secure Journeys spokesperson said the company \u8220\'20\'1Ctakes the health, safety, and wellbeing of our people seriously\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have established systems, training and support arrangements in place to help staff perform their duties safely, including initial role-based training and ongoing refresher training to support currency of knowledge, safe work practices, and operational readiness.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe continue to review and strengthen these arrangements as part of our ongoing commitment to safe operations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Contractor ordered to do better\par \par \uc2 On the afternoon of 30 September 2025, the mood in MTC\u8217\'20\'19s national office was tense.\par \par \uc2 Heavy-hitters were in town to meet Burke, including the MTC global president, Dan Marquardt, usually based at the multinational\u8217\'20\'19s Utah headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Things were not going well.\par \par \uc2 Four months earlier, a detainee being transported from Villawood immigration detention centre in western Sydney to the airport for deportation had allegedlystabbed an MTC worker, assaulted another and fled, sparking a police manhunt. The escape hit the headlines in May.\par \par \uc2 But what was not reported was that the escape was the result of disturbing process failures.\par \par \uc2 The detainee was deemed to be high risk but was not handcuffed. And he was driven in a standard Kia Carnival lacking any of the usual safety features, such as Perspex barriers.\par \par \uc2 Registration records obtained by the Guardian suggest the Kia used by MTC was a hire car.\par \par \uc2 Three sources across MTC and the department have confirmed that the company was using hire cars to escort detainees.\par \par \uc2 There were other worrying incidents.\par \par \uc2 Internal Comcare documents reveal that on 23 September 2025, a week before the Burke meeting, MTC staff responded to a fire in a locked room at Villawood.\par \par \uc2 Flames and smoke were visible and the detainee inside was unconscious.\par \par \uc2 Such fires are common in detention centres. A Comcare report described them as an \u8220\'20\'1Cinherent and predictable aspect of operating within detention centres\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Despite this, the MTC staff were operating without respiratory equipment, resulting in \u8220\'20\'1Ctwo staff members requiring hospitalisation due to smoke inhalation\u8221\'20\'1D, Comcare said in a notice.\par \par \uc2 When Comcare investigators questioned MTC, the company offered a troubling explanation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COn Friday 3 October, the Centre Manager for Secure Journeys confirmed in response to my inquiries that workers have entered active fire zones voluntarily, without appropriate training, formal risk assessments, or authorisation,\u8221\'20\'1D documents show.\par \par \uc2 Comcare had received a separate complaint in July about an \u8220\'20\'1Calternative place of detention\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 usually hotels where detainees are held \u8211\'20\'13 that lacked basic emergency and evacuation plans.\par \par \uc2 The regulator\u8217\'20\'19s investigation identified a disturbing lack of basic processes, including documented inductions, training records and evidence of emergency preparedness activities, creating \u8220\'20\'1Ca clear and ongoing risk of repeated or continued contravention of [work health and safety laws and regulations]\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe absence of these safety systems, and the Department\u8217\'20\'19s failure to identify or address them through routine oversight, exposes workers and detainees to unnecessary and foreseeable risk in the event of an emergency,\u8221\'20\'1D the regulator told the department in late July.\par \par \uc2 At the meeting, Burke made his displeasure clear. Sources with knowledge of the meeting say MTC\u8217\'20\'19s president was dressed down and told to urgently improve his company\u8217\'20\'19s performance.\par \par \uc2 When the executives returned from the meeting, MTC staff were asked what they needed to improve their performance. Those who did speak up said simply: \u8220\'20\'1CStaff.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hospital getaway\par \par \uc2 The escape in May was far from isolated.\par \par \uc2 Last month Michael Angok, a convicted child sexual abuse offender, was escorted to Bankstown hospital from Villawood. He was deemed a high-risk detainee, a designation supposed to trigger a higher degree of control and monitoring during travel.\par \par \uc2 Angok saw an opportunity and fled the hospital, triggering a four-day police operation to recapture him.\par \par \uc2 Internal documents show MTC executives responded by instructing transport and escort teams to use \u8220\'20\'1Cmandatory restraints\u8221\'20\'1D for every detainee, regardless of whether they posed a risk.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am emailing to further instruct that the following occurs, effective immediately: mandatory restraints must be used for all T&E [transport and escort] tasks, unless medically contraindicated,\u8221\'20\'1D a missive read.\par \par \uc2 That has alarmed many MTC staff, who say body belts and handcuffs are now being applied without any consideration for harm to detainees who pose no risk.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Hall Spence, a principal solicitor at the Justice and Equity Centre, said a blanket policy of handcuffing people for transfers was \u8220\'20\'1Calmost certainly unlawful\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWithout a specific and immediate risk, it\u8217\'20\'19s doubtful the use of handcuffs could be justified as \u8216\'20\'18reasonably necessary\u8217\'20\'19, which is the requirement under current laws,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 On 14 April the Justice and Equity Centre wrote to the home affairs minister and the general counsel of Secure Journeys, warning that a detainee handcuffed under a blanket policy \u8220\'20\'1Ccould well have claims of assault, battery and false imprisonment\u8221\'20\'1D against the company and the government. There has been no response.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople in detention shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be suffering because the contractor can\u8217\'20\'19t get itself sorted to do transfers safely and properly,\u8221\'20\'1D Hall Spence told Guardian Australia. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re receiving more than $2bn to perform these services.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Staff shortages have forced multiple detainees with serious illness to miss medical treatment.\par \par \uc2 A March report by the National Preventive Mechanism, an independent monitor of places of detention, found that \u8220\'20\'1Ccritically\u8221\'20\'1D low levels of staffing were compromising the centres\u8217\'20\'19 safety.\par \par \uc2 Sources said a detainee who had been flagged for more frequent monitoring was able to set fire to a staff compound at Villawood last month.\par \par \uc2 The compound had been left without staff for hours before the fire, the source said.\par \par \uc2 At Brisbane immigration detention centre, staff did not realise that a detainee had climbed up a light pole beside an exterior fence and escaped. The escape was not discovered for 12 hours because of an error in a subsequent headcount.\par \par \uc2 At some point after MTC took over the contract, the home affairs department set up a dedicated team to conduct a six-week inspection blitz at detention centres across the country.\par \par \uc2 The team, known as the operational inspection program, found a \u8220\'20\'1Cminimalist staffing model that does not provide adequate contingencies to manage safety as well as security risks and vulnerabilities\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Problems at Parklea\par \par \uc2 MTC has a chequered past in the US, where it runs private prisons and immigration detention centres, which are being used in Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s ICE crackdown.\par \par \uc2 The company has been sued for a range of alleged security lapses there, court documents show, including alleged failures that led to the gang-rape of a woman in detention and the murder of two retirees by escaped prisoners. Both {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/sep/05/the-egregious-history-of-likely-new-nauru-operator-includes-allegations-of-gang-and-in-its-us-prisons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cases were settled}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In New South Wales, MTC runs Parklea correctional centre but the facility will be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/mar/02/parklea-correctional-centre-to-return-to-public-hands-as-nsw-reverses-failed-prison-for-profit-model"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 taken back}}}\uc2  into public hands in October.\par \par \uc2 The management of the prison by private operators has been marked by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.parliament.nsw.gov.au/lcdocs/inquiries/2470/Report%20No%2038%20-%20Parklea%20Correctional%20Centre%20and%20other%20operational%20issues.pdf?"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 longstanding concerns}}}\uc2  about overcrowding and poor conditions, as well as staffing levels.\par \par \uc2 MTC\u8217\'20\'19s management of Parklea should have been a warning sign, said Troy Wright, the branch assistant secretary of the Community and Public Sector Union in NSW, which represents prison staff.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTheir expertise was just not there,\u8221\'20\'1D he said of MTC\u8217\'20\'19s Parklea takeover, pointing to the company\u8217\'20\'19s limited track record in Australia in 2019. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd, sure enough, it\u8217\'20\'19s been a bloody disaster.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI always say a company can\u8217\'20\'19t make money off the prisoners, so they make it off the staff. They don\u8217\'20\'19t fill all their roles and they run their place understaffed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wright was surprised when MTC was awarded the immigration detention contract and said no one from the government had contacted the union to ask about its experience with MTC in NSW.\par \par \uc2 As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/feb/06/workers-accuse-new-company-managing-australias-immigration-detention-centres-of-running-them-like-a-prison-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guardian Australia has previously reported}}}\uc2 , some MTC staff at Parklea have been seconded to the immigration detention network due to staff shortages. \u8220\'20\'1CThey were short right away in immigration detention,\u8221\'20\'1D Wright said.\par \par \uc2 After MTC took over from GEO Group at Parklea in 2019, the NSW prison inspector {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://inspectorcustodial.nsw.gov.au/documents/inspection-reports/Parklea_CC_report_JUN22.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remarked on}}}\uc2  problems with staff levels \u8211\'20\'13 including \u8220\'20\'1Ctransitional gaps in the staffing model\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was unclear MTC-BRS had engaged sufficient staff to meet the high volume of escorts from Parklea CC for medical and hospital care,\u8221\'20\'1D a 2022 report said.\par \par \uc2 During {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://coroners.nsw.gov.au/documents/findings/2025/Inquest_into_the_death_of_James_Cunneen.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an inquest}}}\uc2  into the death of James Cunneen, a Parklea inmate, the coroner found he had died of heart disease in 2019 after his \u8220\'20\'1Cdeterioration went largely unrecognised\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The coroner noted many failures on the part of the prison\u8217\'20\'19s medical service provider but also examined concerns from the healthcare team that a lack of MTC staff meant it was difficult to ensure inmates were taken to the clinic.\par \par \uc2 MTC said many of the shortcomings in Cunneen\u8217\'20\'19s care involved matters beyond its control, but the coroner said in his 2025 findings this was \u8220\'20\'1Cmisconceived\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The coroner recommended that the corrective services commissioner \u8220\'20\'1Ctake steps to ensure that MTC remains compliant with its contractual obligations with respect to the number of correctional and health staff it is contracted to provide\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Worsening conditions\par \par \uc2 Detainees have reported deteriorating conditions in immigration detention centres.\par \par \uc2 At Villawood, some say they have been sleeping on the floor in common areas after a fire lit by an inmate damaged multiple rooms.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia has seen images of bedrooms with charred mattresses and severely smoke-damaged walls, as well as inmates with multiple mattresses set up next to a pool table and another putting up curtains in a portico for privacy.\par \par \uc2 One detainee said he was sharing a classroom with five others due to the fire damage. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no bathroom, no shower,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just pass the buck.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis company that took over \u8211\'20\'13 they try to run it like a prison.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another said understaffing meant detainees were at risk \u8211\'20\'13 pointing to the alleged murder of a 51-year-old man by another detainee at Villawood in January. \u8220\'20\'1CSince they\u8217\'20\'19ve taken over \u8230\'20\'26 the quality of life has dropped significantly,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The departmental source said there were a lot of good staff working in Secure Journeys, who were doing their best.\par \par \uc2 But morale had reached critically low levels and, despite significant assistance from the department, little was changing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFourteen months into the contract, Secure Journeys still can\u8217\'20\'19t staff their centres, continue to let escapes occur and miss several contract obligations regularly,\u8221\'20\'1D the source said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Department of Home Affairs can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to replace Secure Journeys \u8211\'20\'13 they are stuck with them for five years. It will take a whole facility burning down or deaths for this to come to a head.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Do you know more? Contact christopher.knaus@theguardian.com or ariel.bogle@theguardian.com\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260524em5o002jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
