\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Local US newspaper workers allege Hearst is trying to \u8216\'20\'18destroy unions\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 972 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 May 2026\par 12:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Media company says it is \u8216\'20\'18committed to good faith bargaining\u8217\'20\'19 amid claims including contract violation\par \par \uc2 Workers at local newspapers owned by Hearst allege the company is trying to \u8220\'20\'1Cdestroy unions\u8221\'20\'1D amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/search/case/Hearst?f[0]=case_type:C&s[0]=Open"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claims}}}\uc2  of widespread anti-union tactics, including violating union contracts and bad-faith bargaining.\par \par \uc2 The Albany Newspaper Guild, which represents the Times Union newspaper in Albany, New York, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gofundme.com/f/support-times-union-workers-during-walkout"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  it had been more than 17 years since the union had a contract and there had been little progress toward reaching a new one.\par \par \uc2 Wendy Liberatore, president of the Albany Newspaper Guild and the Saratoga county reporter at the Times Union, said the lack of pay increases at the newspaper had burdened employees who struggle to afford a higher cost of living, including higher deductibles for their healthcare plan. She also said that workers were concerned about Hearst eventually outsourcing jobs to AI.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat bothers me is the effect on our members. It really hurts our members,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Liberatore noted the issue was seen across Hearst publications across the country. \u8220\'20\'1CHearst newspaper management \u8211\'20\'13 in Albany, Connecticut, Austin and Dallas \u8211\'20\'13 is doing its best to destroy unions at its papers,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The Hearst Corporation{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hearst.com/newspapers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 owns}}}\uc2  30 daily newspapers and 50 weekly newspapers across the US, and is one of the largest media companies in the world. The company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2026/02/17/hearst-revenue-profits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  record revenues and profits in 2025, with revenue growing 3% to $13.5bn. Hearst employs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/company/hearst-np"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 2,500 workers}}}\uc2  at its newspapers.\par \par \uc2 The Albany Newspaper Guild is one of five guilds representing workers at local newspapers in Connecticut, Texas, New York and California that have filed formal complaints against Hearst for unfair labor practices.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Hearst said the company cannot comment on pending unfair labor practice complaints but was \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitted to good faith bargaining and have actively met with union representatives across our properties for years\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Hearst Newspapers\uc2  stands out among peers for maintaining competitive and sustainable wages and benefits. We have maintained an annual merit cycle for many years, and all union-represented employees who meet performance expectations have received increases even during contract negotiations. Claims of stagnant wages are simply inaccurate,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson for \uc2 Hearst Newspapers\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson also noted that Hearst had \u8220\'20\'1Ca strict policy governing the use of artificial intelligence in our newsrooms, and it always operates with human oversight\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 When Hearst {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hearst.com/-/hearst-to-acquire-the-austin-american-statesman-from-gannett-co.-inc."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 acquired}}}\uc2  the Austin American-Statesman in Texas in February 2025, the company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://actionnetwork.org/petitions/tell-hearst-respect-austins-local-news?nowrapper=true&referrer=&source="}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 discarded}}}\uc2  the contract that the workers with the Austin NewsGuild had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://actionnetwork.org/petitions/tell-hearst-respect-austins-local-news?nowrapper=true&referrer=&source="}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reached}}}\uc2  with Gannett, the paper\u8217\'20\'19s previous owner, in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Villalpando, chair of the Austin NewsGuild and a health reporter at the Austin American-Statesman, said that after the acquisition, workers lost the pay raise guarantees and bolstered benefits they had in their contract with Gannett.\par \par \uc2 Hearst \u8220\'20\'1Cbasically took the worst parts of our Gannett contract and the worst parts of Hearst policy\u8221\'20\'1D when setting out new terms with employees, Villalpando said.\par \par \uc2 Workers with the Austin NewsGuild have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gofundme.com/f/support-the-austin-americanstatesman-newsguild-strike-fund?attribution_id=sl:d39c9834-f1de-47c9-a72d-66b71dc5754a&lang=en_US&ts=1761260557&utm_campaign=fp_sharesheet&utm_medium=customer&utm_source=copy_link"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raising money}}}\uc2  for a potential strike as the union has not reached a new deal with Hearst.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the company said it gave Austin American-Statesman employees a sign-on bonus, along with increasing the newsroom minimum salary and other benefits, after the acquisition.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, workers with the Dallas News Guild, which represents the Dallas Morning News, filed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/16-CA-375978"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 complaint}}}\uc2  with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) claiming that Hearst violated the union contract after it laid off 26 employees in November, two months after it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.poynter.org/business-work/2025/hearst-buys-the-the-dallas-morning-news-and-140-years-of-local-ownership-ends/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 acquired}}}\uc2  the publication. The Dallas News Guild contract remains in place but is set to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newsguild.org/journalists-at-dallas-morning-news-ratify-first-contract/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expire}}}\uc2  on 30 June.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese Hearst units, we talk to each other, we know what each of us are proposing and what the responses have been like, and it hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been very encouraging,\u8221\'20\'1D said Zaira Perez Viera, a Dallas News Guild member and journalist at the Dallas Morning News. \u8220\'20\'1CIt takes a lot of heart and strength to be able to stand up with your co-workers to say you want something better for yourselves, just to be able to continue to do the work that you\u8217\'20\'19re doing, and hope that you build a place where others of the future can still be coming here to do journalism.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRealistically, how many of us can keep doing this if we don\u8217\'20\'19t make enough money to be able to thrive in a place like Dallas-Fort Worth?\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Hearst said the layoffs \u8220\'20\'1Cinvolved roles that were affected by the integration into our centralized page design and pagination team\u8221\'20\'1D, and that the company opened 18 new positions that the affected employees were encouraged to apply for.\par \par \uc2 In Connecticut, more than 100 workers at Hearst Connecticut Media Group unionized in May 2025, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nyguild.org/post/a-major-win-for-unionized-hearst-journalists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly 80% voting}}}\uc2  in favor, though Hearst initially delayed the union election by challenging the scope of the unit.\par \par \uc2 Though the NLRB dismissed Hearst\u8217\'20\'19s objection to the election results in March, the board has not yet responded to numerous unfair labor practice complaints the Connecticut guild {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nyguild.org/post/a-major-win-for-unionized-hearst-journalists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  against the company alleging refusal to bargain, retaliation and unilateral changes to working conditions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been a lot of stalling,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brian Zahn, unit chair of the Connecticut News Guild and a municipal government reporter at the Hearst Connecticut Media Group. But he remains hopeful that workers will be able to fight for a contract.\par \par \uc2 Zahn noted their first bargaining date is scheduled for 1 June, \u8220\'20\'1Calmost two years after we had first went public with our union drive\u8221\'20\'1D, he added. \u8220\'20\'1CSince then, our unit has remained strong. We\u8217\'20\'19re very excited to bargain a first contract.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Hearst said it will \u8220\'20\'1Ccome to the table in good faith\u8221\'20\'1D with the Connecticut NewsGuild in June.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260522em5m002pb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
