\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US employers spend more than $1.5bn a year to fight labor unions, report finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2026\par 14:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Critics say employers spend money hiring union-avoidance consultants and lawyers while not investing in workers\par \par \uc2 US employers spend more than $1.5bn a year on labor union opposition efforts, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/321180/pre/b2f05dae28c7630f919860d19ce1fa346f5b3d4d5c390ef9a6a57aef8ddb030e/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  published on Wednesday by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  (EPI).\par \par \uc2 Employers spent company money hiring consultants and law firms specializing in union avoidance and on legal counsel, representation, and litigation services during union elections and organizing campaigns.\par \par \uc2 US employers spend $442m on union-avoidance consultants annually, according to an estimate by the EPI. Amazon alone spent $26.6m in 2025 on union-avoidance consultants, based on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/321180/pre/b2f05dae28c7630f919860d19ce1fa346f5b3d4d5c390ef9a6a57aef8ddb030e/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filings}}}\uc2  with the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 An Amazon spokesperson blamed external groups for hiring union-avoidance consultants. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s important that our teammates and partners understand the truth, so we\u8217\'20\'19ve continued to work with experts in the field who are able to share objective facts about what it actually means to have an external party take their voice,\u8221\'20\'1D they said in an email.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is millions or even billions of dollars that\u8217\'20\'19s not going towards workers and investing into their workplace,\u8221\'20\'1D said Margaret Poydock, a co-author of the report and a senior policy analyst at the EPI.\par \par \uc2 Poydock attributed the decline of unionization membership and density over the past several decades, in part, to the role of these union-avoidance law firms and consultants. Union density in the US is now at 10%, compared with 20.3% in 1983.\par \par \uc2 Despite this decline, Gallup polls report {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/694472/labor-union-approval-relatively-steady.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly 70% }}}\uc2 of Americans approve of labor unions.\par \par \uc2 The report noted that one of the law firms, \uc2 Littler Mendelson\uc2 , which has represented Amazon, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  in union campaigns, has its own {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.littler.com/products-services/workplace-policy-institute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Workplace Policy Institute}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Through the institute, the law firm has tracked and opposed legislation aimed at expanding workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights, such as opposing AB5 in California, a bill that sought to combat worker misclassification, and supporting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/25/california-uber-lyft-gig-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Prop 22}}}\uc2 , which allowed ride-share apps and others to continue classifying drivers as independent contractors, not employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese law firms and consultants are essentially exploiting loopholes and weaknesses in our federal labor law and reporting requirements for these persuaders reports, but despite that, workers are still organizing, they are still winning elections and reaching first contracts,\u8221\'20\'1D said Poydock. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re trying to erode worker rights, not just for union workers or workers trying to form unions, but workers at large.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Teke Wiggin, a co-author of the report and strategic coordinator at the non-profit LaborLab, emphasized US employers already leverage significant power over workers and the use of union-avoidance consultants and law firms further magnifies that imbalance.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/unlawful-employer-opposition-to-union-election-campaigns/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previous report}}}\uc2  by the EPI found that US employers are charged with violating labor law in 41.5% of all union elections. Through delay tactics and appeals, it takes an{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/union-first-contract-fact-sheet/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 average of 465 days}}}\uc2 for workers to reach a first union contract, and it can be much longer in many cases, such as at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , where workers have yet to reach a first contract since the first US storewon a union election in 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmployers always have the choice to voluntarily recognize a union or agree to a neutrality agreement,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wiggin. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they don\u8217\'20\'19t, that\u8217\'20\'19s a choice to try to bend the will of workers who are seeking to exercise their rights to free association, and that\u8217\'20\'19s immoral and offensive to democratic values and the right to free association.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260520em5k00462\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
