\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US workers overwhelmingly support union-backed policies on AI, poll says\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 664 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2026\par 15:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nine out of 10 workers express support for policies on artificial intelligence that labor unions may fight for\par \par \uc2 US workers overwhelmingly support pro-worker policies on artificial intelligence (AI) and view {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 labor unions}}}\uc2  as the most reliable protectors of workers from the effects of AI, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/sites/default/files/2026-05/DBR_AFLCIO_AI_Research_Memo.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a new poll}}}\uc2  released by the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the largest federation of labor unions in the US.\par \par \uc2 More than nine out of 10 workers surveyed expressed support for policies on artificial intelligence that labor unions may fight for, including 95% supporting a requirement that a human be the final decision maker on any issues affecting individual workers and their employment.\par \par \uc2 Some 92% also support advanced guardrails against harmful uses of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/artificialintelligenceai"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AI}}}\uc2  in workplaces and require transparency and accountability when employers use AI.\par \par \uc2 Every worker protection polled in the survey received support from at least 75% of those polled, including 75% supporting the expansion of opportunities for workers to form unions to protect their jobs from AI. The poll, conducted with David Binder Research, surveyed 1,588 respondents across the US from 14 April to 22 April.\par \par \uc2 The in-depth polling of workers\u8217\'20\'19 sentiments toward AI comes as workers, through collective bargaining agreements, have increasingly been seeking and securing protections on how artificial intelligence is used in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the latest way that companies can lay people off, reduce their workforce and cut corners by saying, \u8216\'20\'18oh, well, we\u8217\'20\'19ll just have AI do it,\u8217\'20\'19 even if the results are bad, as we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing. So I think it is important for workers to protect themselves,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anna Iovine, former unit chair of the Ziff Davis Creators Guild, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newsguild.org/prime-day-walkout-averted-at-ziff-davis/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won AI protections}}}\uc2  in their contract in 2024, including editorial integrity protections, transparency for when and how AI is used in the workplace, and no layoffs or reduced pay due to AI implementation.\par \par \uc2 Hannah Drummond, a registered nurse in North Carolina and a union member with \uc2 National Nurses United\uc2 , also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.healthcare-brew.com/stories/nurses-setting-rules-ai-contracts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fought}}}\uc2  to include AI protections in her union contract in 2024 to ensure it would not be used without workers\u8217\'20\'19 approval. She noted some hospitals have been implementing AI for scheduling and in using algorithms to estimate if a patient is going to get sicker, despite errors in the statistical models.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have technology language that says no new technology at all can be implemented that affects the delivery of patient care without going through the union first, and no technology should de-skill or undermine our judgment,\u8221\'20\'1D said Drummond. \u8220\'20\'1CWe should not be experimenting on our patients. Patients are not guinea pigs. Patients who participate in clinical trials have informed consent. AI is being used in hospitals without patients\u8217\'20\'19 consent. This violates the heart of health care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Of the workers polled, 78% said it is extremely or very important that some action be taken to protect workers from the potential harms of AI.\par \par \uc2 Only 7% of workers polled said their employers disclosed how and when their work is monitored by AI, while 70% said their employers have not disclosed this, and 23% were unsure. 94% of workers said they believe workers should be informed by their employers if AI is being used to monitor their work.\par \par \uc2 When respondents were asked whether Democrats, Republicans, labor unions or employers were more trustworthy to protect workers from AI, 38% picked labor unions, 17% said Democrats, 10% said Republicans, 6% picked employers and 18% chose none of the options.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese results make it clear: our Workers First Initiative on AI is not just a set of principles, but a mandate to deliver,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2 , president of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , in a statement on the poll results. \u8220\'20\'1CThe vast majority of America\u8217\'20\'19s workers agree on how to move forward on AI and who they trust to do it, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not Democrats, Republicans, Big Tech, or their employers \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s the labor movement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260512em5c00336\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
