\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US added 115,000 jobs in April in surprise gain amid Iran war uncertainty\b0\par\par\uc2 Gaya Gupta \par \uc2 554 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2026\par 15:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unemployment remained steady at 4.3% as the US-Israel war on Iran continues to rattle the American economy\par \par \uc2 US employers added 115,000 jobs in April and the unemployment rate remained steady at 4.3%, a surprisingly robust gain to the labor marketas the US-Israel war with Iran continued to drive up economic uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Economists projected about 55,000 new jobs and a 4.3% unemployment rate. A day earlier, the labor department announced 200,000 people filed for weekly unemployment benefits, a slight increase from the week before.\par \par \uc2 Jobs gains were concentrated in healthcare, transportation and warehousing, retail, and social assistance. Altogether, 106,000 new jobs were added to those four industries.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, job losses were seen in federal government employment \u8211\'20\'13 which is down 348,000 since November 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 and the information sector.\par \par \uc2 White House spokesperson Kush Desai wrote on social media on Friday that the April jobs report \u8220\'20\'1Csmash[ed] expectations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYet another sign that the American economy remains on a solid trajectory under President Trump,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 A series of major changes over the last year \u8211\'20\'13 tariffs, government layoffs, changing immigration policies and, now, rising oil prices amid conflict in the Middle East \u8211\'20\'13 have rattled the US economy and destabilized the labor market.\par \par \uc2 The new data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  also included revisions to previous job figures. Last month, employers added 185,000 jobs, far exceeding economists\u8217\'20\'19 expectations of about 70,000. But in February, the US lost 156,000 jobs \u8211\'20\'13 initially reported as a drop of 92,000 jobs \u8211\'20\'13 an unexpected and major contraction just before the US-Israel war in Iran.\par \par \uc2 Private employers added 109,000 jobs in April, the largest increase in job growth since January 2025, according to the payroll firm ADP. Healthcare industry jobs continued tofuel growth, along with modest increases in construction, trade, transportation and utilities industries. Professional industries, however, lost 8,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSmall and large employers are hiring, but we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing softness in the middle,\u8221\'20\'1D Dr \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , ADP\u8217\'20\'19s chief economist, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CLarge companies have resources to deploy, and small ones are the most nimble, both important advantages in a complex labor environment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  cited slow job growth among several factors in its decision to keep rates steady last month, alongside elevated inflation and continued conflict in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Fluctuating economic data adds to the uncertainty casting a shadow over the Fed. Last month, the outgoing chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , announced he will remain on the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s board as a governor even after \uc2 Kevin Warsh\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s nominee to lead the central bank, is sworn in. Powell {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/apr/29/fed-jerome-powell-decision-hold-interest-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  he will stay on until investigations into renovations at Fed headquarters are \u8220\'20\'1Cwell and truly over with transparency and finality\u8221\'20\'1D, citing concerns that the Fed will be \u8220\'20\'1Cpulled into politics\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Warsh is under pressure from the White House to usher in lower interest rates, though he cannot do so without the support of the other 12 voting members of the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s board.\par \par \uc2 The Fed wields immense influence over the state of unemployment and inflation in the US, and must set rates to fulfill its dual mandate of maximizing employment while maintaining stable prices.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260508em58003s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
