\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 Lawsuit claims \uc2 New York Times\uc2  didn\u8217\'20\'19t promote white male editor because of race or sex\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeremy Barr in Washington \par \uc2 782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2026\par 15:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  files suit claiming newspaper passed over employee due to \u8216\'20\'18diversity aspirations\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In what appears to be a new front in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/24/trump-free-speech-media-attack"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pressure}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/feb/02/trump-media-pressure"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 campaign}}}\uc2  against the media, the \uc2 US Equal Employment Opportunity Commission\uc2  on Tuesday filed a lawsuit against the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/new-york-times"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2 , charging that the news organization improperly passed over a white, male employee for promotion because of his race or sex.\par \par \uc2 The employee believed he had been passed over for a promotion to deputy real estate editor, a position that had been listed in January 2025, despite believing himself to be a \u8220\'20\'1Csignificantly\u8221\'20\'1D more qualified candidate.\par \par \uc2 The employee\u8217\'20\'19s name was not included in the lawsuit, which noted that he had worked as an editor at the Times since 2014 and had served as senior staff editor on the paper\u8217\'20\'19s international desk for more than nine years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCharging Party met all requirements for the Deputy Real Estate Editor position, including experience with real estate journalism,\u8221\'20\'1D the lawsuit claims. \u8220\'20\'1CAs a White male, Charging Party did not match the race and/or sex characteristics NYT sought to increase in its leadership through its diversity actions and aspirations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The employee was not selected as a finalist for the deputy real estate editor position. (No white males were among the four finalists for the role, according to the lawsuit.) The role was ultimately awarded to Monica Burton, who came to the Times from the \uc2 Vox Media\uc2  property Eater.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe unlawful employment practices complained of above were done with malice or with reckless indifference to Charging Party\u8217\'20\'19s federally protected rights,\u8221\'20\'1D according to the complaint, which demanded that the Times provide \u8220\'20\'1Cappropriate backpay with prejudgement interest\u8221\'20\'1D to the employee who was passed over \u8211\'20\'13 among other forms of compensation.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit faulted efforts by the Times to increase diversity in the company\u8217\'20\'19s ranks, including in leadership.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA necessary consequence of NYT\u8217\'20\'19s intent to increase the percentage of non-White leaders would be a decrease in the percentage of White leaders,\u8221\'20\'1D the lawsuit states. \u8220\'20\'1CParticularly, decreasing the percentage of White males in leadership would contribute to the NYT\u8217\'20\'19s dual goals of increasing the percentages of females and \u8216\'20\'18people of color\u8217\'20\'19 (as defined by NYT) in leadership.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The individual had filed a charge with the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  ahead of the lawsuit. The commission informed the individual that they found \u8220\'20\'1Creasonable cause\u8221\'20\'1D that the Times violated Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, which prohibits employment discrimination based on race, color, religion, sex and national origin.\par \par \uc2 The Times dismissed any notion that the hiring process had violated federal labor law \u8211\'20\'13 and said it would \u8220\'20\'1Cvigorously\u8221\'20\'1D defend against any lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 New York Times\uc2  categorically rejects the politically motivated allegations brought by the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 EEOC\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Danielle Rhoades Ha\uc2 , a Times spokesperson, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur employment practices are merit-based and focused on recruiting and promoting the best talent in the world. We will defend ourselves vigorously. Throughout this process, the \uc2 EEOC\uc2  deviated from standard practices in highly unusual ways. The allegation centers on a single personnel decision for one of over 100 deputy positions across the newsroom, yet the \uc2 EEOC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s filing makes sweeping claims that ignore the facts to fit a predetermined narrative. Neither race nor gender played a role in this decision \u8211\'20\'13 we hired the most qualified candidate, and she is an excellent editor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 New York Times\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s commitment to diversity in all its forms is longstanding and unwavering, as is our commitment to a fair and legal hiring process that does not discriminate against anyone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Employment practices at media companies could become a point of leverage for the government. Last week, \uc2 Brendan Carr\uc2 , the chair of the \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2026/apr/30/brendan-carr-fcc-jimmy-kimmel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cited an existing investigation}}}\uc2  into diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI) efforts at \uc2 Disney\uc2  as the statutory basis for a highly unusual decision to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/28/fcc-abc-jimmy-kimmel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 call up for}}}\uc2  early renewal broadcast licenses held by eight television stations owned and operated by ABC. The announcement came one day after Donald and Melania Trump had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/27/melania-trump-jimmy-kimmel-abc-spat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called for}}}\uc2  the network to fire the late-night comedian Jimmy Kimmel over a poorly timed joke the previous week.\par \par \uc2 More broadly, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/14/journalists-of-color-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 media companies have been backing away}}}\uc2  from diversity initiatives that sprang up in the aftermath of the police killing of George Floyd in 2020. In October, CBS News \u8220\'20\'1Cgutted\u8221\'20\'1D its race and culture unit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2025/oct/29/cbs-news-layoffs-paramount"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as part of a broader round of layoffs}}}\uc2 . \uc2 Skydance\uc2  also promised to end \u8211\'20\'13 and not reinstate \u8211\'20\'13any diversity-focused programs at \uc2 CBS\uc2  parent Paramount as a pre-condition for \uc2 FCC\uc2  approval of its 2025 merger.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260505em55006bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
