\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Deplorable\u8217\'20\'19: ICE hires firm accused of \u8216\'20\'18torture\u8217\'20\'19 to track down undocumented children\b0\par\par\uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Olivares in New York \par \uc2 1,364 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2026\par 13:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Exclusive: Contractor denies allegations including \u8216\'20\'18enforced disappearance\u8217\'20\'19 and will help locate unaccompanied minors\par \par \uc2 US Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ice-us-immigration-and-customs-enforcement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ICE}}}\uc2 ) has awarded a contract to a private security company that has faced accusations of \u8220\'20\'1Ctorture\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cenforced disappearance\u8221\'20\'1D to assist in tracking down undocumented immigrant children who arrived in the US alone, a contracting document shows.\par \par \uc2 ICE has stepped up its workso much in pursuing these minors in the US that it has contracted out some of its mission to a third party to put \u8220\'20\'1Cboots on the ground\u8221\'20\'1D and locate immigrant children previously released from \uc2 US government\uc2  custody.\par \par \uc2 The agency characterizes the work of tracing immigrant children who reached the US without authorization and were released into communities while they go through immigration court proceedings as \u8220\'20\'1Csafety and wellness checks\u8221\'20\'1D. ICE says it wants to confirm the children\u8217\'20\'19s location, school enrollment and overall wellness, including checking for signs of abuse or trafficking, according to the contracting document.\par \par \uc2 But an internal ICE document {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/apr/28/ice-unaccompanied-immigrant-children"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reviewed by the Guardian last year}}}\uc2  shows ICE actually runs the operations with the aim of deporting the children or pursuing criminal cases against them \u8211\'20\'13 or their adult sponsors sheltering them legally in the US. A critic at the time called ICE\u8217\'20\'19s efforts \u8220\'20\'1Cbackdoor family separation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAccusations that ICE is \u8216\'20\'18targeting\u8217\'20\'19 and arresting children are FALSE and an attempt to demonize law enforcement,\u8221\'20\'1D a DHS spokesperson said on Friday. \u8220\'20\'1CRather than separating families, ICE asks parents if they want to be removed with their children or if the child should be placed with someone safe the parent designates.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Now, as that program continues, the agency in mid-April gave a contract to a US company, \uc2 MVM Inc\uc2 , to assist in carrying out such operations.\par \par \uc2 MVM is a longtime security contractor, based in Ashburn, Virginia, with about 2,500 employees, and provides detention and transport services to federal immigration agencies. It previously provided security services to the CIA.\par \par \uc2 MVM did not respond to a detailed request for comment by time of publication.\par \par \uc2 In 2024, MVM was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.casd.789549/gov.uscourts.casd.789549.1.0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued}}}\uc2  by two Guatemalan fathers and their respective children in a California federal court for alleged\u8220\'20\'1Ctorture, enforced disappearance and cruel, inhuman, and degrading treatment\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the lawsuit, for the role it played in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2025/jan/17/trump-policy-family-separation-future"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 family separation}}}\uc2  policy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/jun/19/families-border-separations-trump-immigration-policy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at the border}}}\uc2  under the first Trump administration that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/jun/20/donald-trump-pledges-to-end-family-separations-by-executive-order"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prompted}}}\uc2  widespread {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/jun/19/child-separation-camps-trump-border-policy-backlash-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 uproar}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMVM physically took thousands of children away from their parents and transferred them to shelters,\u8221\'20\'1D the lawsuit said. \u8220\'20\'1CMVM transported and harbored these children using unmarked vehicles, commercial airlines, and makeshift detention centers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 MVM {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.casd.789549/gov.uscourts.casd.789549.42.1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked}}}\uc2  a judge to toss the lawsuit, saying the company had \u8220\'20\'1Copenly denounced\u8221\'20\'1D the family separation campaign, adding that since it was a private company, it should not be held liable for a \uc2 US government\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 The two Guatemalan children, a 16-year-old and a three-year-old, were separated from their respective fathers in 2017, \u8220\'20\'1Cwith the substantial assistance of MVM\u8221\'20\'1D, the lawsuit says. The case continues to move through federal court.\par \par \uc2 In March 2025, a judge dismissed some of the claims on procedural grounds but allowed the case to continue based on the torture, enforced disappearance, and inhuman and degrading treatment claims.\par \par \uc2 Eighteen different companies offered their services to ICE to assist in the \u8220\'20\'1Cwellness checks\u8221\'20\'1D operation, according to a document posted publicly on a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sam.gov/workspace/contract/opp/44587f1e9c9045b1b0ffc828f33fe14e/view"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 government contracting website}}}\uc2 . But the other companies that vied for the contract lacked \u8220\'20\'1Cthe critical \u8216\'20\'18boots on the ground\u8217\'20\'19 child welfare personnel and infrastructure needed to physically locate and conduct wellness checks on children\u8221\'20\'1D, the document said. MVM, however, did appear to have the resources ICE was seeking, according to a review of the document.\par \par \uc2 The contract is supposed to run for one year. The amount ICE is paying MVM is redacted, along with the number of \u8220\'20\'1Cwellness checks\u8221\'20\'1D the agency wants the company to perform.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMVM contractors have ZERO immigration enforcement authority. This partnership, as part of the UAC Safety Verification Initiative, represents ICE\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to protect vulnerable children from sexual abuse and exploitation. The primary focus of this initiative is to conduct welfare checks on these children to ensure that they are safe and not being exploited or abused,\u8221\'20\'1D the DHS spokesperson added, using the official term for the program to conduct checks on children who immigrated to the US unaccompanied and have been placed with sponsors.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the Trump administration began efforts to track down immigrant children who had entered the US alone to request asylum or reunite with family members already in the US. Such children largely arrive at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-mexico-border"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US-Mexico border}}}\uc2  and either turn themselves in or are apprehended by border officials.\par \par \uc2 After an unaccompanied immigrant child enters the US, they are placed under the custody of the office of refugee resettlement (ORR). While their immigration cases, which are handled by ICE, play out, ORR will place the children in shelters, in foster homes or under a sponsor\u8217\'20\'19s care if available. Typically, sponsors, who complete an assessment process and background checks, are the children\u8217\'20\'19s relatives in the US; at times, they are unrelated adults.\par \par \uc2 In the past year, ICE, in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dhs.gov/news/2025/11/14/ice-and-state-local-law-enforcement-287g-partners-launch-initiative-protect"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 partnership}}}\uc2  with local law enforcement agencies, has begun to track down those children, many of whom the Trump administration says have gone \u8220\'20\'1Cmissing\u8221\'20\'1D, to provide \u8220\'20\'1Cwellness checks\u8221\'20\'1D. But the operations have been criticized by many immigration attorneys and advocates.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis all seems like a ploy to do two things: one, find either kids or their sponsors to arrest and deport. Or, two, scare children into self-deporting,\u8221\'20\'1D said Michael Lukens, the executive director of the Amica Center for Immigrant Rights, which provides legal representation to immigrant children. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really deplorable. It\u8217\'20\'19s really concerning.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For years, Trump administration allies pointed to a 2024 homeland security inspector general {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oig.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/assets/2024-08/OIG-24-46-Aug24.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  that found that ICE was not able to adequately track unaccompanied minors. They used that report to push a narrative that unaccompanied immigrant children have been lost and trafficked, Lukens said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTheir parents know where they are, their lawyers know where they are, usually the courts know where they are. It\u8217\'20\'19s just ICE doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have their address in a file,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lukens. \u8220\'20\'1CThose kids were never missing but they\u8217\'20\'19re using it as an excuse to do these \u8216\'20\'18wellness checks\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The inspector general report suggested understaffing at ICE and deficient cross-agency communication are mostly to blame for the agency\u8217\'20\'19s inability to keep track of the children, rather than actual trafficking.\par \par \uc2 MVM is a longtime government contractor that now mostly works with federal agencies to transport immigrant children and families between government-run facilities. It was started in the late 1970s by former Secret Service agents and ballooned into a significant government contractor. The Wall Street Journal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB121485921602717113"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  in 2008 that MVM had a secretive contract with the CIA in Iraq for security guards to protect CIA staff.\par \par \uc2 MVM also has a track record of allegations of abuse with its previous immigration-related contract work. In 2018, MVM was accused of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://revealnews.org/article/defense-contractor-detained-migrant-kids-in-vacant-phoenix-office-building/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 holding immigrant children}}}\uc2  in a vacant office building for three weeks amid the family separation crisis under the first Trump administration. During the Covid-19 pandemic, MVM {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/16/us/migrant-children-hotels-coronavirus.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 detained immigrant children and families}}}\uc2  in hotels before they were removed from the country. MVM also had the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usaspending.gov/award/CONT_AWD_70CDCR21FR0000031_7012_HSCEDM17D00011_7012"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 contract}}}\uc2  to run the secretive Guant\u225\'00\'E1namo Bay immigration detention center, until it was taken over by another company in 2025. Most recently, last August, the non-profit newsroom Injustice Watch reported that MVM {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.injusticewatch.org/civil-courts/immigration/2025/ice-detention-sonesta-ohare-airport-hotel-chicago/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 locked an immigrant woman and her baby}}}\uc2  inside a Chicago hotel for five days.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have seen MVM harm children in federal immigration custody in egregious ways for many years now,\u8221\'20\'1D said Neha Desai, the managing director of children\u8217\'20\'19s human rights and dignity at the National Center for Youth Law. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is both deeply disturbing and completely unsurprising that this government has hired MVM to conduct so-called \u8216\'20\'18wellness checks\u8217\'20\'19. These checks have already terrorized numerous children and have led to family separation throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat will come next once MVM is involved will surely be even worse,\u8221\'20\'1D Desai added.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260502em52001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
