\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18demolishes\u8217\'20\'19 Voting Rights Act, gutting provision that prevented racial discrimination\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levine \par \uc2 1,853 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2026\par 18:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 24\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices rule in landmark decision Louisiana must redraw congressional map, largely killing major civil rights lawSign up for the Breaking News US newsletter email\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-109_21o3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has ruled }}}\uc2 that Louisiana will have to redraw its congressional map, in a landmark decision that effectively guts a major section of the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 In a 6-3 decision along partisan lines, the court rendered ineffective section 2 of the Voting Rights Act, the last remaining powerful provision of the 1965 civil rights law that prevents racial discrimination in voting. Section 2 has long been used to ensure minority voters are treated fairly in redistricting.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAllowing race to play any part in government decision-making represents a departure from the constitutional rule that applies in almost every other context,\u8221\'20\'1D Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , a conservative, wrote for the majority opinion. \u8220\'20\'1CCompliance with section 2 thus could not justify the state\u8217\'20\'19s use of race-based redistricting here. The state\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to satisfy the middle district\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, although understandable, was an unconstitutional racial gerrymander.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision is a major upheaval in US civil rights law and gives lawmakers permission to draw districting plans that weaken the influence of Black and other minority voters. Some states may even rush ahead to try to redraw districts ahead of this year\u8217\'20\'19s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Asked by reporters on Wednesday whether states should redraw their congressional maps in response to the ruling, Donald Trump said: \u8220\'20\'1CI would.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a dissenting opinion, Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  wrote the court had now accomplished a \u8220\'20\'1Cdemolition of the Voting Rights Act\u8221\'20\'1D. The court\u8217\'20\'19s decision on Wednesday is the latest in a series that dismantled the law, she wrote, including a major decision in 2013 case, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep570/usrep570529/usrep570529.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Shelby County v Holder}}}\uc2 , that nullified another major provision in the law that required places with a history of discrimination to get changes pre-approved by the federal government before they went into effect.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnder the court\u8217\'20\'19s new view of section 2, a state can, without legal consequence, systematically dilute minority citizens\u8217\'20\'19 voting power,\u8221\'20\'1D Kagan wrote in a dissent that was joined by Justices \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe majority claims only to be \u8216\'20\'18updat[ing]\u8217\'20\'19 our section 2 law, as though through a few technical tweaks. In fact, those \u8216\'20\'18updates\u8217\'20\'19 eviscerate the law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s decision renders section 2 all but a dead letter,\u8221\'20\'1D she continued. \u8220\'20\'1CThe decision here is about Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s district 6. But so too it is about Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s district 2. And so too it is about the many other districts, particularly in the south, that in the last half-century have given minority citizens, and particularly African Americans, a meaningful political voice. After today, those districts exist only on sufferance, and probably not for long.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the heart of the case, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/24/supreme-court-louisiana-redistricting-voting-rights-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana v Callais}}}\uc2 , was a question of how much lawmakers are allowed to consider race when they redraw districts to ensure that Black voters are adequately represented. The supreme court initially heard oral arguments in the case last March, but took the unusual step of asking lawyers to re-argue the case last fall. In setting the case for a re-argument, the justices raised the stakes of the case, asking lawyers to focus on whether section 2 of the Voting Rights Act was constitutional.\par \par \uc2 In its decision on Wednesday, the court\u8217\'20\'19s majority stopped short of saying outright that section 2 was unconstitutional. Instead, the majority significantly reworked a three-part test that plaintiffs need to pass in order to win a section 2 redistricting test. The new test is significantly harder to pass and designed to require plaintiffs to prove intentional racial discrimination \u8211\'20\'13 an extremely difficult burden.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn short, section 2 imposes liability only when the evidence supports a strong inference that the state intentionally drew its districts to afford minority voters less opportunity because of their race,\u8221\'20\'1D Alito wrote.\par \par \uc2 Such a declaration \u8220\'20\'1Cis not only out of line with text, it is also out of line with the history of section 2\u8221\'20\'1D, Richard Hasen, an election law scholar at the \uc2 University of California, Los Angeles{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://electionlawblog.org/?p=155728"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote in a blogpost}}}\uc2 . In 1982, Congress amended the Voting Rights Act to clarify that proving intentional discrimination was not necessary to win a case under section 2.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the first part of the three-part test in a section 2 lawsuit has required plaintiffs to show the minority group alleging discrimination is large and compact enough to constitute a majority in a single-member district. Alito\u8217\'20\'19s decision adds two new requirements to this \u8211\'20\'13 plaintiffs may not consider race in drawing a hypothetical alternative map and must also ensure that it achieves the state\u8217\'20\'19s traditional districting criteria and partisan goals.\par \par \uc2 That change alone is a major blow to the Voting Rights Act. In the US south, voting is highly racially polarized, so drawing a district that prevents racial discrimination is likely to also affect the partisan makeup of a map. It may be impossible for a state to achieve its partisan goals without discriminating against minority voters.\par \par \uc2 It also gives lawmakers virtually unlimited leeway to justify drawing districts that discriminate based on race, Kagan wrote.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSuppose the state asserted that it drew the lines to protect an incumbent, who just so happened to be favored by Black residents,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CThe possibilities are endless. And each would have the same result. Because a section 2 plaintiff \u8217\'20\'19s map could not as well advance the bespoke political (or other) goal(s) favoring the Black voters\u8217\'20\'19 chosen candidate, the suit would fail \u8211\'20\'13 even if non-Black votes, election year in and election year out, had been made to count for nothing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The second and third conditions of the traditional test requires plaintiffs to show that the minority group is politically cohesive and that the majority group votes as a bloc to defeat the minority\u8217\'20\'19s preferred candidate. Alito\u8217\'20\'19s new test requires plaintiffs to show that cohesiveness is driven specifically by race and not by party. That is extremely hard to do when race and party are often closely intertwined.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo in offering evidence of polarized voting preferences, a plaintiff must remove from the equation \u8230\'20\'26 polarized voting preferences,\u8221\'20\'1D Kagan wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CPartisan difference is the way those divergent preferences are expressed \u8211\'20\'13 and the way one racial group\u8217\'20\'19s vote can swamp another\u8217\'20\'19s, again and again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As part of section 2 cases, courts have also looked at the \u8220\'20\'1Ctotality of circumstances\u8221\'20\'1D to assess whether the political process is equally open to minority voters. Plaintiffs often offer evidence of ongoing effects of the legacy of discrimination to show that discrimination still exists. The court\u8217\'20\'19s new test says that evaluation must be focused on \u8220\'20\'1Cpresent-day intentional racial discrimination regarding voting\u8221\'20\'1D. Such direct evidence of intentional racial discrimination rarely exists.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDiscrimination that occurred some time ago, as well as present-day disparities that are characterized as the ongoing \u8216\'20\'18effects of societal discrimination,\u8217\'20\'19 are entitled to much less weight,\u8221\'20\'1D Alito wrote.\par \par \uc2 Civil rights advocates were quick to denounce the decision. Derrick Johnson, the head of the NAACP, said in a statement: \u8220\'20\'1CThe supreme court betrayed Black voters, they betrayed America, and they betrayed our democracy.\u8221\'20\'1D Barack Obama said the decision allowed \u8220\'20\'1Cstate legislatures to gerrymander legislative districts to systematically dilute and weaken the voting power of racial minorities \u8211\'20\'13 so long as they do it under the guise of \u8216\'20\'18partisanship\u8217\'20\'19 rather than explicit \u8216\'20\'18racial bias\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, including Louisiana attorney general Liz Murrill, praised the decision. \u8220\'20\'1CThe supreme court has ended Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s long-running nightmare of federal courts coercing the state to draw a racially discriminatory map. That was always unconstitutional \u8211\'20\'13 and this is a seismic decision reaffirming equal protection under our nation\u8217\'20\'19s laws,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The White House and called the decision \u8220\'20\'1Ca complete and total victory\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe color of one\u8217\'20\'19s skin should not dictate which congressional district you belong in,\u8221\'20\'1D Abigail Jackson, a White House spokesperson, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CWe commend the court for putting an end to the unconstitutional abuse of the Voting Rights Act and protecting civil rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The decision comes after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thearp.org/litigation/callais-v-landry/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 years of legal wrangling}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After the 2020 census, the Republican-controlled state legislature drew a new congressional map in which Black voters comprised a majority in just one district despite being about a third of the state\u8217\'20\'19s population. A group of Black voters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/24/supreme-court-louisiana-redistricting-voting-rights-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued the state in 2022}}}\uc2  under the Voting Rights Act, arguing that the map diluted the influence of Black voters in the state by packing them into one district and spreading them out over the remaining ones.\par \par \uc2 The Black voters won the case and a federal judge blocked Louisiana from using the map and instructed the state to draw a new one with a second majority-Black district. The state complied, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleofields.com/_files/ugd/d177ba_76c94f9453274750ba63189a0dd82eee.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 drawing a new map}}}\uc2  with a second majority-Black congressional district that stretches diagonally across the state from Shreveport to Baton Rouge.\par \par \uc2 But a group of non-Black voters challenged that new map, claiming that voters had unlawfully been sorted by their race in violation of the 14th amendment\u8217\'20\'19s guarantee of equal protection. A three-judge panel {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://vhdshf2oms2wcnsvk7sdv3so.blob.core.windows.net/thearp-media/documents/LA_324-cv-122_198.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed with those plaintiffs}}}\uc2  and blocked the new map from going into effect last year. That decision was paused by the supreme court and the remedial map was used in the 2024 election last fall\u8217\'20\'19s election. Cleo Fields, a Black Democrat, won the seat.\par \par \uc2 During oral arguments in March, Edward Greim, a lawyer for the plaintiffs, said it was obvious that race had predominated in drawing the district because it was so irregularly shaped.\par \par \uc2 But lawyers representing Louisiana as well as the Black voters who brought the original claim said that there was a clear explanation for the strange shape. When they were drawing the map, Louisiana Republicans had wanted to preserve the safe seats of the House speaker, Mike Johnson; the House majority leader, Steve Scalise; and Julia Letlow, a member of the House appropriations committee. They had rejected the possibility of drawing a more compact district in order to preserve those seats.\par \par \uc2 Janai Nelson, a lawyer with the NAACP Legal Defense Fund, who argued in defense of Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s existing maps at the supreme court last year, said that although there might be theoretical opportunities to bring section 2 claims, she did not see any practical way of doing so right now. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no mincing words here. This is a day of tremendous loss,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Press Robinson, a Louisiana voter who led an earlier lawsuit that resulted in the creation of the second majority-Black congressional district in the state, also expressed disappointment in the decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a citizen of Louisiana and living in a state where a third of the population are people of color, I see that our state legislature, the governor, all of the main offices in the state, or super-majority, follow the Republican party, and they are determined to see to it that we not have a voice at all,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260429em4t00461\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
