\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Fema employees who criticized Trump cuts reinstated after months on leave\b0\par\par\uc2 Gabrielle Canon and agencies \par \uc2 928 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2026\par 13:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers wrote \u8216\'20\'18Katrina declaration\u8217\'20\'19, warning that funding cuts made US dangerously unprepared for natural disasters\par \par \uc2 Fourteen employees with the \uc2 US Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2  returned to work this week, after spending eight months on administrative leave for signing a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/aug/25/fema-trump-hurricane-katrina"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public letter criticising the Trump administration}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The so-called \u8220\'20\'1CKatrina declaration\u8221\'20\'1D, sent last August to members of Congress and a federal council formed to help determine Fema\u8217\'20\'19s future, was written as a rebuke from the workers about the dangerous {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/04/donald-trump-disaster-response-preparedness"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 erosion in US capacity to prepare}}}\uc2  for and respond to natural disasters.\par \par \uc2 Timed with the 20th anniversary of Katrina, the catastrophic storm that killed 1,833 people and devastated parts of New Orleans and the Gulf coast in 2005, it served as a warning that the stage was set for history to repeat itself.\par \par \uc2 More than 190 current and former Fema employees signed on to the letter. Thirty-six signed their names. Those who were still actively employed at the agency were put on indefinite paid administrative leave one day later.\par \par \uc2 The group was reinstated briefly in December before being returned to leave, a hiccup a DHS spokesperson at the time blamed on \u8220\'20\'1Cbureaucrats acting outside of their authority\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Abby McIlraith, a Fema emergency management specialist who is among the reinstated workers, said the group received emails on Wednesday instructing them to return to work. On Thursday she was back at the Fema office in Maryland, waiting to regain access to her work devices. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel pretty vindicated,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe did the right thing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Their reinstatement marks one of many reversals made by the new homeland security secretary, \uc2 Markwayne Mullin\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a signal he may be moving away from the harsher approach toward Fema taken by his predecessor, \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , before she was fired as DHS leader.\par \par \uc2 Pressed by the Democratic senator Andy Kim of New Jersey about the suspended staffers\u8217\'20\'19 fate in his Senate confirmation hearing last month, Mullin called whistleblower retaliation unlawful and vowed to work \u8220\'20\'1Cwithin the law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Mullin has also reversed Noem\u8217\'20\'19s policy that her office approve any DHS expenditure over $100,000, and has released more than $1bn in backlogged Fema grants and reimbursements to states, tribes and territories since being sworn in last month.\par \par \uc2 The $100,000 policy was one of several actions criticized in the public letter. Others included the DHS decision to reassign some Fema employees to \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2 ; the failure to appoint a qualified Fema administrator as stipulated by law; and cuts to mitigation programs, preparedness training and the Fema workforce. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.standupforscience.net/fema-katrina-declaration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Many of the concerns outlined}}}\uc2  in the letter remain.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of millions of dollars in national preparedness funding was cut in 2025, and Fema lost roughly a third of its full-time staff \u8211\'20\'13 experienced leaders among them \u8211\'20\'13 to firings, retirements and resignations. The letter also called for Fema to be taken out from under DHS and restored to a cabinet-level agency.\par \par \uc2 The agency is still severely hindered for upcoming high-risk seasons for hurricanes, extreme heat and fires, according to staffers and emergency management experts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am very happy these career civil servants are getting their due and getting back to work,\u8221\'20\'1D said a former Fema employee who spoke to the Guardian under the condition of anonymity. \u8220\'20\'1CBut it might be too little, too late.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The worst effects, experts say, may not reveal themselves until catastrophe strikes.\par \par \uc2 Already, there have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/fema-delay-disaster-response-st-louis-1152f03c?st=4jNbdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 severe delays in aid}}}\uc2  distributed to communities affected by Hurricane Helene, which struck the US south-east in 2024. It took administration officials more than 72 hours to authorise the deployment of federal search-and-rescue teams after the Guadalupe River in Texas surged into a summer camp and through nearby communities last July, a flood that left more than 135 people dead.\par \par \uc2 When deadly tornadoes hit the midwest and Great Plains in March, state and local search-and-rescue teams {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/13/politics/fema-response-tornado-tracking-tool-kristi-noem"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had to deploy without key tornado-tracking tools}}}\uc2 , because a $200,000 Fema contract was allowed to lapse in February.\par \par \uc2 The former employee said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you think about potential lives lost and the people who weren\u8217\'20\'19t made whole because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t get the assistance they needed because there were less people on the job \u8230\'20\'26 what did any of this accomplish besides putting us in a weaker position when it comes to responding to disasters?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump, meanwhile, has repeatedly called for states to take on more responsibility with disaster response and preparedness, but most are not equipped to pick up the slack. It could take years to make key changes, according to Bill Turner, the emergency management director at the Connecticut department of emergency services and public protection, who also serves as the resilience committee chair for the National Emergency Management Association, a non-profit professional association for US state and territory directors.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s budget proposal for next year would slash grant programs relied on to increase preparedness at the state and local level by $1.3bn if adopted.\par \par \uc2 Fema\u8217\'20\'19s future could become more clear next week, when the Trump-appointed Fema Review Council will present its highly anticipated and months-overdue recommendation report. It is expected to propose sweeping changes to the agency.\par \par \uc2 McIlreath said she was paying close attention to what changes the council proposes and was undeterred by what happened to her and her colleagues. She said: \u8220\'20\'1CUntil Fema capabilities are restored and disaster survivors are served I\u8217\'20\'19m going to continue speaking out.\u8221\'20\'1DThe Associated Press contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260501em5100002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
