\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A day of loss for our democracy\u8217\'20\'19: civil rights groups slam supreme court ruling that weakens key part of Voting Rights Act \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Mackey, Lucy Campbell, Lauren Gambino and Tom Ambrose \par \uc2 11,238 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2026\par 02:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This live blog is now closed.Sign up for the Breaking News US emailUS supreme court \u8216\'20\'18demolishes\u8217\'20\'19 key Voting Rights Act provision that prevented racial discrimination\par \par \uc2 3.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 This concludes our live coverage of the day in US politics. Here are the latest developments:\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority struck down a major element of the Voting Rights Act which protects against racial discrimination in redistricting, in a ruling that paves the way for aggressive gerrymandering in states across the nation that could affect elections for years to come.\par \par \uc2 * \u8220\'20\'1CToday, six Supreme Court justices closed their eyes to the continuing realities of racial discrimination, racial injustice, and inequity,\u8221\'20\'1D voting rights activist Bishop William Barber said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn 2020, after Biden was elected President, with majorities in the House and Senate, many of us argued that COVID-19 relief to corporations shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t pass without a renewal of the Voting Rights Act and a commitment to living wages. This did not happen, so here we are.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * The Florida Legislature approved a new congressional map intended to maximize Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 advantage in the state as part of the national redistricting battle that Republicans launched ahead of this year\u8217\'20\'19s midterms.\par \par \uc2 * Outgoing Federal Reserve chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said he will stay on as a central bank governor when his leadership term ends in just over two weeks.\par \par \uc2 * The Department of Justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/three-individuals-federally-charged-assault-and-intimidating-turning-point-usa-journalist"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  on Wednesday that three anti-ICE protesters have been charged with allegedly assaulting Savannah Hernandez, a rightwing video journalist who was shoved to the ground during a skirmish with three members of a family outside an immigration detention facility in St Paul Minnesota this month.\par \par \uc2 1.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Trump threatens to withdraw US troops from Germany after chancellor's Iran war criticism\par \par \uc2 Two days after Germany\u8217\'20\'19s chancellor said that the US \u8220\'20\'1Cis being humiliated\u8221\'20\'1D by Iran\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cvery skilled\u8221\'20\'1D negotiators, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  suggested on Wednesday that the US could withdraw some troops from that country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe United States is studying and reviewing the possible reduction of Troops in Germany, with a determination to be made over the next short period of time,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/116490196510215628"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the president wrote}}}\uc2  on his social media platform.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Americans clearly have no strategy,\u8221\'20\'1D Germany\u8217\'20\'19s chancellor, \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/27/us-humiliated-iran-leadership-trump-merz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told students}}}\uc2  on Monday. \u8220\'20\'1CThe problem with conflicts like this is always that you don\u8217\'20\'19t just have to go in; you also have to get out again. We saw that all too painfully in Afghanistan, for 20 years. We saw it in Iraq. So this whole affair is, as I said, ill-considered, to say the least.\par \par \uc2 Merz also suggested that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s self-described genius for deal-making was nowhere to be seen in talks with Iran.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Iranians are obviously very skilled at negotiating, or rather, very skilful at not negotiating, letting the Americans travel to Islamabad and then leave again without any result,\u8221\'20\'1D Merz said. \u8220\'20\'1CAn entire nation is being humiliated by the Iranian leadership\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first response to Merz, in a social media post on Tuesday, was to repeat the same lie about the German chancellor he told recently about Pope Leo.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Chancellor of Germany, \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2 , thinks it\u8217\'20\'19s OK for Iran to have a Nuclear Weapon,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump wrote, despite the fact that the German leader was repeatedly said Iran should never be allowed to have a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/30/trump-threatens-to-reduce-troop-numbers-in-germany-amid-growing-row-with-nato-allies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump threatens to reduce troop numbers in Germany amid growing row with Nato allies}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.57am BST\par \par \uc2 12.46am BST\par \par \uc2 US plans to remove aircraft carrier from Middle East - report\par \par \uc2 A the stalemate in the strait of Hormuz drags on, a US aircraft carrier, the USS \uc2 Gerald R Ford\uc2 , will leave the Middle East within days, bringing about 4,500 sailors who have been deployed for 10 months home, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/national-security/2026/04/29/us-aircraft-carrier-iran-war/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Washington Post reports}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Although the Ford is reportedly leaving the Red Sea, the US will still have two aircraft carriers named for Republican presidents in the region, the USS George H W Bush and the USS \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , to enforce the US blockade in the Arabian Sea against vessels carrying oil or goods from Iranian ports.\par \par \uc2 This month the Ford {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.usni.org/2026/04/15/uss-gerald-r-ford-breaks-post-cold-war-deployment-record"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 broke a record}}}\uc2  for the longest deployment of any aircraft carrier since the Cold War, reaching 295 days on 15 April. In March, a laundry fire broke out on the ship, which has also been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.navytimes.com/news/your-navy/2026/01/22/the-navys-largest-ship-continues-to-be-plagued-by-plumbing-issues/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 plagued}}}\uc2  with plumbing problems.\par \par \uc2 Photographs of scant rations and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2026/04/16/iran-war-mail-packages-middle-east/89609308007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mostly empty dinner plates}}}\uc2  on the USS \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , shared with USA Today this month by family members of sailors and Marines on board, prompted concerns about how long their deployment to the region can last.\par \par \uc2 The mother of one sailor on the USS \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  told the newspaper she was worried that her son had lost 20 pounds since his deployment began, thanks to meals like one dry meat square on a small scoop of rice and off-color burger patty with a side of liquid nacho cheese.\par \par \uc2 12.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Florida moves to redraw congressional maps to boost Republicans\par \par \uc2 The Florida Legislature approved a new congressional map intended to maximize Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 advantage in the state as part of the national redistricting battle that Donald Trump launched ahead of this year\u8217\'20\'19s midterms, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 The vote came just two days after Florida\u8217\'20\'19s governor, Ron DeSantis, unveiled his proposal and the same day that the US supreme court rolled back a key provision of the Voting Rights Act. That decision makes it harder for Democrats to challenge Republican efforts to redraw congressional districts in ways that limit the influence of nonwhite voters.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis\u8217\'20\'19 map could increase Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 advantage in Florida\u8217\'20\'19s House delegation to 24 to 4, up from the current split of 20 to 8. The potential four-seat gain is the same as what Virginia Democrats expect from a recent redistricting referendum , which is being challenged in state court there.\par \par \uc2 Florida\u8217\'20\'19s new districts are certain to face lawsuits as well, especially because the state constitution prohibits redistricting for explicitly partisan purposes.\par \par \uc2 12.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Civil rights activist William Barber condemns gutting of Voting Rights Act\par \par \uc2 Bishop William Barber, a veteran voting rights activist, lent his voice to the chorus of civil rights leaders condemning the US supreme court decision to effectively gut the Voting Rights Act of its protections for Black and Brown voters.\par \par \uc2 Barber, who helped start the Moral Mondays series of protests, was arrested at sit-ins at the offices of Democratic senators Krysten Sinema and Joe Manchin in 2021, while calling on the then Democratic majority in the Senate to create an exception to the filibuster to pass voting rights legislation.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Barber said:\par \par \uc2 11.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump claims Jerome Powell is staying on Federal Reserve board 'because he can\u8217\'20\'19t get a job anywhere else'\par \par \uc2 With what might pass for restraint, for him, Donald Trump limited his response to the news that Jerome Powell{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/apr/29/fed-jerome-powell-decision-hold-interest-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will remain on the Federal Reserve board}}}\uc2  after his term as chair ends in May to just 21 insulting words.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/116490221112397902"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a brief post}}}\uc2  on his social media platform, the president said: \u8220\'20\'1CJerome \u8216\'20\'18Too Late\u8217\'20\'19 Powell wants to stay at the Fed because he can\u8217\'20\'19t get a job anywhere else \u8212\'20\'14 Nobody wants him.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Powell, whose term as a Fed governor runs until January 2028, could have retired after he is replaced as chair, but, having endured months of insults from the president for refusing to disregard the threat of inflation and lower interest rates, and a criminal investigation that appears off, for now, the central banker appears determined to deny the president the chance to replace him with a more pliant appointee.\par \par \uc2 11.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Three anti-ICE protesters charged with assault for skirmish with Turning Point USA influencer in Minnesota\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/three-individuals-federally-charged-assault-and-intimidating-turning-point-usa-journalist"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  on Wednesday that three anti-ICE protesters have been charged with allegedly assaulting Savannah Hernandez, a rightwing video journalist who was shoved to the ground during a skirmish with three members of a family outside an immigration detention facility in St Paul, Minnesota this month.\par \par \uc2 Hernandez, a former host for the far-right, conspiracy theory outlet Infowars who now creates content for Frontlines, a Turning Point USA streaming show, claimed that she was assaulted for merely reporting on the protest.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.mnd.233615/gov.uscourts.mnd.233615.1.0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The indictment}}}\uc2  of the Ostroushkos for allegedly assaulting Hernandez comes after a weeks-long campaign in the rightwing media to punish the protesters.\par \par \uc2 Three days after the incident, vice-president JD Vance{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/shorts/_oJjgeV6jis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  at a Turning Point USA event in Georgia that he had asked the FBI director, Kash Patel, what the administration was doing about it. Patel had assured him, Vance said, that multiple agents were already investigating.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to us the video to try to go after the people who assaulted her and then try to defund the networks that fund those radicals who are going around assaulting activists,\u8221\'20\'1D Vance said, apparently sharing the perspective of anti-ICE protesters that Hernandez is a partisan activist rather than a nonpartisan journalist.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/live/KuSOPFFVB5s?si=yWUlpuqPumy0v5_S&t=3732"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a recent podcast interview}}}\uc2 , however, two of the three family members indicted by the federal grand jury, Chris and Deyanna Ostroushko blamed Hernandez for starting the violence. The melee began, they said, when Hernandez shoved her hand into the face of their daughter Paige Ostroushko, who was blowing a whistle at the influencer to disrupt her filming.\par \par \uc2 Hernandez\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/Savsays/status/2043088956351189051"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 viral clip}}}\uc2  of the incident does not show the start of the physical confrontation clearly, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/shorts/DOmQohAf6QM"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another protester\u8217\'20\'19s recording of the confrontation}}}\uc2  does.\par \par \uc2 That video, recorded by Oskar Quentin, seemed to show that Hernandez did shove Paige Ostroushko\u8217\'20\'19s face twice, knocking the whistle out of her mouth, before the young woman took a swing at Hernandez and knocked her against a fence.\par \par \uc2 Quentin\u8217\'20\'19s video also shows that when Hernandez got back up, she was confronted by Deyanna Ostroushko, and the two women exchanged blows. Moments later Chris Ostroushko was then filmed shoving the influencer to the ground. Hernandez then she got back up and appeared to punch Paige Ostroushko in the face before being tackled to the ground.\par \par \uc2 During {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AB63tN8FcbI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an emotional interview}}}\uc2  with Laura Ingraham on Fox News after the incident, Hernandez claimed that she had been confronted and assaulted \u8220\'20\'1Cfor journalism\u8221\'20\'1D because protesters knew that she worked for Turning Point USA, the conservative political movement founded by the late Charlie Kirk.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://youtu.be/56XHzlXD2To?si=n2typ2Z1FiLaSDfh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hernandez\u8217\'20\'19s video of the protest}}}\uc2  in Minnesota this month, which showed her arguing with protesters before the skirmish, another demonstrator could be heard shouting that she was \u8220\'20\'1Ca paid agitator\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 While it is impossible to excuse assaulting anyone for filming a protest, it is not hard to see why anti-ICE activists do not consider Hernandez a nonpartisan reporter. She was previously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.foxnews.com/media/young-conservative-women-stand-liberal-mob-ingraham"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 celebrated on Fox News}}}\uc2  in the summer of 2020 as a political activist who had herself filmed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/sav_says_/status/1282740963971137536"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 holding up a sign}}}\uc2  that read \u8220\'20\'1CPolice Lives Matter\u8221\'20\'1D during a Black Lives Matter protest that followed the murder of George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 Hernandez was invited to the White House twice last year. In February, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://youtu.be/r0P_27cujgk?si=-40xP7-68A3nE3Ss&t=48"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she said}}}\uc2  she was one of a group of influencers who met there with Vance, Patel and then attorney general Pam Bondi and was handed a binder of previously released material on the late sex offender Jeffrey Epstein.\par \par \uc2 In October, her profile was further elevated when Hernandez was one of 11 rightwing influencers who describe themselves as journalists {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/16/rightwing-influencers-trump-antifa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invited to the White House}}}\uc2  to brief Donald Trump and his attorney general, FBI director, homeland security secretary and deputy attorney general on the supposed threat from antifascists. Eight of the 11 conservative content creators who called for a crackdown on leftwing protesters that day were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bsky.app/profile/jaredlholt.bsky.social/post/3m2re73uauk2m"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 current or former employees}}}\uc2  of Turning Point USA.\par \par \uc2 Hernandez, who has appeared regularly on Fox News, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cjr.org/feature/savannah-hernandez-turning-point-usa-canal-street-ice-raid-law-and-order-influencer.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Columbia Journalism Review}}}\uc2  in January that she identifies as a \u8220\'20\'1CTrump supporter, absolutely.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.38am BST\par \par \uc2 9.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris: court ruling 'designed to give an upper hand to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s Republican Party'\par \par \uc2 The US Supreme court decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to effectively gut the Voting Rights Act}}}\uc2  has been denounced as \u8220\'20\'1Cmotivated by politics\u8221\'20\'1D by Kamala Harris, the former vice-president who says she is contemplating another run for the presidency in 2028.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/p/DXuoHooCD-P/?img_index=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a statement}}}\uc2  released on social media, Harris said:\par \par \uc2 The losing candidate in the 2024 presidential election went on to say that the fight now returns to the states, particularly those of the old confederacy, which are expected to redraw their congressional maps to dilute the power of votes cast by Black Americans in ways that would have been illegal before the court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTheir politically-motivated power grab is meant to protect elected Republicans from any consequences for their failure to make groceries, gas, health care, or housing more affordable for you and your family,\u8221\'20\'1D Harris wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CThey want to cheat and choose their voters, instead of the voters deciding who they choose.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 9.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 Summary of the day so far: outrage as supreme court deals major blow to landmark voting law\par \par \uc2 Today, the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority struck down a major element of the Voting Rights Act which protects against racial discrimination in redistricting, in a ruling that paves the way for aggressive gerrymandering in states across the nation that could affect elections for years to come.\par \par \uc2 As my colleague Sam Levine notes, at the heart of the case, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/24/supreme-court-louisiana-redistricting-voting-rights-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana v Callais}}}\uc2 , was a question of how much lawmakers are allowed to consider race when they redraw districts to ensure that black voters are adequately represented.\par \par \uc2 In a 6-3 decision, split along partisan lines, the court struck down a majority-black congressional district in Louisiana, rendering ineffective section 2 of the Voting Rights Act, the last remaining powerful provision of the 1965 civil rights law that prevents racial discrimination in voting. Section 2 has long been used to ensure minority voters are treated fairly in redistricting.\par \par \uc2 The ruling gives lawmakers permission to draw districting plans that weaken the influence of black and other minority voters. It comes as Donald Trump has pushed for red states to redraw their congressional maps in ways that would help Republicans win more seats in this year\u8217\'20\'19s elections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAllowing race to play any part in government decision-making represents a departure from the constitutional rule that applies in almost every other context,\u8221\'20\'1D conservative Justice Samuel Alito wrote for the majority opinion. \u8220\'20\'1CCompliance with section 2 thus could not justify the state\u8217\'20\'19s use of race-based redistricting here. The state\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to satisfy the middle district\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, although understandable, was an unconstitutional racial gerrymander.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a dissenting opinion, liberal Justice Elena Kagan wrote the court had now accomplished a \u8220\'20\'1Cdemolition of the Voting Rights Act\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Reactions have poured in from lawmakers and civil rights groups, condemning the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 * The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) called the ruling \u8220\'20\'1Ca devastating blow to what remains of the Voting Rights Act\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Ca major setback for our nation\u8221\'20\'1D. The ruling is \u8220\'20\'1Ca license for corrupt politicians who want to rig the system by silencing entire communities\u8221\'20\'1D, president Derrick Johnson said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Supreme Court betrayed Black voters, they betrayed America, and they betrayed our democracy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Former president Barack Obama said the ruling \u8220\'20\'1Ceffectively gut[s] a key pillar of the Voting Rights Act\u8221\'20\'1D andfrees \u8220\'20\'1Cstate legislatures to gerrymander legislative districts to systematically dilute and weaken the voting power of racial minorities - so long as they do it under the guise of \u8216\'20\'18partisanship\u8217\'20\'19 rather than explicit \u8216\'20\'18racial bias\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a day of tremendous loss. It\u8217\'20\'19s a day of loss for our democracy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Janai Nelson, who leads NAACP LDF and argued the case at the supreme court. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a day of loss for critical protections for the right to vote that have served our multiracial democracy for over six decades. It\u8217\'20\'19s a day of loss for Black voters in Louisiana who have counted on fair maps to allow them representation that they have been denied for their entire existence in that state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Sophia Lin Lakin, the director of the ACLU Voting Rights Project, called the ruling \u8220\'20\'1Ca profound betrayal of the civil rights movement\u8221\'20\'1D. Today\u8217\'20\'19s decision in Callais renders Section 2 moot as \u8220\'20\'1Cit will be difficult, if not impossible, to enforce in the vast majority of cases,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 * Former House speaker Nancy Pelosi called the ruling a \u8220\'20\'1Cnew blow\u8221\'20\'1D against the \u8220\'20\'1Csacred right to vote\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThe consequences will be felt across the country: fewer voices heard, fewer communities represented and a democracy diminished,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 * Senate majority leader, Chuck Schumer, said, the \u8220\'20\'1Cawful\u8221\'20\'1D decision represented \u8220\'20\'1Canother step towards resurrecting the Jim Crow south\u8221\'20\'1D. The ruling, he said, \u8220\'20\'1Copens the floodgates for states across the south to redraw their Congressional districts and make voters of color essentially invisible in our democracy\u8221\'20\'1D. The court, he said, was \u8220\'20\'1Ctrying to give Republicans a leg up, an illegitimate leg up, in future elections\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * His House counterpart, Hakeem Jeffries, called the decision \u8220\'20\'1Ccorrupt\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CVoter suppression is a way of life for Donald Trump and far right extremists on the Supreme Court,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CRepublicans know they cannot win a free and fair election in November and so they are desperate to rig it. We will never let them succeed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Georgia senator Reverend Raphael Warnock said the decision \u8220\'20\'1Cfurther ravaged\u8221\'20\'1D the Voting Rights Act and left the country \u8220\'20\'1Cat a crossroads where politicians are picking their voters\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CClearly, we are straying further from the core voting principles that helped create the diverse body that people see representing them today,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe must restore the Voting Rights Act and ban gerrymandering. Our democracy is on the line.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Representative Troy Carter, whose predominately black congressional district encompasses New Orleans, said \u8220\'20\'1Cthe consequences of the high court\u8217\'20\'19s decision will be \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediate and severe\u8221\'20\'1D and that Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s two majority-black congressional districts are now at risk of being dismantled. \u8220\'20\'1CWithout the protections of the Voting Rights Act, there is no evidence to suggest that Black voters in our state will be able to elect candidates of their choice,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 * New Orleans mayor Helena Moreno, a Democrat who represents the largest city in Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s other predominantly black congressional district, said\par \par \uc2 the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling was \u8220\'20\'1Ca step backward\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CStriking down a district that reflected diversity suppresses voices and weakens our democracy. We should be working to expand representation, not roll it back,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 * Lauren Groh-Wargo, executive director of Fair Fight Action, said the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cguts\u8221\'20\'1D voting rights protection while \u8220\'20\'1Cpretending to uphold it\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CIt allows states, counties and cities to shield their discriminatory maps by claiming they are advancing their own partisan interests, ignoring that race and party are highly correlated in places across the country, particularly the south,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 For more reaction to this landmark decision, please read my colleague Fabiola Cineas\u8217\'20\'19s piece here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/black-lawmakers-supreme-court-voting-rights-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Black lawmakers decry supreme court voting decision: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re going backwards\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 8.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump administration's legal attacks are 'battering' the Fed, Powell says\par \par \uc2 Asked about his decision to stay on as a Fed governor, Powell said his concern was about \u8220\'20\'1Cthe series of legal attacks on the Fed, which threaten our ability to conduct monetary policy without considering political factors\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mind verbal criticism by elected officials, but said the administration\u8217\'20\'19s legal attacks on him \u8220\'20\'1Care unprecedented in our 113-year history, and there are ongoing threats of additional such actions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 8.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 Powell also congratulated Kevin Warsh on his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/apr/29/king-charles-new-york-donald-trump-zohran-mamdani-iran-us-politics-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-69f236b38f087c2bf49475fa#block-69f236b38f087c2bf49475fa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 advancement towards being confirmed}}}\uc2  as his successor as Federal Reserve chair by the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Powell described the Senate vote as \u8220\'20\'1Can important step\u8221\'20\'1D, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CI wish him well as that process continues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 8.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 More now from outgoing Fed chair Jerome Powell, who said that while he US economy is expanding, \u8220\'20\'1Cinflation has moved up with growing global energy prices\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDevelopments in the Middle East are contributing to high levels of uncertainty,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Consumer sentiment was \u8220\'20\'1Cresilient\u8221\'20\'1D though, he added, and \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomic activity has been expanding at a solid pace\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 8.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Barack Obama: court ruling frees states to 'systematically weaken voting power of racial minorities'\par \par \uc2 Former president Barack Obama has issued this statement in reaction to the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s landmark {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision}}}\uc2  today to \u8220\'20\'1Ceffectively gut a key pillar of theVoting Rights Act\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said the ruling frees \u8220\'20\'1Cstate legislatures to gerrymander legislative districts to systematically dilute and weaken the voting power of racial minorities - so long as they do it under the guise of \u8216\'20\'18partisanship\u8217\'20\'19 rather than explicit \u8216\'20\'18racial bias\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 8.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 I asked Sophia Lin Lakin, the ACLU\u8217\'20\'19s top voting rights lawyer, and Janai Nelson, the NAACPLDF lawyer who argued in defense of Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s map at the supreme court, whether or not there was any pathway for continuing to bring Section 2 claims in federal court after today\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTheoretically, yes, there are still some possibilities there ... academically speaking there are some pathways there,\u8221\'20\'1D Nelson said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I think that those require conditions that I don\u8217\'20\'19t see present based on current demographics, voting patterns and what has become an increasingly hyper polarized partisan landscape.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lakin added that non-partisan elections are one area where plaintiffs might have some additional remaining leeway to bring Section 2 claims.\par \par \uc2 8.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Powell to stay on as Fed governor after term as chair ends\par \par \uc2 Outgoing Federal Reserve chair Jerome Powell said he will stay on as a central bank governor when his leadership term ends in just over two weeks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter my term as chair ends on May 15, I will continue to serve as a governor for a period of time to be determined,\u8221\'20\'1D Powell said, adding that he planned to \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep a low profile\u8221\'20\'1D as a governor.\par \par \uc2 Powell noted US attorney Jeanine Pirro\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/usattypirro/status/2047679907312939264?s=46"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announcement}}}\uc2  on Friday, ending the criminal investigation against him but also cautioning that she could reopen it.\par \par \uc2 The move cleared a hurdle for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s pick Kevin Warsh to be confirmed, as GOP senator Thom Tillis had said he wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t vote to confirm him until the probe into Powell was dropped. The Fed\u8217\'20\'19s inspector general has instead been asked to look into potential \u8220\'20\'1Cbuilding cost overruns\u8221\'20\'1D in the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s renovation of its Washington DC headquarters, Pirro said.\par \par \uc2 He said he would not be leaving the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s board of governors until the DOJ\u8217\'20\'19s investigation into him is \u8220\'20\'1Cwell and truly over\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He added: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m encouraged by recent developments, and I\u8217\'20\'19m watching the remaining steps in this process carefully. My decisions on these matters will continue to be guided entirely by what I believe is in the best interest of the institution and the people we serve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 7.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump was then asked a follow-up question about the ruling. The president asked again when the ruling came out (this morning!), before asking the reporter if it was a win for Republicans. Told that it was, he said:\par \par \uc2 He was asked if he believed more Republican governors should pursue redrawing their congressional maps following the ruling, given that early voting in some places begins on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Trump said it would depend on the state and whether they have time to do it, but generally they should do it.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 7.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump says 'that\u8217\'20\'19s the kind of ruling I like' when asked about supreme court decision\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump was also asked for his reaction to today\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling on the Voting Rights Act}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 to which the president said he didn\u8217\'20\'19t know about it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have to tell me, when did the ruling come out?\u8221\'20\'1D he asked the reporter, adding he\u8217\'20\'19s been meeting with the astronauts and with contractors trying to get his ballroom built.\par \par \uc2 When he was told that the ruling might help Republicans gain more congressional seats in the south, the president responded:\par \par \uc2 He then asked when this happened, and was told this morning.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 7.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Just before we get to that, Donald Trump has been taking questions from reporters in the Oval Office following his meeting with the Artemis II astronauts.\par \par \uc2 The president said he had a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery good conversation\u8221\'20\'1D with Vladimir Putin today about Ukraine and Iran.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think he\u8217\'20\'19d like to see a solution, and that\u8217\'20\'19s good,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, though it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t clear if he was talking about Ukraine or Iran, or both (unlikely).\par \par \uc2 Trump then said that he \u8220\'20\'1Csuggested a little bit of a ceasefire\u8221\'20\'1D to Putin \u8220\'20\'1Cand I think he might do that\u8221\'20\'1D. He then asked the reporter if Putin had announced it yet (spoiler: he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t).\par \par \uc2 7.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 And with that, Jerome Powell is due to shortly give what will likely be his last press conference as chair of the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ll be watching and will bring you any key lines that come out of that.\par \par \uc2 7.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Fed leaves interest rates unchanged in defiance of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s calls for cuts\par \par \uc2 The US Federal Reserve left interest rates unchanged after its latest board meeting, defying once again Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s call for a cut as the central bank prepares for a leadership shake-up next month.\par \par \uc2 Today, Fed officials continued to cite elevated inflation, slow job growth and uncertainty in the Middle East as reasons why rates were left untouched.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJobs gains have remained low, on average, and the unemployment rate has been little changed in recent months. Inflation is elevated, in part reflecting the recent increase in global energy prices,\u8221\'20\'1D the board said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 While only one of the board\u8217\'20\'19s 12 voting members voted against leaving the rate unchanged, the Fed board also signaled growing dissent within its ranks: three members supported maintaining the current rate, but did not agree with the Fed suggesting it will lower rates later this year.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\u8217\'20\'19s meeting ended hours after the US Senate banking committee confirmed former Fed governor Kevin Warsh, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/apr/29/king-charles-new-york-donald-trump-zohran-mamdani-iran-us-politics-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with%3Ablock-69f236b38f087c2bf49475fa#block-69f236b38f087c2bf49475fa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 clearing a procedural path}}}\uc2  for the whole Senate to confirm him as new chair of the central bank.\par \par \uc2 Warsh is expected to be more amenable to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s calls for a rate cut than current chair Jerome Powell, who has been the target of hostile attacks toward himself and the central bank over its rates agenda. But with just one vote of 12 on the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s rate-setting board, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/apr/22/trump-fed-chair-kevin-warsh-interest-rate-cut"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Warsh can\u8217\'20\'19t deliver}}}\uc2  cuts without the support of his fellow board members.\par \par \uc2 Questions still remain over whether Powell will stay on the Fed board after his term ends 15 May. Powell can stay on the board until his term as a Fed governor is up in 2028. Though it would be unprecedented in modern history for a Fed chair to stay on, Powell has suggested it\u8217\'20\'19s still a possibility.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/apr/29/fed-jerome-powell-decision-hold-interest-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fed leaves interest rates unchanged in defiance of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s calls for cuts}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 7.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court appears inclined to back Trump's push to end TPS for Haitian and Syrian immigrants\par \par \uc2 The supreme court appeared inclined to back Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s push to strip temporary protected status (TPS) for hundreds of thousands of Haitians and Syrians in the United States, as part of his harsh crackdown on immigration rights.\par \par \uc2 The case will likely have broader implications for the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s wider push to end legal protections for migrants fleeing war and natural disaster, which could potentially effect up to 1.3 million people from over 17 countries, exposing them to possible deportation.\par \par \uc2 The justices earlier heard arguments in the administration\u8217\'20\'19s appeal of rulings by federal judges in New York and Washington DC, halting its actions to terminate TPS previously provided by the government to allow more than 350,000 people from Haiti (following a major earthquake there in 2010) and 6,100 from Syria (after the country descended into civil war in 2012) to remain and work in the US.\par \par \uc2 The court last year allowed the administration to end TPS for hundreds of thousands of Venezuelans.\par \par \uc2 Today, several of the conservative justices appeared sympathetic toward the administration\u8217\'20\'19s arguments that the law limits what courts can do on the government\u8217\'20\'19s decision to end or extend protections.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, liberal JusticesSonia Sotomayor and KetanjiBrownJackson suggested that the decision to end the program was racially motivated, citing Trump\u8217\'20\'19s inflammatory, racist language about Haitian people during his last election campaign.\par \par \uc2 The decision will likely come down to the votes of Chief Justice John Roberts and Justice Amy Coney Barrett. A decision is expected in late June or early July.\par \par \uc2 6.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Bondi to appear before House oversight panel over Epstein files\par \par \uc2 The House oversight and government reform committee said earlier that former attorney general Pam Bondi will now appear before the panel on 29 May to answer questions about the Department of Justice\u8217\'20\'19s handling of the Jeffrey Epstein investigation and its release of the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 The announcement of the date came shortly after the Democrats on the committee announced that they had filed a civil {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://oversightdemocrats.house.gov/imo/media/doc/garcca_054_xml.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 contempt resolution}}}\uc2  against Bondi after she did not appear for her deposition earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 This morning, Robert Garcia, the top Democrat on the committee, announced the contempt resolution, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/OversightDems/status/2049479253566067168"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 saying in a statement}}}\uc2  that Bondi had \u8220\'20\'1Cillegally defied our committee, skipped her deposition, and has refused to cooperate\u8221\'20\'1D, adding that Democrats had introduced the contempt measure \u8220\'20\'1Cto hold her accountable\u8221\'20\'1D.Read Anna\u8217\'20\'19s full report here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/pam-bondi-epstein-files-house-oversight-committee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pam Bondi to appear before House oversight panel over Epstein files}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 6.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 Has King Charles salvaged the US-UK so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial relationship\u8221\'20\'1D with his charm offensive on Donald Trump?\par \par \uc2 In the latest edition of our Today in Focus podcast, Helen Pidd speaks to Guardian columnist and host of Politics Weekly America Jonathan Freedland to unpack the British royals\u8217\'20\'19 state visit so far.\par \par \uc2 6.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back at Pete Hegseth\u8217\'20\'19s appearance before the House armed services committee, representative Sara Jacobs of California had a tense back and forth with the defense secretary, going through Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s unhinged social media posts over recent months.\par \par \uc2 It started when Jacobs asked how he would be able to explain Trump\u8217\'20\'19s mental state, after he demanded in Truth Social posts that Iran \u8220\'20\'1Copen the fucking strait\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CA Whole Civilization Will Die Tonight\u8221\'20\'1D. In return, Hegseth asked whether she asked Joe Biden those same types of questions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow do you explain to your constituents what happened on October 7, or what happened, or what happened when the troops were left? How did you explain that to the Marines that didn\u8217\'20\'19t get medal? We restored their medals because Joe Biden was asleep at the wheel,\u8221\'20\'1D Hegseth said.\par \par \uc2 Jacobs then pulled out the post the president shared that included an AI-generated image {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/13/trump-ai-image-christ-like-figure-backlash"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 depicting himself as a Christ-like figure}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which Christian supporters of the president decried as \u8220\'20\'1Cdisgusting\u8221\'20\'1D, and which Trump ended up deleting after the backlash.\par \par \uc2 Hegseth brushed off the questions as someone digging for better ratings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMr Secretary, if you think that this is what ratings looks like, then maybe we should be questioning your mental stability,\u8221\'20\'1D Jacobs said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 6.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 'Very, very deeply disappointed': plaintiff in voting rights case speaks after supreme court loss\par \par \uc2 Press Robinson,\par \par \uc2 the lead plaintiff in an earlier lawsuit that struck down Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s congressional map leading to the creation of a second majority-Black district told reporters \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t say that I am surprised by the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision, but I\u8217\'20\'19m very, very deeply disappointed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs a citizen of Louisiana and living in a state where 1/3 of the population of people of color. I see that our state legislature, the governor, all of the main offices in the state, or super majority follow the Republican Party, and they are determined to see to it that we not have a voice at all,\u8221\'20\'1D Robinson said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 6.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Associated Press is reporting from Alexandria that the former FBI Director James Comey has made a brief appearance in court. The AP reports:\par \par \uc2 6.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 NAACP official calls supreme court ruling on voting 'a day of loss for critical protections'\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Sam Levine is on a press call with the NAACP Legal Defense Fund and the ACLU, who represented those defending Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s existing maps.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no mincing words here. This is a day of tremendous loss. It\u8217\'20\'19s a day of loss for our democracy,\u8221\'20\'1D Janai Nelson, who leads NAACP LDF and argued the case at the supreme court, told reporters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a day of loss for critical protections for the right to vote that have served our multiracial democracy for over six decades. It\u8217\'20\'19s a day of loss for Black voters in Louisiana who have counted on fair maps to allow them representation that they have been denied for their entire existence in that state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe implications are as bad as we thought they could be,\u8221\'20\'1D Nelson said.\par \par \uc2 Sophia Lin Lakin, the director of the ACLU Voting Rights Project, says today\u8217\'20\'19s decision in Callais renders Section 2 moot as \u8220\'20\'1Cit will be difficult, if not impossible, to enforce in the vast majority of cases.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s decision is a profound betrayal of the civil rights movement,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 6.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 James Comey surrenders amid justice department charges over social media post\par \par \uc2 Former FBI director James Comey on Wednesday surrendered to law enforcement at federal court in the Eastern District of Virginia, according to CNN, citing a source familiar.\par \par \uc2 His hearing was set to begin at 1pm EST. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s justice department filed has charged Comey with making threats against the president, stemming from a picture he posted on Instagram while on vacation last year in which sea shells were arranged to say \u8220\'20\'1C86 47\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Comey said in a video message on Substack on Tuesday that he is \u8220\'20\'1Cstill innocent\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 5.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Warsh clears key Senate hurdle to replace Fed chair Powell\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, KevinWarsh, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s pick to lead the Federal Reserve, cleared a key procedural hurdle on Wednesday, opening the way for him to succeed Jerome Powell next month amid the White House\u8217\'20\'19s unprecedented efforts to exert control over the world\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful central bank.\par \par \uc2 Warsh\u8217\'20\'19s nomination was approved in a 13 to 11 vote, strictly along party lines with Republicans supporting the nomination, setting up a confirmation vote in the US Senate in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 All 13 Republicans on \u8203\'20\'0Bthe panel voted in support of Warsh after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/us-senator-tillis-says-hes-ready-advance-confirmation-warsh-fed-chair-2026-04-26/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Thom Tillis}}}\uc2 , a North Carolina senator, dropped his opposition following the Department of Justice\u8217\'20\'19s \u8203\'20\'0Bdecision on Friday to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/justice-dept-close-investigation-federal-reserve-renovations-us-attorney-pirro-2026-04-24/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 end a criminal investigation}}}\uc2  into Powell that Tillis viewed as a threat to the \u8288\'20\'60Fed\u8217\'20\'19s political independence.\par \par \uc2 The panel\u8217\'20\'19s 11 Democrats, who say they doubt Warsh\u8217\'20\'19s promise to set policy without regard to the president\u8217\'20\'19s wishes, \u8203\'20\'0Bvoted against him.\par \par \uc2 In a statement before the vote, Elizabeth Warren, a Democratic senator and ranking member of the Senate\u8217\'20\'19s banking committee, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.banking.senate.gov/newsroom/minority/warren-delivers-opening-remarks-ahead-of-first-ever-party-line-committee-vote-on-fed-chair"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 repeated}}}\uc2  her concern that Warsh will be a \u8220\'20\'1Csock puppet\u8221\'20\'1D for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/kevin-warsh-fed-chair-powell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kevin Warsh clears key Senate hurdle to replace Fed chair Powell}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 5.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers are celebrating the ruling. Here\u8217\'20\'19s a few reactions:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGreat news,\u8221\'20\'1D Utah senator Mike Lee said on X. \u8220\'20\'1CRace-based gerrymanders have no place in our country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHuge,\u8221\'20\'1D senator John Cornyn of Texas cheered.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 5.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 'A democracy diminished': Pelosi urges Congress to act after supreme court voting decision\par \par \uc2 Former House Speaker Nancy Pelosi called the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling in Louisiana v Callais}}}\uc2  a \u8220\'20\'1Cnew blow\u8221\'20\'1D against the \u8220\'20\'1Csacred right to vote\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe consequences will be felt across the country: fewer voices heard, fewer communities represented and a democracy diminished,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said.\par \par \uc2 She urged Congress to pass the John Lewis Voting Rights act, which would modernize the Voting Rights Act of 1965 but has been repeatedly blocked by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCongress must urgently pass the John R Lewis Voting Rights Advancement Act to restore the full strength of the Voting Rights Act before this latest blow becomes fatal,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 The Senate majority leader, Chuck Schumer, said the \u8220\'20\'1CSupreme Court just turned its back on one of the most sacred promises in American democracy\u8212\'20\'14the promise that every voice counts\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 5.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Democratic senator Chris Coons said the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruling in Louisiana v Callais}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Chas told the nation that some voices and votes are worth less than others\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He wrote in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/ChrisCoons/status/2049503396260925544"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thread}}}\uc2  on X:\par \par \uc2 5.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Hegseth attacks Democrat for calling war on Iran 'a quagmire'\par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth further defended the Iran war in fiery remarks to Congress, insisting that the unpopular conflict is not a quagmire.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou call it a quagmire, handing propaganda to our enemies? Shame on you for that statement,\u8221\'20\'1D Hegseth told Democrat John Garamendi, claiming that that language \u8220\'20\'1Cundermines the mission\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Garamendi had said Trump\u8217\'20\'19s war strategy was one of \u8220\'20\'1Castounding incompetence\u8221\'20\'1D, describing it as a \u8220\'20\'1Cserious self-inflicted wound to America\u8221\'20\'1D and highlighting the thousands of civilians and 13 US service members killed.\par \par \uc2 The blocking of the strait of Hormuz was \u8220\'20\'1Cforeseeable\u8221\'20\'1D, he added, telling the defense secretary:\par \par \uc2 He noted that the Iranian regime is intact, along with Iran\u8217\'20\'19s missile and drone systems, and that the war has strengthened Iran\u8217\'20\'19s coordination with China, Russia and North Korea. Trump is \u8220\'20\'1Cstuck in a quagmire\u8221\'20\'1D of another war in the Middle East, Garamendi said.\par \par \uc2 Hegseth lambasted Garamendi\u8217\'20\'19s statement as \u8220\'20\'1Creckless\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CYour hatred for President Trump blinds you,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 5.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back at Pete Hegseth\u8217\'20\'19s hearing at the House armed services committee earlier, the defense secretary got into a heated exchange with ranking member AdamSmith when he was asked how the government plans to end the nuclear threat from Iran.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is worth noting that every president prior to this one, including President Trump in his first term, also prevented Iran from getting a nuclear weapon without actually having to go to war in Iran,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said.\par \par \uc2 He then asked Hegseth why the United States attacked Iran in February if the operation last summer had successfully \u8220\'20\'1Cobliterated\u8221\'20\'1D Iran\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear program, as Donald Trump claimed, and questioned if there was an imminent threat that made the war necessary.\par \par \uc2 When Hegseth repeated that claim that Iran\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear facilities were \u8220\'20\'1Cobliterated\u8221\'20\'1D underground, Smith chimed in: \u8220\'20\'1CYou just said 60 days ago \u8230\'20\'26 the nuclear weapon was an imminent threat. Now you\u8217\'20\'19re saying that it was completely obliterated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hegseth said Iran\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear ambitions had not been eliminated but argued that its nuclear program had been.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 4.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 US senator for Georgia Reverend Raphael Warnock said the decision \u8220\'20\'1Cfurther ravaged\u8221\'20\'1D the Voting Rights Act and left the country \u8220\'20\'1Cat a crossroads where politicians are picking their voters\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s Supreme Court decision marks a profound defeat for American democracy and will pave the way for partisan politicians to pick their voters,\u8221\'20\'1D the Democrat said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CClearly, we are straying further from the core voting principles that helped create the diverse body that people see representing them today. We must restore the Voting Rights Act and ban gerrymandering. Our democracy is on the line.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 4.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 US representative Troy Carter, whose predominately black congressional district encompasses New Orleans, said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://troycarter.house.gov/media/press-releases/congressman-carter-statement-louisiana-v-callais-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Carter said the consequences of the high court\u8217\'20\'19s decision will be \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediate and severe\u8221\'20\'1D and that Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s two majority-black congressional districts are now at risk of being dismantled.\par \par \uc2 4.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 The mayor of New Orleans, Helena Moreno, a Democrat who represents the largest city in Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s other predominantly black congressional district, said the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling was \u8220\'20\'1Ca step backward\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 4.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lauren Groh-Wargo, executive director of Fair Fight Action, a Georgia-based voting rights group founded by Democrat and former US representative Stacey Abrams, said the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision \u8220\'20\'1Cguts\u8221\'20\'1D voting rights protection while \u8220\'20\'1Cpretending to uphold it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She said the court rewrote the law to require a showing of intentional discrimination. That\u8217\'20\'19s after Congress in the early 1980s specifically rewrote the Voting Rights Act to overturn an earlier supreme court decision in an Alabama case that tried to do the same thing.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Chief Justice John Roberts was a justice department attorney advocating for a showing of intentional discrimination.\par \par \uc2 She said:\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the ruling could open the door for Republican-led states to eliminate Black and Latino electoral districts that tend to favor Democrats and affect the balance of power in Congress. Donald Trump has already sparked a nationwide redistricting battle to boost the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s chances.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 4.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 'A devastating blow': NAACP says supreme court ruling is 'a major setback for our nation'\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Derrick Johnson, president of the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People(NAACP), the nation\u8217\'20\'19s oldest civil rights group, said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the high court\u8217\'20\'19s decision}}}\uc2  in Louisiana v Callais delivers \u8220\'20\'1Ca devastating blow to what remains of the Voting Rights Act\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The ruling is \u8220\'20\'1Ca license for corrupt politicians who want to rig the system by silencing entire communities\u8221\'20\'1D, Johnson said in a statement today.\par \par \uc2 He went on:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 4.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 White House hails supreme court ruling that undermines key provision of Voting Rights Act\par \par \uc2 The White House has hailed the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to effectively demolish a key provision of the Voting Rights Act which prevented racial discrimination in a landmark decision that Louisiana{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will have to redraw its congressional map}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a complete and total victory for American voters,\u8221\'20\'1D White House spokesperson Abigail Jackson said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe color of one\u8217\'20\'19s skin should not dictate which congressional district you belong in. We commend the court for putting an end to the unconstitutional abuse of the Voting Rights Act and protecting civil rights,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.54am BST\par \par \uc2 4.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 US war on Iran has cost $25bn so far, Pentagon official says\par \par \uc2 Answering questions from ranking Democrat AdamSmith, defense department official Jules Hurst told the committee that the federal government has spent about $25 billion on the war so far, providing the first official estimate of the war\u8217\'20\'19s cost.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost of that is in munitions, there\u8217\'20\'19s part of that that\u8217\'20\'19s obviously O&M [operations and maintenance] and equipment replacement\u8221\'20\'1D as well, said Hurst, who is performing the duties of the comptroller.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m glad you answered that question because we\u8217\'20\'19ve been asking for a hell of a long time and no one\u8217\'20\'19s given us the answer,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith replied.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 4.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 4.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back to the House armed services committee hearing, Pete Hegseth said the president\u8217\'20\'19s request for $1.5tn for the Pentagon \u8220\'20\'1Creflects the urgency of the moment\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 This is an \u8220\'20\'1Chistoric\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cwarfighting budget\u8221\'20\'1D, he said, and his department needed to get back on a \u8220\'20\'1Cwartime footing\u8221\'20\'1D after what he called years of underinvestment under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 In his opening remarks, General Dan Caine called the budget request \u8220\'20\'1Ca historic downpayment for future security\u8221\'20\'1D as he argued that \u8220\'20\'1Cglobal risk is scaling\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 3.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court rules Louisiana must redraw its congressional map in landmark case\par \par \uc2 The US supreme court has ruled that Louisiana will have to redraw its congressional map, in a landmark decision that effectively guts a major section of the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 In a 6-3 decision along partisan lines, the court rendered ineffective section 2 of the Voting Rights Act, the last remaining powerful provision of the 1965 civil rights law that prevents racial discrimination in voting. Section 2 specifically has long been used to ensure minority voters are treated fairly in redistricting.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAllowing race to play any part in government decision-making represents a departure from the constitutional rule that applies in almost every other context,\u8221\'20\'1D Justice Samuel Alito, a conservative, wrote for the majority opinion. \u8220\'20\'1CCompliance with section 2 thus could not justify the state\u8217\'20\'19s use of race-based redistricting here. The state\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to satisfy the Middle District\u8217\'20\'19s ruling, although understandable, was an unconstitutional racial gerrymander.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a dissenting opinion, Justice Elena Kagan wrote the court had now accomplished a \u8220\'20\'1Cdemolition of the Voting Rights Act\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of the case, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/mar/24/supreme-court-louisiana-redistricting-voting-rights-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana v Callais}}}\uc2 , was a thorny question of how much lawmakers are allowed to consider race when they redraw districts to ensure that Black voters are adequately represented. The supreme court initially heard oral arguments in the case last March, but took the unusual step of asking lawyers to re-argue the case last fall. In setting the case for a re-argument, the justices raised the stakes of the case, asking lawyers to focus on whether section 2 of the Voting Rights Act was constitutional.\par \par \uc2 The decision comes after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thearp.org/litigation/callais-v-landry/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 years of legal wrangling}}}\uc2  over the boundaries of the map.Here\u8217\'20\'19s Sam\u8217\'20\'19s full report:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court rules Louisiana must redraw its congressional map in landmark case}}}\par \par \uc2 3.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Supreme court hearing on whether protected status of Haitians and Syrians can be revoked begins\par \par \uc2 US supreme court oral arguments have begun in a case that will ultimately decide whether the Trump administration can strip temporary protected status (TPS) from hundreds of thousands of Haitians and Syrians living in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The hearing was supposed to begin at 10am ET, but it started late as the court released decisions, including a major ruling that Louisiana will have to{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/supreme-court-louisiana-congressional-map-case-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  redraw its congressional map}}}\uc2  (more on that to follow).\par \par \uc2 As my colleague Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Olivares wrote {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/us-supreme-court-haitians-syrians-tps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in this preview}}}\uc2 , people with TPS are given the permission to live and work in the US because the government has deemed their home countries to be unsafe due to war, political instability or natural disasters.\par \par \uc2 In the past year, the Trump administration has attempted to cut the program for various countries, opening the door to the removal of hundreds of thousands of protected immigrants in the US.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the supreme court allowed the administration to strip TPS status for more than 300,000 Venezuelans under the court\u8217\'20\'19s emergency docket. Now, the court will hear arguments challenging the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to remove the same protections from Syrians and Haitians.\par \par \uc2 If the supreme court sides with the Trump administration in its effort to cut the program for Syrians and Haitians, analysts say the administration would likely seek to end the TPS program for all countries. Nearly 1.3 million people were TPS holders at the start of the second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/us-supreme-court-haitians-syrians-tps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court to hear whether protected status of Haitians and Syrians can be revoked}}}\par \par \uc2 3.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth defends war on Iran as he appears before House armed services committee\par \par \uc2 In his opening statement, Pete Hegseth defended the war on Iran, saying of the US-Israeli war:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe biggest challenge, the biggest adversary we face at this point are the reckless, feckless and defeatist words of congressional Democrats and some Republicans,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 3.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 'Where is this going?': top Democrat on committee slams administration's strategy on Iran\par \par \uc2 In his opening remarks, ranking member Adam Smith praised the US military before expressing concern about the Pentagon\u8217\'20\'19s budget request given the state of the national debt.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat then went on to call Pete Hegseth\u8217\'20\'19s past comments about how the US military strategy is based on realism \u8220\'20\'1Cabsurd\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is the exact opposite of realism,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Running through some of those costs, Smith underlined the human cost in thousands of civilian deaths, the deaths of 13 US service members, other countries being \u8220\'20\'1Cdragged into the war\u8221\'20\'1D, gas prices at home, and the cost of fertilizer.\par \par \uc2 He then moved to a big question for Hegesth today, which is what is the US\u8217\'20\'19s endgame in Iran, given that fundamentally nothing has changed in Iran despite the war?\par \par \uc2 Smith also criticized the administration\u8217\'20\'19s harsh treatment of Nato for not joining the war, highlighting that Nato supported the US by invoking article 5 after 9/11 and adding:\par \par \uc2 In his final point, Smith stressed the need for answers regarding the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/11/iran-war-missile-strike-elementary-school"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strike on the Iranian girls\u8217\'20\'19 school}}}\uc2  that killed at least 175 people, most of them children:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 2.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Hegseth to face questions from lawmakers for first time since US-Israeli war on Iran began\par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth is finally due to appear before the House armed services committee at 10am ET, giving lawmakers their first opportunity to publicly question the defense secretary since the US and Israel launched war on Iran over two months ago.\par \par \uc2 Since the war began, Hegseth and General Dan Caine, chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, have only given press conferences and haven\u8217\'20\'19t testified before Congress. Caine is also set to testify today. They are both also due to appear before the Senate armed services committee tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s appearance is formally billed as a routine hearing on the Pentagon\u8217\'20\'19s budget request (the administration is requesting a record $1.5tn in defense spending, and the request was put together before the war), and comes amid intensifying questions over the rate at which the department is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/national-security/2026/04/iran-war-vance-hegseth-trump/686905/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 depleting weapon stockpiles}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hegseth is also likely to face scrutiny over civilian casualties in the war on Iran \u8211\'20\'13 not least the horrendous {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/11/iran-war-missile-strike-elementary-school"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strike on an Iranian girls\u8217\'20\'19 school}}}\uc2  that killed at least 175 people, most of them children. We can also expect questions on the US\u8217\'20\'19s strategic rationale for the war, the effectiveness of the US-Israeli bombing campaign, the US\u8217\'20\'19s preparedness for retaliatory strikes from Tehran, the strait of Hormuz crisis, his abrupt firings of senior defense officials, and more.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPete Hegseth\u8217\'20\'19s got a lot to answer to from this disastrous war,\u8221\'20\'1D Jason Crow, a Democratic representative on the House committee, told Politico. \u8220\'20\'1CHow much is this costing? What\u8217\'20\'19s the end game?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania Democrat Chris Deluzio told the outlet that Hegseth has been \u8220\'20\'1Cdodging congressional questions about the Iran war since day one\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll be watching the hearing and will you bring you all the key lines here.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 James Comey expected to self-surrender to law enforcement, CNN reports\par \par \uc2 Former FBI director James Comey is expected to self-surrender to law enforcement at federal court in the eastern district of Virginia today, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2026/04/28/politics/justice-department-indicts-ex-fbi-director-james-comey-again"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN (paywall) is reporting}}}\uc2 , citing a federal official familiar.\par \par \uc2 It comes after the US justice department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/28/james-comey-fbi-second-indictment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed new criminal charges against Comey}}}\uc2  yesterday, in what appears to be the latest instance of the DOJ wielding its power to target DonaldTrump\u8217\'20\'19s political adversaries.\par \par \uc2 A reminder that Comey was this time charged over a picture he posted on Instagram last year while on vacation, showing seashells arranged to say \u8220\'20\'1C86 47\u8221\'20\'1D. The post was read as a threat to Trump, the 47th president, as the number 86 can be used as shorthand for getting rid of something. Comey had captioned the image: \u8220\'20\'1CCool shell formation on my beach walk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Comey then deleted the post and apologized, saying he had not realized the numbers were associated with violence. \u8220\'20\'1CIt never occurred to me, but I oppose violence of any kind so I took the post down,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote on Instagram.\par \par \uc2 The charges against Comey, approved by a grand jury in the eastern district of North Carolina where he allegedly took the photo of the shells, include making a threat against the president and transmitting a threat, via social media, across state lines, according to court documents.\par \par \uc2 According to the indictment, the seashell numbers are something a reasonable person \u8220\'20\'1Cwould interpret as a serious expression of an intent to do harm to the President of the United States\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Comey published a video of himself yesterday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamescomey.substack.com/p/seashells?r=16z5v&triedRedirect=true"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 saying}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 The justice department previously indicted Comey, who has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/26/donald-trump-james-comey-timeline-feud"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long been the subject}}}\uc2  of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s wrath, last year and charged him with lying to Congress. That case was thrown out when a judge in the eastern district of Virginia ruled that the prosecutor overseeing the case {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/nov/24/james-comey-letitia-james-charges-case-dismissed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had been wrongfully appointed}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Updated at 1.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 1.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump tells aides to prepare for extended blockade of Iran, WSJ reports\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump has told aides to prepare for an extended blockade of Iranian ports, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/world/middle-east/trump-tells-aides-to-prepare-for-extended-blockade-of-iran-da3be7a4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Wall Street Journal (paywall) reported}}}\uc2  last night, citing US officials.\par \par \uc2 Trump has reportedly said in recent meetings that he prefers the blockade over other methods to increase the pressure on Iran as peace talks stall and Tehran keeps the critical strait of Hormuz closed.\par \par \uc2 According to the WSJ\u8217\'20\'19s report, Trump believes that his other options, such as resuming bombing or walking away from the war, would carry greater risks than maintaining the blockade.\par \par \uc2 The US military has been preventing ships from entering and departing Iranian ports since 13 April, after direct talks with Iran failed to yield results.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 12.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Fed likely to leave rates unchanged at what may be Powell's last meeting\par \par \uc2 The US Federal Reserve is widely expected to hold interest rates steady on Wednesday after a key policy meeting, likely the last chaired by central bank chief Jerome Powell, a frequent target of president Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s ire.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers will weigh the risks of surging energy prices and snarled supply chains due to the US-Israel war on Iran, with analysts widely expecting a third pause in a row as the effects of the conflict ripple through the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy.\par \par \uc2 All eyes will be on Powell\u8217\'20\'19s future plans at what could be his final press conference as head of the Fed on Wednesday afternoon, AFP reported.\par \par \uc2 While the central bank chief\u8217\'20\'19s tenure as chair ends 15 May, his term as a member of the board of governors continues until January 2028.\par \par \uc2 Since returning to power last year, Trump has frequently criticized and insulted Powell for not cutting interest rates \u8211\'20\'13 a policy that would turbocharge economic activity but could fuel inflation.\par \par \uc2 12.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 An attempt by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  to gain access to Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s detailed voter records was thwarted by the courts on Tuesday, when a federal judge dismissed the US justice department\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit against the state.\par \par \uc2 The ruling marks the latest legal setback {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/15/justice-department-voter-information"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in an unprecedented nationwide effort by the administration}}}\uc2  before the midterm elections to collect sensitive information about tens of millions of Americans. The DoJ has sued at least 30 states and the District of Columbia seeking to force release of the data, which includes dates of birth, addresses, driver\u8217\'20\'19s license numbers and partial social security numbers.\par \par \uc2 The US district judge Susan Brnovich, a Trump appointee, ruled that Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s statewide voter registration list was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot a document subject to request by the Attorney General\u8221\'20\'1D under federal law. The judge dismissed the lawsuit with prejudice because, she wrote, \u8220\'20\'1Camendment would be legally futile\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At least 13 states have either {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/tracker-justice-department-requests-voter-information"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 handed the information over voluntarily}}}\uc2  or promised to provide their detailed voter registration lists to the department, according to the Brennan Center for Justice and Associated Press reporting: Alaska, Arkansas, Indiana, Louisiana, Mississippi, Nebraska, Ohio, Oklahoma, South Carolina, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas and Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 In addition to Arizona, judges have ruled against the administration in Rhode Island, California, Massachusetts, Michigan and Oregon. In Georgia, a judge dismissed a DoJ lawsuit because it had been filed in the wrong city, prompting the government to refile elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/28/arizona-voter-rolls-trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Judge thwarts Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to access Arizona voter rolls }}}\par \par \uc2 12.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 US lawmakers to grill Pentagon chief on Iran war\par \par \uc2 US defense secretary Pete Hegseth will face tough questions from lawmakers about the Iran war on Wednesday during his first testimony to Congress since the start of the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Hegseth\u8217\'20\'19s appearance before the House Armed Services Committee \u8211\'20\'13 for a hearing on president Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s $1.5tn defense budget request \u8211\'20\'13 comes with the war still unresolved and the economic fallout from it continuing around the globe.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers from both parties have previously expressed dissatisfaction with the information provided in classified briefings on the war, setting up a potentially fiery public hearing in which top US military officer general Dan Caine is also set to testify, AFP reported.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFinally, Secretary Hegseth will come before the House Armed Services Committee this week. It is time to answer for this war of choice,\u8221\'20\'1D Representative Maggie Goodlander, a Democratic member of the committee, said in a post on X.\par \par \uc2 11.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian president Volodymyr Zelenskyy has thanked King Charles for his speech in Washington yesterday.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 11.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Iran 'better get smart soon' warns Trump in social media post\par \par \uc2 US president Donald Trump has warned Iran that \u8220\'20\'1Cthey better get smart soon\u8221\'20\'1D and sign a non-nuclear deal, in his latest social media post.\par \par \uc2 Trump shared an image of himself, wearing sunglasses and carrying a gun, set against the backdrop of \u8211\'20\'13 presumably \u8211\'20\'13 Middle Eastern towns and villages being bombed, with the caption \u8220\'20\'1CNo more Mr Nice Guy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He wrote:\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 11.31am BST\par \par \uc2 In his state dinner speech King Charles appeared to suggest to president Trump that the purpose of his state visit was to \u8220\'20\'1Cput the \u8216\'20\'18special\u8217\'20\'19 back into our relationship\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 just as Queen Elizabeth II did almost 70 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Charles spoke about the ties between Britain and the US, and implied it mirrored events in the aftermath of the 1956 Suez crisis, when Elizabeth toured the US to help repair relations.\par \par \uc2 Britain was left humiliated when the US refused to support its campaign with France to regain control of the Suez canal from Egypt, and the brief conflict marked the end of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s role as a global military power.\par \par \uc2 Charles told the dinner guests, who included the Amazon founder, Jeff Bezos, and the golfer Rory McIlroy: \u8220\'20\'1CAnd yes, we have had our moments of difficulty, even in more recent history. When my mother visited in 1957, not the least of her tasks was to help put the \u8216\'20\'18special\u8217\'20\'19 back into our relationship after a crisis in the Middle East.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some of the guests laughed when the king said: \u8220\'20\'1CNearly 70 years on, it is hard to imagine anything like that happening today\u8230\'20\'26\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.32am BST\par \par \uc2 11.22am BST\par \par \uc2 King Charles to visit New York to commemorate 9/11 victims\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s King Charles and his wife Queen Camilla arrive in New York on Wednesday to commemorate victims of the 11 September terror attacks on the city.\par \par \uc2 The king and queen\u8217\'20\'19s scheduled arrival in New York follows a packed day in Washington on Tuesday, when Charles delivered a speech to Congress, held private meetings with president Donald Trump amid tensions between the US and UK over the Iran war and sat down with leaders of the US tech industry, Reuters reported.\par \par \uc2 Charles and Camilla will begin their day in New York with a ceremony at the 9/11 memorial in lower Manhattan, where the twin towers of the World Trade Center were destroyed by al-Qaeda suicide bombers on 11 September, 2001. Charles is expected to meet with New York City\u8217\'20\'19s mayor, Zohran Mamdani, at the ceremony.\par \par \uc2 The king will then head to Harlem to visit a grassroots community organization that created a sustainable after-school urban farming initiative in an effort to combat food insecurity, according to local media.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Camilla will celebrate the 100th birthday of AA Milne\u8217\'20\'19s fictional character Winnie the Pooh on behalf of her charity, The Queen\u8217\'20\'19s Reading Room, which Buckingham Palace is calling a \u8220\'20\'1Cliterary engagement\u8221\'20\'1D event.\par \par \uc2 11.11am BST\par \par \uc2 US supreme court to hear whether protected status of Haitians and Syrians can be revoked\par \par \uc2 The supreme court will hear oral arguments on Wednesday over whether the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump administration}}}\uc2  can strip the temporary protected status (TPS) of hundreds of thousands of Syrians and Haitians, under a program that has protected them from deportation due to safety concerns in their home countries.\par \par \uc2 People with TPS are given the permission to live and work in the US because the government has deemed their home countries to be unsafe due to war, political instability or natural disasters. In the past year, the Trump administration has attempted to cut the program for various countries, opening the door to the removal of hundreds of thousands of protected immigrants in the US.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the supreme court allowed the administration to strip TPS status for more than 300,000 Venezuelans under the court\u8217\'20\'19s emergency docket. Now, the court will hear arguments challenging the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to remove the same protections from Syrians and Haitians.\par \par \uc2 If the supreme court sides with the Trump administration in its effort to cut the program for Syrians and Haitians, analysts say the administration would likely seek to end the TPS program for all countries. Nearly 1.3 million people were TPS holders at the start of the second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 The TPS program, established in 1990, does not offer a pathway to citizenship but allows citizens from designated countries to live and work in the US if they are unable to return safely to their home countries. TPS designations can be extended by the Department of Homeland Security (DHS).\par \par \uc2 Haitians have been protected from deportation under the TPS program since 2010 and Syrians have been protected since 2012. Earlier this month, the House passed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1689"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legislation}}}\uc2  to extend the protection for Haitian immigrants under the TPS program for three years.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/us-supreme-court-haitians-syrians-tps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court to hear whether protected status of Haitians and Syrians can be revoked}}}\par \par \uc2 10.57am BST\par \par \uc2 A flick of Oscar Wilde here, a nod to Henry Kissinger there, a sprinkling of Charles Dickens here, a dollop of Abraham Lincoln and Theodore Roosevelt there. Job done!\par \par \uc2 The British monarch mobilised an elite squad of dead white men, leavened with humour and subliminal politicking, on Tuesday in a charm offensive aimed over Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s head and squarely at the US Congress. Judging by the cheers and minute-long applause he received at the end, the soft power flex worked a treat and the special relationship lives to fight another day.\par \par \uc2 But the king\u8217\'20\'19s central message \u8211\'20\'13 of two great nations entwined in destiny \u8211\'20\'13 was also an inadvertent reminder of two empires that look increasingly shabby these days with rightwing populists on the march and the ghost of the sex offender Jeffrey Epstein hovering in the shadows.\par \par \uc2 Charles became the first British king to address the Congress almost exactly 250 years after the US denounced his fifth great-grandfather as a tyrant and declared its independence. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ll be back,\u8221\'20\'1D predicted George III in Hamilton and yet cricket, damp and a lack of air conditioning never clinched the deal.\par \par \uc2 What would America\u8217\'20\'19s founding fathers have made of seeing George III\u8217\'20\'19s direct descendant speak to their successors? Donald Trump mused at the White House on Tuesday: \u8220\'20\'1CThey might be absolutely shocked but probably only for a moment. Surely they would be delighted that the wounds of war healed into the most cherished friendship.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Well, Thomas Jefferson, Benjamin Franklin and co would surely be more shocked to discover that they now have their own mad king in the White House. If Charles spots signs saying \u8220\'20\'1CNo kings\u8221\'20\'1D on his travels, he shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t take it personally.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/28/king-charles-congress-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mission accomplished as king\u8217\'20\'19s speech to Congress goes down a storm }}}\par \par \uc2 10.51am BST\par \par \uc2 King Charles visits New York after Trump says UK monarch \u8216\'20\'18agrees with me\u8217\'20\'19 on Iran\par \par \uc2 Hello and welcome to the US politics live blog.\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s King Charles will use a trip to New York today to showcase cultural and economic ties between the UK and the US at a time when the so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial relationship\u8221\'20\'1D is under strain.\par \par \uc2 It is the third day of a four-day state visit, clouded by tensions over the Iran war, that began in Washington with president Donald Trump greeting the monarch and his wife, Queen Camilla, AFP reported.\par \par \uc2 The New York leg will first see the royals take part in a wreath-laying ceremony at the 9/11 memorial to mark 25 years since the terror attacks that killed nearly 3,000 people. The city\u8217\'20\'19s mayor Zohran Mamdani is not expected to meet Charles privately but will join him for the ceremony.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis atrocity was a defining moment for America and your pain and shock were felt around the whole world,\u8221\'20\'1D Charles told the US Congress on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe stood with you then. And we stand with you now in solemn remembrance of a day that shall never be forgotten,\u8221\'20\'1D he added in a speech that called for unity among western powers.\par \par \uc2 It comes as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/29/king-charles-agrees-with-me-on-iran-nuclear-weapon-ban-says-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump said Charles agrees with him}}}\uc2  that Iran should never be allowed nuclear weapons. The president\u8217\'20\'19s comments are likely to cause some embarrassment to royal aides that his views have been made public.\par \par \uc2 Trump said in his speech at the white-tie event on Tuesday evening:\par \par \uc2 A Buckingham Palace spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 In other developments:\par \par \uc2 * Todd Blanche, the former defense lawyer for Donald Trump now serving as acting US attorney general, announced two charges against James Comey, the former FBI director and deputy attorney general for allegedly \u8220\'20\'1Cknowing and willfully making a threat to kill\u8221\'20\'1D the president of the United States in a social media post.\par \par \uc2 * Patrick Fitzgerald, a former US attorney for the northern district of Illinois who now represents James Comey, said that his client, \u8220\'20\'1Cvigorously denies the charges\u8221\'20\'1D filed against him.\par \par \uc2 * US defense secretary PeteHegseth will face tough questions from lawmakers about the Iran war on Wednesday during his first testimony to Congress since the start of the conflict.\par \par \uc2 * President Trump will welcomes the Artemis II astronauts to the White House later today. The capsule returned to Florida\u8217\'20\'19s Kennedy Space Center on Tuesday, almost a month after blasting off on humanity\u8217\'20\'19s first lunar trip in more than a half century.\par \par \uc2 * The supremecourt will hear arguments Wednesday over the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s push to end legal protections for migrants fleeing war and natural disaster, one in a series of immigration cases the high court is considering against the backdrop of the president\u8217\'20\'19s far-reaching immigration crackdown.\par \par \uc2 * The US Federal Reserve is widely expected to hold interestrates steady on Wednesday after a key policy meeting, likely the last chaired by central bank chief Jerome Powell. Policymakers will weigh the risks of surging energy prices and snarled supply chains due to the US-Israel war on Iran, with analysts widely expecting a third pause in a row.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260429em4t002ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
