\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Facing AI and a tough job market, gen Z turns to entrepreneurship: \u8216\'20\'18I have to prove myself\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Arielle Pardes \par \uc2 2,406 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2026\par 12:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As AI erases the bottom rungs of the corporate ladder, some gen Z workers skip the entry level to become their own CEOs\par \par \uc2 When Ashley Terrell graduated from the \uc2 University of Hawaii\uc2  in 2024, she planned to find a job in marketing, maybe for a tech company. She had a bachelor\u8217\'20\'19s degree in business administration and a college r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 that included a student marketing job for \uc2 Red Bull\uc2 . But after months of applying, her only offer was to work in the power tools section at \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt was quite a shock,\u8221\'20\'1D she told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CI searched for jobs every single day in that \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  bathroom.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Terrell\u8217\'20\'19s generation is entering the workforce in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/12/college-graduates-job-market-ai"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 particularly unlucky moment}}}\uc2 . Hiring in the United States has slumped to its lowest rate since 2020, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/jolts.nr0.htm#:~:text=202)%20691-5902-,%E2%80%A2,it%20was%20also%203.1%20percent."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics}}}\uc2 . While workers of all ages are feeling the pressure of an uncertain economy, it\u8217\'20\'19s gen Z who is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/linkedin-news_whos-struggling-most-with-their-job-prospects-activity-7392604581517516800-ubpa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the most pessimistic}}}\uc2  about their job prospects: entry-level jobs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.weforum.org/stories/2026/03/how-ai-is-changing-the-nature-of-entry-level-work/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are the most vulnerable}}}\uc2  to impacts from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/artificialintelligenceai"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 artificial intelligence}}}\uc2 , and some younger workers are seeing their careers stall before they have even started. Terrell felt she was not just competing with other people for jobs. \u8220\'20\'1CEspecially with marketing, a lot of people think it can be replaced with AI,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate for Americans between 22 and 27 is now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market#--:overview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at its highest level }}}\uc2 since the pandemic. \u8220\'20\'1CThe job market is really sluggish right now,\u8221\'20\'1D said Daniel Zhao, the chief economist at \uc2 Glassdoor\uc2 , a workplace review company. \u8220\'20\'1CEntry-level workers are finding it difficult right now to get their foot on the ladder at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/21/healthcare-nurses-gig-work-ai-apps"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Uber for nurses\u8217\'20\'19: gig-work apps lobby to deregulate healthcare, report finds}}}\par \par \uc2 For many young would-be workers, that has translated into taking jobs they never imagined after earning a four-year degree: retail work, dog walking or other part-time jobs without benefits. Some have remained unemployed months or years after graduating.\par \par \uc2 Others are taking a different approach: when no jobs exist, they\u8217\'20\'19re creating their own.\par \par \uc2 Terrell, who started a \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel as a student, decided to build a marketing portfolio by making videos for brands. She started by direct messaging companies she liked and offering to make them content \u8211\'20\'13 sometimes for free. Eventually, \uc2 Jamba Juice\uc2  bought a video she had made to use as an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 TikTok\uc2  ad. Two years later, armed with a portfolio of videos like it, Terrell parlayed her experience into a part-time marketing role for a local distillery. Along the way, she built a roster of clients she continues to works with on branded content.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo one was offering me anything like what I wanted to do,\u8221\'20\'1D said Terrell. \u8220\'20\'1CSo I just tried to see what I could do on my own.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  interviewed more than a dozen young workers who feel, like Terrell, that the rules for finding a job changed just as they were entering the workforce. As the number of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.weforum.org/stories/2025/09/gen-z-are-competitive-job-market-randstad/#:~:text=New%20data%20reveals%20state%20of,and%20fewer%20entry%2Dlevel%20opportunities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 entry-level job postings}}}\uc2  have gone down in recent years, the expectations for early-career workers have gone up. For a generation that wants more purpose, more flexibility and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.economist.com/business/2022/07/21/what-gen-z-graduates-want-from-their-employers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more alignment with their work}}}\uc2 , the recent job market has felt disenchanting.\par \par \uc2 This is unfolding against the backdrop of the AI boom \u8211\'20\'13 something they see as a threat, a boon or maybe both. The same tech that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global/2025/jun/02/artificial-intelligence-jobs-techscape"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 threatens}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/ng-interactive/2026/feb/20/ai-future-work-technology-white-collar"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to cut into}}}\uc2  entry-level positions is also making it easier for some of these workers to start their own businesses, by compensating for skills they don\u8217\'20\'19t yet have, offering tools and platforms they can put to use, and allowing them to do more things at once.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of the sudden, you\u8217\'20\'19ve got to have some way to get up to the fourth rung of the career ladder,\u8221\'20\'1D said Joseph Fuller, a professor at \uc2 Harvard Business School\uc2  and the co-director of the Project on Workforce at Harvard. One way to ascend the rungs? Make your own ladder.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I was kind of forced into it\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Suhit Agarwal graduated from the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2  in 2025, hoping to use his degree in computational and applied mathematics to secure a job at \uc2 Google\uc2 . But after applying more than six times for both internships and jobs, he never even got an interview. Applications to other big tech companies were also dead ends, so Agarwal pivoted. At 24, his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 includes job titles such as \u8220\'20\'1Cfounding engineer\u8221\'20\'1D for companies he helped start. In those roles, he said he used AI tools including Claude Code to take on bigger responsibilities than he would have been able to on his own.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not the path he expected to be on \u8211\'20\'13 or the one his parents hoped for \u8211\'20\'13 but \u8220\'20\'1Ccharting my own course has been working so far\u8221\'20\'1D, he told the Guardian. One of the startups he helped found was acquired, netting him a small equity payout. Recently, that work experience helped him to land a job at a fin-tech startup.\par \par \uc2 Shola West, who is 25, has a similar story. She worked at media agencies in her early 20s and never planned to create her own business. But in 2024, shortly after starting a new job, her entire team was laid off. As she navigated an unpromising job market, she came to believe that working for herself was the most viable path. Now, she runs her own brand consultancy in London and works as a content creator on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/apr/14/ai-productivity-workplace-errors"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bosses say AI boosts productivity \u8211\'20\'13 workers say they\u8217\'20\'19re drowning in \u8216\'20\'18workslop\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was kind of forced into it, given how the market was,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. Since she started her consultancy, she\u8217\'20\'19s partnered with high-profile brands such as Paramount and Sony Music. \u8220\'20\'1CThe transition was definitely hard, but I had that motivation of, well, my career basically flopped,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow I have to prove to myself and everyone else that I can survive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The reasons for the poor job market are complex, involving an uncertain political climate, an unstable global economy, and emerging technological disruptions \u8211\'20\'13 specifically AI, and the anticipation that it will upend not only how employees do their work, but the need for some positions entirely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is particularly true for recent college graduates because many of the types of jobs that are entry-level involve substantial amounts of routine cognitive work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Harvard\u8217\'20\'19s Fuller. In a 2025 LinkedIn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/linkedin-economic-graph_redefining-entry-level-roles-innovation-activity-7328833531374108672-ZHUZ/?utm_source=share&utm_medium=member_desktop&rcm=ACoAABTEARUB3DqvBLONb1r6EcmmOVjinNqM3Wo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2 , 63% of executives reported that AI would replace at least some of the work of entry-level employees at their companies.\par \par \uc2 As a result, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe expectations of entry-level workers have completely changed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ethan Choi, a partner at the venture capital firm Khosla Ventures. As recently as two years ago, Choi worked with a fairly big team of associates, who were earlier in their careers and learning the ropes. Today? \u8220\'20\'1CI have zero associates.\u8221\'20\'1D Instead, he said partners and more tenured employees use AI to do the work that associates would have previously done.\par \par \uc2 A recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://digitaleconomy.stanford.edu/app/uploads/2025/12/CanariesintheCoalMine_Nov25.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from Stanford University\u8217\'20\'19s Digital Economy Lab found a \u8220\'20\'1Csubstantial decline\u8221\'20\'1D in employment for early-career workers in AI-exposed fields, such as customer service, data entry and coding. The impacts seem greatest on so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cknowledge workers\u8221\'20\'1D, who are likely to have university degrees, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 The upside? With the rise of \u8220\'20\'1Clow-code\u8221\'20\'1D AI tools \u8211\'20\'13 ones that allow people to build and deploy AI models without technical experience \u8211\'20\'13 Choi said anyone, at any level of seniority, can use AI to handle parts of their jobs. At the extreme end, it\u8217\'20\'19s becoming possible to build nearly an entire company by leveraging these AI tools, instead of hiring a whole team of engineers. Companies might be using these tools to cut down on workers, but workers could also use them to start their own projects, said Choi: \u8220\'20\'1CThe ones getting jobs will be the ones who are building stuff.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I definitely don\u8217\'20\'19t think I could\u8217\'20\'19ve done it without AI\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s what Madison Hsieh, a 25-year-old program manager at Amazon, is doing. Earlier this year, she used the coding platform Cursor to create a prototype of a social media app in her spare time. \u8220\'20\'1CI definitely don\u8217\'20\'19t think I could\u8217\'20\'19ve done it without AI,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, adding that it took her only about a month to get a prototype working. Without a platform like Cursor, building an app like this would\u8217\'20\'19ve required several months and several skilled engineers.\par \par \uc2 While she remains employed at Amazon, Hsieh said she likes the idea of starting her own company. \u8220\'20\'1CI want to have a more impactful role if I\u8217\'20\'19m going to do something for the rest of my life,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really hard to find that passion in your corporate 9 to 5.\u8221\'20\'1D She also likes the idea of jumping from the bottom of the career ladder to the top. At a big company like Amazon, \u8220\'20\'1Cthere are very limited roles that I saw for people who just came out of college to have an impact, without already having five to eight years of experience.\u8221\'20\'1D She plans to continue {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2026/feb/11/gen-z-side-hustles-job-market"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 working on the social media app in her spare time}}}\uc2  until it becomes a viable project to work on full-time.\par \par \uc2 Celeste Amadon, who is 22, turned down an investment banking internship at JP Morgan last summer to start a dating app company called Known. At first her parents were not supportive. \u8220\'20\'1CMy mom called me, like, three different times to try and stage an intervention,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. When she raised over $9m in venture capital last year, they came around. Now, she is the CEO of her own company \u8211\'20\'13 one that uses AI to help singles meet.\par \par \uc2 Amadon said that the experience of starting a company is \u8220\'20\'1Clike having done an MBA\u8221\'20\'1D. Before becoming the CEO of Known, her r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 included a series of internships. Now, she has had to learn how to hire, how to fire, how to manage a growing team, and how to allocate millions of dollars in funding.\par \par \uc2 Moving from \u8220\'20\'1Cintern\u8221\'20\'1D to \u8220\'20\'1CCEO\u8221\'20\'1D can have its growing pains, said Elijah Khasabo, the 22-year-old co-founder and CEO of Vidovo, a content platform. \u8220\'20\'1CThe last job I had was at TJ Maxx, folding clothes,\u8221\'20\'1D he told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat do I know about managing a marketing team, or a sales team? It\u8217\'20\'19s all learned by doing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Both Amadon and Khasabo emphasized the importance of surrounding themselves with mentors, hiring employees with more years of experience, and being open to learning from their employees. \u8220\'20\'1CI think a lot of young founders make the mistake of only hiring young people because they\u8217\'20\'19re intimidated by having to create a serious work environment for serious real adults,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAt the end of the day, I am a 22-year-old. I have 22-year-old friends. But I also now have 34-year-old friends. That has been the biggest growth opportunity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The old promise was stability. The new promise is ownership\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Starting a company is far from a golden ticket. Entrepreneurship comes with its own significant financial risks: most startups do not get funding and do not succeed. Founders who do succeed also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.weforum.org/stories/2020/08/diversity-gap-startups-gender-ethnicity/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tend to be}}}\uc2  white, male, well-educated and well-connected \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mckinsey.com/featured-insights/diversity-and-inclusion/underestimated-start-up-founders-the-untapped-opportunity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stacking the deck}}}\uc2  against those who come from underrepresented groups. And even successful startups require founders to live modestly for many years. Rather than working a 9 to 5, many founders are on the clock 24/7. Yet in an uncertain market, young people say it offers a surprising benefit: a sense of control.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor our parents or grandparents, the job felt like the prize, because if you had a good job, you could get a house, you could have a nice car, you could go on holiday. People weren\u8217\'20\'19t just randomly getting laid off or replaced by AI,\u8221\'20\'1D said West, the media consultant. Now, \u8220\'20\'1Cthere is no guaranteed outcome with any job.\u8221\'20\'1D Working for yourself at least allows you some control over your fate.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/apr/12/college-graduates-job-market-ai"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I feel helpless\u8217\'20\'19: college graduates can\u8217\'20\'19t find entry-level roles in shrinking market amid rise of AI}}}\par \par \uc2 Even those who aren\u8217\'20\'19t starting companies full-time may be turning to entrepreneurship in the form of side-hustles or a back-up plan in case they get laid off. A global {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fiverr.com/news/fiverr-next-gen-of-work-report-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from the freelancing services platform Fiverr found that 67% of gen Z workers wanted to have multiple income streams in order to feel financially secure in today\u8217\'20\'19s economy. About half of those respondents also believed that traditional employment would soon become \u8220\'20\'1Cobsolete\u8221\'20\'1D. The report also noted that gen Z sees AI-integration as \u8220\'20\'1Cimperative\u8221\'20\'1D, and most trusted AI to do parts of their work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CClimbing your own ladder can actually be safer, because it\u8217\'20\'19s yours,\u8221\'20\'1D said Francesca Albo, the 29-year-old co-founder and CEO of Puppy Sphere, a company that offers puppy yoga and dog therapy. Albo previously worked at a biotech company but left in part because she wanted to have more control over her work, and to spend her time doing something she was more passionate about.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI always thought the traditional path was safe. But that\u8217\'20\'19s a completely wrong mindset,\u8221\'20\'1D said Albo. \u8220\'20\'1CThe old promise was stability. The new promise is ownership.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That idea of stability still appeals, though: Terrell, the content marketer, said she is still looking for full-time work because she would like a steady paycheck and employer-sponsored health insurance.\par \par \uc2 As gen Z workers navigate their way through a changing economy, their choices could be a guide for how everyone else will soon have to adapt. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2025/05/19/opinion/linkedin-ai-entry-level-jobs.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an op-ed}}}\uc2  for the New York Times, Aneesh Raman, LinkedIn\u8217\'20\'19s chief economic opportunity officer, argued that resolving the entry-level work crisis is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe first step to fixing all work\u8221\'20\'1D. The young people struggling to find their place in the corporate world are indicating what\u8217\'20\'19s already starting to affect the rest of the workforce: \u8220\'20\'1CAll our jobs are going to come up against this same wave of change sooner or later.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fuller, the Harvard Business School professor, said: \u8220\'20\'1CPlenty of opportunities will open up. They just won\u8217\'20\'19t look like the ones your high school counselor may have suggested.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260425em4p001uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
