\par\par \uc2 Environment\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18They want to keep denying us our rights\u8217\'20\'19: workers in Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s $5.4bn dairy industry fight for basic labor protections\b0\par\par\uc2 Austyn Gaffney with visuals by Anna Watts \par \uc2 2,373 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2026\par 15:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Migrant labor fuels much of Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s dairy industry, but workers are exempt from minimum wage rules, overtime protections and the right to unionize\u8216\'20\'18I don\u8217\'20\'19t go out\u8217\'20\'19: Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s undocumented dairy workers live in fear after immigration raids\par \par \uc2 Hilario\u8217\'20\'19s work shift on a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/vermont"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vermont}}}\uc2  dairy farm began at 10.30pm when he lifted a red fleece blanket and rose from a makeshift bed next to the kitchen sink.\par \par \uc2 The 65-year-old pushed aside a lace curtain that covered his apartment door, dividing his room from the dairy\u8217\'20\'19s sour-smelling milking parlor. In the barn, a horseshoe-shaped milking platform hummed awake. Super-producer black-and-white Holstein cows, twice Hilario\u8217\'20\'19s size, peered out from vinyl curtains.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re smart and curious, and they\u8217\'20\'19re nervous,\u8221\'20\'1D Hilario said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou have to be gentle with them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He and his co-worker began a rhythmic routine: they clapped the bumpy rears of the cows, twirled towels and, in one fluid motion, attached the milking machine on to beach ball-sized udders. They finished at about 2.30am, hosing down the parlor before falling asleep next door. Hilario, who asked not to have his full name used due to safety concerns, began his next shift at 6.30am.\par \par \uc2 Hilario did this work, roughly 60 hours, seven days a week, for $650, he said, well below the state minimum wage. There were no days off, no clock to punch, no clear line between night and morning, no moment when the job truly ended. When a cow was injured or a piece of equipment broke, he worked more, without extra pay.\par \par \uc2 As Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s $5.4bn dairy industry has consolidated and farm family labor has disappeared, workers without permanent legal status have become indispensable to the dairy business, which comprises more than half of the state\u8217\'20\'19s agricultural economy. More than nine in 10 Vermont dairies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vermontdairy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Vermont-Dairy-Delivers.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surveyed in a 2025 state report}}}\uc2  employed a migrant workforce.\par \par \uc2 But the state has refused to codify rights for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.vermont.gov/Documents/2024/WorkGroups/AgLabor/Legal%20Documents/W~Damien%20Leonard~Statistical%20Overview%20of%20Vermont%20Farm%20Labor~9-20-2024.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 any of the state\u8217\'20\'19s 8,300 farm workers}}}\uc2 , including roughly 1,000 undocumented workers, according to numbers provided by Migrant Justice, a Vermont-based human rights organization founded and led by farm workers. These workers remain exempt from minimum wage rules, overtime protections and the right to unionize. And increasing immigration enforcement has made them more legally vulnerable and kept them cloistered on farms. Their vulnerability stands in stark contrast to Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s progressive identity and the values espoused by many of its leaders.\par \par \uc2 Under the second Trump administration, the outlook has darkened for dairy workers with the constant threat of detention and deportation. \uc2 US Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  runs surveillance operations in the state and has a growing presence, detaining community members, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vermontpublic.org/local-news/2026-03-11/ice-enforcement-action-multi-car-crash-standoff-south-burlington"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 including three without a warrant}}}\uc2 , in early March.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have workers who are now living arguably even more in the shadows than they have in the past,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Democratic state representative David Durfee, chair of the house committee on agriculture, food resiliency and forestry.\par \par \uc2 More than a year ago, Durfee led a taskforce \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.vermont.gov/committee/detail/2024/389"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the agricultural worker labor and employment laws study committee}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 that recommended {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.vermont.gov/Documents/2024/WorkGroups/AgLabor/Legal%20Documents/W~Sophie%20Zdatny~Agricultural%20Worker%20Labor%20and%20Employment%20Laws%20Study%20Committee%20-%20draft%20report~12-10-2024.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 establishing a minimum wage and overtime pay}}}\uc2  for farm workers, while rejecting calls for collective bargaining and unionization. At least two bills introduced to address the recommendations stalled in 2025, and lawmakers gave little attention to the issue during the 2026 legislative session.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn Vermont, we want to protect farms more than we care about workers,\u8221\'20\'1D said the state representative Kate Logan, a Progressive/Democrat from Burlington who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.vermont.gov/bill/status/2026/H.347"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sponsored one such bill}}}\uc2  last year. \u8220\'20\'1CWe care more about making sure a farm can be profitable or continue to exist than we care about whether or not the person working at the farm can afford their rent.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Only 13% of Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s dairy workforce receives minimum wage, according to a 2024 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://migrantjustice.net/sites/default/files/2024MDReport.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey of 212 Spanish-speaking dairy workers}}}\uc2  by Migrant Justice. Hilario\u8217\'20\'19s hourly wage was roughly $11 per hour, more than $3 less than the state minimum wage of $14.42 per hour.\par \par \uc2 Vanishing farms, changing workforce\par \par \uc2 Since the 1930s, the US has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/national-labor-relations-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 barred agricultural workers}}}\uc2  from basic labor rights such as the minimum wage and overtime pay. Vermont followed suit in the 1960s, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://vlrb.vermont.gov/sites/vlrb/files/documents/State%20Labor%20Relations%20Act.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reinforcing the federal policy}}}\uc2  with its own state law. The precedent of exclusion is so entrenched that in 2024, when Vermont {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.vermont.gov/bill/status/2024/S.102"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expanded unionization rights}}}\uc2 , farm workers\u8217\'20\'19 protections were stripped at the 11th hour.\par \par \uc2 Agricultural industries such as Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s no longer rely solely on the work of farm-owning families but depend on hired employees, the majority of whom are immigrants from southern Mexico, Will Lambek, a Migrant Justice staff member, told the agricultural taskforce in October 2024. Nationally, immigrants now make up more than half of dairy laborers, according to estimates from the \uc2 National Milk Producers\uc2  Federation.\par \par \uc2 Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s image as an agrarian utopia dotted by thousands of dairy farms has receded as the industry has consolidated. In the 1940s there were 11,000 dairies across the state. By 2024, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vermontdairy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Vermont-Dairy-Delivers.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only about 480}}}\uc2  remained.\par \par \uc2 Even as farms have vanished, dairy production has grown. Since 2013, the number of cows per farm has increased by nearly 70%, to an average of about 250, and the state\u8217\'20\'19s income from dairy has doubled over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 But farmers operate within a pricing system that offers little room to absorb labor costs, limiting their ability to pay more. The industry relies on an 89-year-old {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R45044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal milk pricing program}}}\uc2  that fluctuates with global supply and demand and, farmers say, has not kept pace with inflation. Milk\u8217\'20\'19s perishability further limits flexibility, requiring producers to sell at least every other day regardless of price and making dairy especially labor-intensive. Farmers milk at least two, and often three, times a day.\par \par \uc2 Those constraints have become more acute as milk prices have fallen. On Maple Grove Farm in Derby, Andy Birch milks 50 cows with the help of his wife and a high school student he pays $15 an hour. He lost his other employee, a second high school student, when her family moved out of state in January. He couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford to replace her. The check he receives every two weeks from his milk buyer declined by about $5,500 between 2024 and 2025.\par \par \uc2 Many farmers maintain that they compensate their workers fairly and treat them like family. The Vermont Dairy Producers Alliance says a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.vermont.gov/Documents/2024/WorkGroups/AgLabor/Written%20Testimony/W~Brian%20Carpenter~Vermont%20Dairy%20Producers%20Alliance%20Survey~11-15-2024.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2024 survey created for the farm labor study committee}}}\uc2  showed more than two-thirds of its members paid their workers the state minimum wage. Brian Carpenter, a former chair of the association, told the committee that if extraneous costs like heating, housing and transportation were factored in, the wage rose above $23 an hour.The survey did not include data on when, how or how many members were surveyed.\par \par \uc2 Mary White, president of the Vermont Farm Bureau and owner of Fort Waite Farm, said the quest for increased wages overlaps with a dwindling industry. Where White lives in Corinth in Orange county, there were once 52 dairy farms. Now, White said, her farm is one of only two still shipping milk.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like that in many, many towns, where there\u8217\'20\'19s only one or two farms left,\u8221\'20\'1D White said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe workforce has in a sense disappeared.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dairy jobs with dignity\par \par \uc2 Hilario spent his youth next to his father in coffee fields in Chiapas, Mexico, before taking a factory job in Mexico City and then coming to the US in 2006. He\u8217\'20\'19s worked in Vermont since 2013, paying to put his four daughters through college. Vermont is now his home. His children are grown, and he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t plan to return to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor most people when they come, they\u8217\'20\'19re coming and they\u8217\'20\'19re staying here for quite a while because we don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be putting our lives at risk coming and going, crossing back and forth between the US and Mexico,\u8221\'20\'1D a former dairy worker testified to a Vermont legislative committee in October. \u8220\'20\'1CThe people I know in the community, they\u8217\'20\'19re staying here for a long time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hilario\u8217\'20\'19s life outside work revolves around his church and volunteering for Migrant Justice, which formed 15 years ago after a 19-year-old farm worker was strangled to death by dairy machinery.\par \par \uc2 For years, much of his organizing work pushed businesses to join the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.milkwithdignity.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Milk With Dignity}}}\uc2  program that creates partnerships between private companies and participating farms to pay a premium on milk in exchange for better working conditions, improved housing and higher wages, along with a protected complaint system for workers. In 2017, Ben & Jerry\u8217\'20\'19s, a subsidiary of Unilever, committed to sourcing milk for its ice-cream from Milk With Dignity farms. Today, that accounts for about 54 farms, or only about an eighth of Vermont\u8217\'20\'19s dairies.\par \par \uc2 Most dairy farms in Vermont remain outside the program. Last November, Hilario joined a group of workers and supporters in front of the Hannaford supermarket in Middlebury and picked up a microphone.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m a dairy worker. I\u8217\'20\'19ve worked on the same farm for five years. I\u8217\'20\'19ve been paid below minimum wage, living in a house that isn\u8217\'20\'19t fit for human habitation,\u8221\'20\'1D Hilario said in Spanish through an interpreter. Hannaford representatives accompanied by the sheriff\u8217\'20\'19s department issued a trespass citation to at least one supporter, a former dairy worker.\par \par \uc2 The protest was part of a years-long campaign to force Hannaford to join Milk With Dignity. The supermarket chain, which is owned by the Dutch company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aholddelhaize.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ahold Delhaize}}}\uc2 , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://migrantjustice.net/node/594"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 resisted the workers\u8217\'20\'19 pleas}}}\uc2  for years.\par \par \uc2 In a response to allegations of worker abuse in its supply chain by Migrant Justice, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.business-humanrights.org/media/documents/Response_from_Ahold_Delhaize_and_Hannaford_to_Migrant_Justice.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ahold Delhaize wrote in 2024}}}\uc2  that it recognized \u8220\'20\'1Cmigrant workers are vulnerable throughout supply chains worldwide, particularly in agriculture\u8221\'20\'1D, and the company took reports of abuse \u8220\'20\'1Cvery seriously\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is why Hannaford has been, and continues to be, engaged in a thorough due diligence review across its dairy supply chain,\u8221\'20\'1D the company wrote in response to the Business and Human Rights Resource Centre, a global organization investigating human rights.\par \par \uc2 Hannaford has said it works with other \u8220\'20\'1Cindustry and supply chain partners\u8221\'20\'1D that advance human rights. \u8220\'20\'1CThe concerns and issues facing agricultural workers are systemic, complex and extend far beyond Hannaford\u8217\'20\'19s supply chain and the state of Vermont,\u8221\'20\'1D the company said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hannaford.com/pages/migrant-justices-milk-with-dignity-campaign"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2023 statement}}}\uc2 . Hannaford and parent company Ahold Delhaize did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf working conditions and pay protections are important to the state, we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily be leaving it to private individuals or the market to make that happen,\u8221\'20\'1D Durfee, the state representative, said of the program. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s passing along the buck to somebody else.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Last April, Migrant Justice filed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://migrantjustice.net/sites/default/files/2025.Ahold_.NCP_.Public.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an international human rights complaint}}}\uc2  against Ahold Delhaize, alleging worker abuse in its supply chain. In response, the company launched an investigation into dairy suppliers in the north-eastern US, including the farm where Hilario works. In October, Hilario and Nicolas, another dairy worker, laid out their demands on a Zoom call to third-party investigators: their own bedrooms, the state minimum wage and one day off a week.\par \par \uc2 Migrant Justice launched a new campaign this April in the US and the Netherlands, asking Ahold Delhaize to publish its findings after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.aholddelhaize.com/media/toibtcys/ahold-delhaize-annual-report-2025-interactive.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the company\u8217\'20\'19s 2025 annual report}}}\uc2  stated the results of its investigation \u8220\'20\'1Cconfirmed the importance of confidentiality and trust in conducting a meaningful dialogue\u8221\'20\'1D. Migrant Justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://migrantjustice.net/node/594"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 feared this meant the report}}}\uc2  would be buried.\par \par \uc2 The workers are still waiting to hear back.\par \par \uc2 Remaking farm workers\u8217\'20\'19 future\par \par \uc2 At 65, Hilario could be at the verge of retirement and eligible for receiving social security benefits from the taxes he pays every year. Instead, for years, his one form of relief came on Sundays, when he paid his co-worker $100, roughly a sixth of his weekly $650 salary, to cover his morning shift so he could leave the farm for church.\par \par \uc2 Last September, Benjamin, one of two church leaders, greeted Hilario at the door in a shiny pink tux. Benjamin moved to Vermont and grew his 30-member congregation, composed mostly of farm workers, by traveling between dairies with his King James Bible in hand. Today, many of those members are afraid to travel to the church.\par \par \uc2 Hilario still attends all three services on Sunday, from 10am to 5pm. That September morning, Benjamin told parishioners about the work of Noah to save the world from the flood. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to serve in something that\u8217\'20\'19s never been seen,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Hilario\u8217\'20\'19s bosses at his old farm discouraged contact with Migrant Justice, but he continued fighting for the future he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t yet seen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMaybe it has to do with racism,\u8221\'20\'1D Hilario said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey want to keep denying us our rights, but at this point they have to listen more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hilario left his old farm in February, after working every day since he arrived five years earlier. Nicolas remains on his farm, where he arrived three years earlier. In December, when he had flu-like symptoms for two weeks, he had to work without sick days. When two co-workers suddenly left the dairy, Nicolas worked three shifts a day for two days before his farm found replacements. One was a friend from his childhood in Puebla. They grew up 20 minutes down the road; they now share a bedroom.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNothing\u8217\'20\'19s difficult about the work,\u8221\'20\'1D Nicolas said while milking, a wry smile under his thin moustache. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just the same thing every day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Through the door of the milk barn, the scene looked pleasant and pastoral: soft lines of fog settling over farm fields, ramshackle barns along winding roads, black-spotted milk cows peering between fence rails, all within the humpback hills that give Vermont its Green Mountain state nickname.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose on the outside think everything\u8217\'20\'19s fine,\u8221\'20\'1D Nicolas said.This story was co-published and supported by the journalism non-profit the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://705e1645.streak-link.com/CuhJbVpNTYtVJdk_hA4StLc9/https%3A%2F%2Feconomichardship.org%2F"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Economic Hardship Reporting Project}}}\uc2 . Anna Watts interpreted and contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260415em4f00361\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
