\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I feel helpless\u8217\'20\'19: college graduates can\u8217\'20\'19t find entry-level roles in shrinking market amid rise of AI\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2026\par 15:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Young American graduates expressed frustration over fewer job openings and longer searchesSign up for the Breaking News US email to get newsletter alerts in your inbox\par \par \uc2 American {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/education/colleges"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 college}}}\uc2  graduates are facing the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market#--:overview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worst entry-level job market}}}\uc2  since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/coronavirus-outbreak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pandemic}}}\uc2 , with the underemployment rate reaching 42.5% \u8211\'20\'13 its highest level since 2020.\par \par \uc2 Several young graduates told the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  about their struggles navigating a job market shaped by tightening opportunities, the rise of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/artificialintelligenceai"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AI}}}\uc2  and shifting employer expectations.\par \par \uc2 Gillian Frost, a 22-year-old student at \uc2 Smith College\uc2  in Massachusetts, has been searching for work since last September. Majoring in quantitative economics with a minor in government and set to graduate in May, she described a grueling and often discouraging process.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery weekend, I dedicate over two hours to job applications. As of today, I\u8217\'20\'19ve applied to over 90 jobs. I\u8217\'20\'19ve been ghosted by nearly 25% of them and rejected automatically from around 55%,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Despite securing some interviews, Frost said the lack of communication from employers had been particularly frustrating.\par \par \uc2 She said: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve gotten around 10 interviews but many of them don\u8217\'20\'19t even bother to tell you you\u8217\'20\'19re not a good fit \u8230\'20\'26 I feel helpless. No one seems to know how best to prepare due to the unique conflux of events occurring. How do you prepare for a tight labor market coinciding with the emergence of AI and direct US involvement in war? Most generations have dealt with maybe one of these but our generation is the first to deal with all three.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Jeff Kubat, a 31-year-old in St Cloud, Minnesota, the challenge is different but no less severe. After spending eight years optimizing accounts payable at a construction company, he returned to school to pursue a master\u8217\'20\'19s in accounting. He has since struggled to secure a role.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI should be about to graduate and it\u8217\'20\'19s been a struggle to job hunt \u8230\'20\'26 Even companies out in small-town Minnesota are being incredibly literal in who they\u8217\'20\'19re looking for and it\u8217\'20\'19s just a dearth of willingness to train people who have relatable backgrounds into what they need,\u8221\'20\'1D Kubat said.\par \par \uc2 As his job search continues, Kubat said he was beginning to lower his expectations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m about to drop my standards for salary after this next round of interviews gets hashed out since my next job isn\u8217\'20\'19t my forever job \u8230\'20\'26 but I still need to make money and it really does feel like I\u8217\'20\'19m in an area that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t match with my industry. It is a reflection of these stories that say that hiring has fallen to the point of the Covid years. It seems like the only roles that are opening are due to people falling out of roles rather than genuine growth in the area,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Others said the difficulty lay not just in finding jobs, but in meeting increasingly demanding requirements. A 25-year-old graduate from \uc2 New York University\uc2  who majored in media, culture and communications said many so-called entry-level roles felt out of reach.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDecent-paying jobs that are listed as entry-level will often ask for candidates with three to five years of experience \u8211\'20\'13 an amount of time that simply cannot be achieved if one is fresh out of college,\u8221\'20\'1D the graduate said. \u8220\'20\'1CMost job descriptions make me feel so un- or under-qualified, I won\u8217\'20\'19t even bother applying since I don\u8217\'20\'19t have years of experience to draw from.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The graduate also described the growing influence of automated hiring systems, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CFor every job, especially ones for larger entities who are likelier to use AI in the hiring process, it\u8217\'20\'19s essential to tailor my r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 explicitly for that position and include as many keywords as possible. It\u8217\'20\'19s aggravating and exhausting, but sadly a necessity in this fucked-up market and point in technological development.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They added: \u8220\'20\'1CI hate that I have to worry about passing a machine\u8217\'20\'19s arbitrary and unknowable tests before anyone considers my human capability and what I could bring to a given position as an individual.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Anna Waldron, a 22-year-old originally from Portland, Oregon, structural barriers in hiring practices have made the job search especially challenging.\par \par \uc2 Waldron, who is set to graduate from \uc2 Loyola University Chicago\uc2  in May with a double major in political science and journalism, said she usually applied \u8220\'20\'1Con job boards like Handshake, \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , FlexJobs, etc, but other times I look for places in Chicago that I know of and apply through their careers section on their website if they have one\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She continued: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat I\u8217\'20\'19ve found is that a lot of jobs don\u8217\'20\'19t get posted on these sites because they hire internally or keep it \u8216\'20\'18in the circle of the company\u8217\'20\'19, which makes it hard for entry people like me who don\u8217\'20\'19t have as many connections.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite multiple internships and relevant experience, Waldron said she had yet to secure a position: \u8220\'20\'1CI have done three internships since being in college and have skills both in writing for news publications and doing policy work, including working for the US Senate, but despite me applying to all kinds of jobs related to both of these fields, I am still struggling to find something.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260412em4c001s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
