\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Federal workers struggle to find roles a year after Trump cuts: \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19ve applied to over 250 jobs\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,057 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2026\par 06:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Current and former government employees say they\u8217\'20\'19re facing a tough job market over a year on from sweeping cuts\par \par \uc2 Maggie was faced with a tough choice in February 2025: quit her job at the US office of personnel management or be unceremoniously fired.\par \par \uc2 Though she was a few months pregnant at the time, Maggie was offered one of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opm.gov/policy-data-oversight/workforce-restructuring/voluntary-separation-incentive-payments/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 buyouts}}}\uc2  that were offered to tens of thousands of federal government employees by the office of personnel management.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be without health insurance through the delivering of my baby,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maggie, who requested to omit her last name for fear of professional repercussions. \u8220\'20\'1CI was going to have six to seven months of paid parental leave, because I\u8217\'20\'19d been on my job for five years and I accrued time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She took a buyout offer in May 2025 and, like many federal employees who took buyouts, and was placed on administrative leave until September 2025. She delivered her baby in September, just 10 days before she formally lost her job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThen I lost my insurance at the end of October,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI had 10 days after having my baby before I lost my job, technically, and then I lost my insurance at the end of October,\u8221\'20\'1D Maggie said. \u8220\'20\'1CMy job was to make the government more efficient and work better for the people it served and the people who do the serving. Cutting us is making the government less efficient.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Maggie has been applying to jobs since then, though she is still waiting for her agency to give her an ethics letter that would allow her to start work at another job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf they were organized and going back through this in a methodical way, they would have all of our paperwork and everything in order, but this was thoughtless and careless,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Since Trump took office last January, the federal workforce has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/empsit.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined}}}\uc2  by about 355,000 employees, with 18,000 workers leaving the federal workforce in March 2026. Furloughed workers during the partial government shutdown of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  were counted as employed in March job numbers. The Trump administration had attempted even more expansive firings and cuts but was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afge.org/article/court-reverses-firing-of-federal-workers-during-shutdown/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked}}}\uc2  by court decisions and, in some cases, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2025/06/06/doge-staff-cuts-rehiring-federal-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rehired}}}\uc2  some workers after it realized it had cut too many jobs.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  spoke to current and former federal government employees who said they were dealing with a difficult job market, flooded with other former government workers. The cuts have also left remaining government workers scrambling to keep important government functions afloat as they absorb the workloads of those who left.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We just got thrown away like garbage\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Charles Melton had spent 20 years at the \uc2 US Department of Agriculture\uc2  and planned to stay at least another 10 years before he took an early retirement offer in September.\par \par \uc2 Though he has found another job outside Washington DC, Melton still helps former colleagues with r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 and cover letter writing who are still struggling to find a new job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m still mad as hell,\u8221\'20\'1D said Melton. \u8220\'20\'1CWe never had a chance to make our cases. We just got thrown away like garbage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One worker had moved from Michigan to Washington DC for a new job at the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2 . Because the role was considered probationary, the job was soon cut once Trump took office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis was that kind of opportunity that [was] hard to get into, but I took the chance, moved and I\u8217\'20\'19ve been wholly unable to find something adequate in DC. The job market has just collapsed,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker, who requested anonymity, said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve applied to over 250 jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Operationally, federal agencies are struggling to keep up with the demand for public services due to the cuts.\par \par \uc2 Amid staffing losses, customer service at the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2  has worsened, with staff being {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.warren.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/letter_from_warren_senators_to_social_security_admin_on_new_staff_reassignments.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reassigned}}}\uc2  to assist on the agency\u8217\'20\'19s national phone line. Healthcare workers at the Department of Veterans Affairs have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://capitalandmain.com/despite-vow-to-protect-health-care-for-veterans-va-losing-doctors-and-nurses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported }}}\uc2 ongoing staffing issues due to cuts and hiring freezes that have reduced services. Federal labor enforcement and inspections have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://goodjobsfirst.org/worker-protections-in-freefall-the-collapse-of-federal-labor-enforcement-under-the-second-trump-administration/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropped}}}\uc2  significantly.\par \par \uc2 Other agencies have been effectively rendered inoperable. The shutdown of \uc2 USAID\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hsph.harvard.edu/news/usaid-shutdown-has-led-to-hundreds-of-thousands-of-deaths/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 resulted}}}\uc2  in hundreds of thousands of deaths around the world due to the spread of infectious diseases and malnutrition. At the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 , multiple consumer fraud cases and protections were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://advocacy.consumerreports.org/press_release/cfpb-on-life-support-one-year-after-it-was-targeted-for-shutdown/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropped}}}\uc2  amid efforts to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://advocacy.consumerreports.org/press_release/cfpb-on-life-support-one-year-after-it-was-targeted-for-shutdown/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shut down }}}\uc2 the agency.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was not any thought put into what they were doing,\u8221\'20\'1D Melton said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s hit home yet. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think the American public realizes how much has been lost yet.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A current \uc2 US Department of Education\uc2  worker who requested anonymity said the department was a skeleton of what it used to be.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just been really sad to see how our agency was decimated,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s our life\u8217\'20\'19s work. For a lot of people, this is 20 to 30 years of work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another current federal employee said many government workers had stuck around, despite the hostility toward federal workers, because they deeply care about public service.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are there because they deeply believe in the mission, and the mission is keeping the public safe, healthy, alive and well-treated. They deeply believe in that and so they\u8217\'20\'19ve decided to go down with the ship, even though that ship is not treating them very well,\u8221\'20\'1D said the worker, who also requested anonymity. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re sticking with it because they care, because they\u8217\'20\'19re good people, doing good work and in some cases, keeping extremely important government functions alive by the skin of their teeth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The White House declined to comment, deferring to office of personnel management (OPM) and office of management and budget, which did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Scott Kupor, OPM\u8217\'20\'19s director, said \u8220\'20\'1Creshaping the federal workforce is essential to building a government that works for the American people, not the bureaucracy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy realigning roles, streamlining operations, and modernizing how agencies manage talent, we are strengthening performance and accountability across government,\u8221\'20\'1D Kupor added. \u8220\'20\'1CThis effort ensures taxpayer dollars support a workforce that delivers efficient, responsive, and high-quality services.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260411em4b0020a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
