\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Porn, dog poo and social media snaps: the \u8216\'20\'18taskers\u8217\'20\'19 scraping the internet for AI firm part-owned by \uc2 Meta\b0\par\par\uc2 Aisha Down \par \uc2 1,511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2026\par 15:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  gig workers describe desperation of using people\u8217\'20\'19s personal profiles and copyrighted work to train AI\par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of people have been paid by a company part-owned by \uc2 Meta\uc2  to train AI by combing \uc2 Instagram\uc2  accounts, harvesting copyrighted work and transcribing pornographic soundtracks, the Guardian can reveal.\par \par \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  , 49%-controlled by \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s social media empire, has recruited experts across fields such as medicine, physics and economics \u8211\'20\'13 putatively to refine top-level artificial intelligence systems through a platform called Outlier. \u8220\'20\'1CBecome the expert that AI learns from,\u8221\'20\'1D it says on its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://outlier.ai/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 site}}}\uc2 , advertising flexible work for people with strong credentials.\par \par \uc2 However, workers for the platform said they have become involved in scraping an array of other people\u8217\'20\'19s personal data \u8211\'20\'13 in what they described as a morally uncomfortable exercise that diverged significantly from refining high-level systems.\par \par \uc2 Outlier is managed by \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  , which has contracts with the Pentagon and US defense companies.\par \par \uc2 Its former CEO, \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2  , who is \uc2 Meta\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s chief AI officer,was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/profile/alexandr-wang/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 described}}}\uc2  by \uc2 Forbes\uc2  as the \u8220\'20\'1Cworld\u8217\'20\'19s youngest self-made billionaire\u8221\'20\'1D. Its former managing director, \uc2 Michael Kratsios\uc2  , is the science adviser to the US president, Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 One Outlier contractor based in the US said users of \uc2 Meta\uc2  platforms, including \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , would be surprised at how data from their accounts was collected \u8211\'20\'13 including pictures of users and their friends.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think people understood quite that there\u8217\'20\'19d be somebody on a desk in a random state, looking at your [social media] profile, using it to generate AI data,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  spoke to 10 people who have worked for Outlier to train AI systems, some for more than a year. Many of them had other jobs \u8211\'20\'13 as journalists, graduate students, teachers and librarians. But in an economy struggling under the threat of AI, they wanted the extra work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of us were really desperate,\u8221\'20\'1D said one. \u8220\'20\'1CMany people really needed this job, myself included, and really tried to make the best of a bad situation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Like the growing class of AI gig workers worldwide, most believed they had been training their own replacements. One artist described \u8220\'20\'1Cinternalised shame and guilt\u8221\'20\'1D for \u8220\'20\'1Ccontributing directly to the automation of my hopes and dreams.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs an aspiring human, it makes me angry at the system,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Danas, a partner at Clarkson, a law firm representing AI gig workers in lawsuits against \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  and several similar platforms, estimates that hundreds of thousands of people worldwide now work for platforms such as Outlier. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  spoke to Outlier workers, also called \u8220\'20\'1Ctaskers\u8221\'20\'1D, in the UK, the US and Australia.\par \par \uc2 In interviews, taskers described the increasingly familiar humiliations of AI gig work: constant monitoring and piecemeal, unstable employment. \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sfchronicle.com/tech/article/scaleai-sued-alleged-labor-violations-19970083.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused}}}\uc2  of using \u8220\'20\'1Cbait-and-switch\u8221\'20\'1D tactics to lure in potential workers \u8211\'20\'13 promising workers a high salary during initial recruitment, and then offering them significantly less. \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  declined to comment on ongoing litigation, but a source said pay rates change after recruitment only if workers opt in to different, lower-paid projects.\par \par \uc2 Some taskers said they were asked to submit to repeated, unpaid AI interviews to qualify for certain assignments; several believed these interviews were recycled to train AI. All of them said they were constantly monitored through a platform called \u8220\'20\'1CHubstaff\u8221\'20\'1D, which could screenshot the websites they visited while working. The \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  source said that no interviews were required to work on Outlier projects, although \u8220\'20\'1Coptional onboarding\u8221\'20\'1D was offered to get contributors started; and that Hubstaff was used to ensure contributors were paid accurately but not to \u8220\'20\'1Cactively monitor\u8221\'20\'1D taskers.\par \par \uc2 Several taskers described being asked to transcribe pornographic soundtracks, or label photos of dead animals or dog faeces. One doctoral student said they had to label a diagram of baby genitalia. There were police calls that described violent scenarios.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe had already been told before that there would be no nudity in this mission. Appropriate behaviour, no gore, like no blood,\u8221\'20\'1D said the student. \u8220\'20\'1CBut then I would get an audio transcript thing for porn or there would be just random clips of people throwing up for some reason.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  has seen videos and screenshots of some of the tasks that Outlier required its workers to perform. These included photos of dog faeces, and tasks with prompts such as \u8220\'20\'1CWhat would you do if an inmate refused to follow orders in a correctional facility?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  , the source said, shuts down tasks if inappropriate content is flagged, and workers are not required to continue with tasks that make them feel uncomfortable. The source added that \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  did not take on projects involving child sexual abuse material or pornography.\par \par \uc2 There was an expectation of social media scraping, the Outlier workers suggested. Seven of the taskers described scouring other people\u8217\'20\'19s Instagram and Facebook accounts, tagging individuals by name, as well as their locations and their friends. Some of these involved training the AI on the accounts of people under the age of 18. The assignments were structured to require new data other taskers had not yet uploaded, pushing workers to plumb the social accounts of more people.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian has seen one such task, which required workers to select photos from individuals\u8217\'20\'19 Facebook accounts and sequentially order them by the age of the user in the photo.\par \par \uc2 Several taskers said they found these assignments unsettling; one tried to complete them using only photos of celebrities and public figures. \u8220\'20\'1CI was uncomfortable including pictures of kids and stuff, but like the training materials would have kids in it,\u8221\'20\'1D said one.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t use any friends or family to submit [tasks] to the AI,\u8221\'20\'1D said another. \u8220\'20\'1CI do understand that I don\u8217\'20\'19t like it ethically.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Scale source said taskers did not review social media accounts set to \u8220\'20\'1Cprivate\u8221\'20\'1D, and was not aware of tasks that involved labelling the ages of individuals, or their personal relationships. They added that Scale AI did not take on projects with explicit sensitive content related to children, but did use children\u8217\'20\'19s public social media data. Workers did not log on to personal Facebook or Instagram accounts to complete these tasks.\par \par \uc2 For another assignment, taskers described harvesting images of copyrighted artwork. As with the social media training, the task required constant new input \u8211\'20\'13 apparently to train an AI to produce its own artistic images. As workers ran out of other options, they plumbed social media accounts of artists and creators.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian has seen documentation of this assignment, which included AI-generated paintings of \u8220\'20\'1Ca Native American caregiver\u8221\'20\'1D, and the prompt, \u8220\'20\'1CDO NOT use AI-generated images. Only select hand-drawn, painted or illustrated artwork created by human artists.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Scale AI did not ask contributors to use copyrighted artwork to complete assignments, the source said, and it declined work that violated this standard.\par \par \uc2 Taskers also expressed uncertainty about what they might be training the AI to do \u8211\'20\'13 and how their submissions would be used.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt does seem like labelling diagrams is something an AI can already do so I\u8217\'20\'19m really curious as to why we need like, dead animals,\u8221\'20\'1D said one.\par \par \uc2 Scale AI has counted among its clients major technology companies such as Google, Meta and OpenAI, as well as the US department of defense and the government of Qatar. It fills a need that is becoming more pronounced as AI models grow larger: for new, labelled data that can be used to train them.\par \par \uc2 Taskers described interacting with ChatGPT and Claude, or using data from Meta to complete certain assignments; some thought they might be training Meta\u8217\'20\'19s new model, Avocado.\par \par \uc2 Meta and Anthropic did not respond to a request for comment. OpenAI said it stopped working with Scale AI in June 2025, and its \u8220\'20\'1Csupplier code of conduct sets out clear expectations for the ethical and fair treatment of all workers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Most taskers the Guardian spoke to are still accepting assignments on the Outlier platform. The pay is unsteady; they face occasional mass layoffs. But with the AI future fast arriving, they feel there may not be any other choice.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have to be positive about AI because the alternative is not great,\u8221\'20\'1D said one. \u8220\'20\'1CSo I think eventually things will get figured out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A Scale AI spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1COutlier provides flexible, project-based work with transparent pay. Contributors choose when and how they participate, and availability varies based on project needs. We regularly hear from highly skilled contributors who value the flexibility and opportunity to apply their expertise on the platform.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 7 and 8 April 2026. Alexandr Wang is the former, not current, CEO of Scale AI. The company is part-owned by Meta, not owned by it as an earlier headline said. And a response from Scale AI has been added regarding claims by taskers that they had to take on unpaid interviews.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260407em47002uv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
