\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US jobs market surpassed expectations in March but February losses were worse than first reported\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2026\par 12:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employers added 178,000 new jobs in March and unemployment rate fell to 4.3%, ahead of economists\u8217\'20\'19 predictionsSign up for the Breaking News US email to get newsletter alerts in your inbox\par \par \uc2 The US labor market picked up in March as employers showed signs of resilience amid the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/us-israel-war-on-iran"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US-Israel war in Iran}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/mar/06/february-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extraordinary contraction in February}}}\uc2 , employers added 178,000 jobs last month, ahead of economists\u8217\'20\'19 expectations of about 70,000.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate fell to 4.3%, according to data from the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . In February, the economy lost 133,000 jobs, according to revised figures. Job figures for January were revised up, from 126,000 to 160,000. With revisions, total employment in January and February is 7,000 lower than previously reported. .\par \par \uc2 Previous data painted a mixed picture of the US labor market, which economists say has been in a static \u8220\'20\'1Clow-fire, low-hire\u8221\'20\'1D state, where both layoffs and new hires are down.\par \par \uc2 Outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.challengergray.com/blog/challenger-report-march-cuts-rise-25-from-february-ai-leads-reasons/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that employers announced 217,362 job cuts in the first quarter of 2026 \u8211\'20\'13 the lowest total for that period since 2022. But hiring in February slowed to a six-year low, according to data released earlier this week, with dips seen in construction and leisure and hospitality.\par \par \uc2 The so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cquits rate\u8221\'20\'1D fell to 1.9%, the lowest since 2020, suggesting that uncertainty in the labor market has prompted more workers to stay put at their jobs.\par \par \uc2 The trend follows sluggish overall growth in the US jobs market since last year. In 2025, just 116,000 jobs were added to the economy for the entire year \u8211\'20\'13 around the same number that was added per month in previous years.\par \par \uc2 The slowdown points to caution among employers, particularly as consumer inflation experienced whiplash over the last year. US inflation dipped down to 2.3% in April 2025 before jumping to 3% in September. Price increases have been steady at 2.4% since the start of this year, though the US-Israel war with Iran is expected to drive inflation higher if the fallout continues to grow. Last month, US average gas prices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/mar/31/us-average-fuel-prices-iran-war"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 broke through}}}\uc2  $4 a gallon, and the squeeze on oil and gas is expected to trickle into other industries.\par \par \uc2 The oil price shock is reminiscent of higher prices that were seen in 2022, after Russia invaded Ukraine. US average gas prices reached $5 a gallon at the time while inflation reached a generational high of 9%. Experts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/08/us-drivers-oil-prices-iran"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 say}}}\uc2  that every $10 increase in the price of a barrel of oil can lead to 0.2% climb in inflation.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260403em43002ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
