\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California renames Cesar Chavez Day as Farmworkers Day\b0\par\par\uc2 Dani Anguiano and agencies \par \uc2 632 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 March 2026\par 13:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawmakers earlier passed bill to rename 31 March holiday following sexual abuse allegations against labor leader\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  of California signed legislation on Thursday renaming Cesar Chavez Day as Farmworkers Day in the wake of shocking allegations that the labor leader sexually abused women and young girls.\par \par \uc2 The bill, passed by the state senate earlier on Thursday, authorized the renaming ahead of the state holiday on 31 March. The state has observed the holiday honoring Chavez, who in the 1960s built a major farm-worker labor rights movement in California\u8217\'20\'19s agricultural heartland, for more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 State lawmakers quickly moved ahead with the legislation after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/18/us/cesar-chavez-sexual-abuse-allegations-ufw.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2  published a report last week in which several women, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/mar/18/dolores-huerta-cesar-chavez-abuse-allegations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dolores Huerta}}}\uc2 , who co-led the movement with Chavez, said they were sexually abused by the president of the \uc2 United Farm Workers (UFW)\uc2 . Two women who were daughters of fellow organizers said they were children when he began to groom and abuse them. The Times reported that Chavez used women who worked and volunteered in the organizing movement \u8220\'20\'1Cfor his own sexual gratification\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The allegations have sparked a wave of efforts to rename or alter memorials to Chavez, who had long been admired for helping secure better wages and working conditions for farm workers. The swift and sweeping effort to erase Chavez\u8217\'20\'19s name from public life was previously unthinkable, as his status had only grown since his death in 1993.\par \par \uc2 California was the first state to designate Chavez\u8217\'20\'19s birthday, 31 March, as a holiday nearly 30 years ago. The legislature in 2000 passed a bill to make it an official paid day off for state employees and require that students learn about his legacy and his role in the labor movement in California. Barack Obama moved to honor Cesar Chavez on that date in 2014.\par \par \uc2 The California bill passed in the assembly with bipartisan support on Monday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe cannot ignore wrongdoing and we should not continue to celebrate a single person when the movement itself is so much bigger,\u8221\'20\'1D assembly member Cecilia Aguiar-Curry said before the vote on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The Republican assembly member Alexandra Macedo said the change was to honor workers and their families.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis isn\u8217\'20\'19t just about a date on a calendar or a name on a building,\u8221\'20\'1D Macedo said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is about the hands that feed this nation. It is about the men and women who are in the orchards, in the fields, before the sun even touches the horizon, and who are still there long after it sets.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since the allegations came to light, California State University, Fresno, has covered up Chavez\u8217\'20\'19s statue on campus, while San Francisco, Los Angeles and Sacramento have taken steps to erase his name from public landmarks. Some advocated for Huerta\u8217\'20\'19s name to replace Chavez\u8217\'20\'19s, and several states had said they would not observe the day.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the city council in Phoenix, Arizona, voted to remove his name from city facilities and rename the day honoring him, while in Texas the state department of education said it would remove him from its curriculum.\par \par \uc2 The California state senate\u8217\'20\'19s pro tempore president, Monique Lim\u243\'00\'F3n, said honoring farm workers was especially important in the face of a series of federal raids across the state last year. A worker in her district died while being chased by a \uc2 US Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  (ICE) agent last summer, Lim\u243\'00\'F3n said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHis death is a reminder of how much farm workers risk every day to put food on our table,\u8221\'20\'1D she said before the vote. \u8220\'20\'1COur farm workers remind us that everyone deserves to be treated with dignity and respect.\u8221\'20\'1DThe Associated Press contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260326em3q006sh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
