\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  shareholders push to oust board members over stalled union talks\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 860 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2026\par 18:15 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Board members J\u248\'00\'F8rgen Vig Knudstorp and \uc2 Beth Ford\uc2  face scrutiny for the coffee chain\u8217\'20\'19s ongoing labor dispute\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  shareholders are pushing to remove two board members at the company who they argue have contributed to stalling the coffee chain\u8217\'20\'19s long-fought-over union drive.\par \par \uc2 The SOC Investment Group, Trillium Asset Management, Merseyside Pension Fund, the non-profit Shareholder Association for Research and Education (Share), and the New York state and New York City comptrollers wrote a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static1.squarespace.com/static/5d374de8aae9940001c8ed59/t/6995d4bf08ada36fe81bc9e2/1771427008184/SBUX+Vote+No+Letter.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter }}}\uc2 to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  shareholders to vote \u8220\'20\'1Cno\u8221\'20\'1D on the re-election of board members J\u248\'00\'F8rgen Vig Knudstorp and \uc2 Beth Ford\uc2  at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s annual shareholders meeting on 25 March.\par \par \uc2 More than{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unionelections.org/data/starbucks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  680 }}}\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores have voted to form unions since the barista-led organizing campaign started in 2021. The union has reached 34 tentative agreements with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , but the company has not reached a single final agreement.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers began an unfair labor practice strike at the coffee chain in November 2025, escalating up to the holidays in December 2025 with several thousand workers on strike. The union has since tapered down the number of workers on strike and pushed campaigns for the public to pressure the company, with actions such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/feb/04/starbucks-strike-workers-urge-customers-delete-app"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deleting }}}\uc2 the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  app, until a first contract is reached.\par \par \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO, pledged to \u8220\'20\'1Cengage constructively and in good faith\u8221\'20\'1D when he took the top job in 2024. But critics argue the company has since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/07/starbucks-brian-niccol-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gone back }}}\uc2 on those pledges.\par \par \uc2 In their letter, the shareholders argue Knudstorp, former CEO of the \uc2 Lego Group\uc2 , and Ford, CEO of the Land O\u8217\'20\'19Lakes agricultural cooperative, bear responsibility for \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ongoing labor dispute.\par \par \uc2 Knudstorp and Ford have \u8220\'20\'1Chad labor relations, board structure, and investor engagement responsibilities over the relevant time period. Shareholders should evaluate their performance against those responsibilities\u8221\'20\'1D, the shareholders wrote.\par \par \uc2 Tejal Patel, executive director of the SOC Investment Group, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  shareholder affiliated with labor unions, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a shift in 2025 which raised renewed concerns for us. Labor disputes have continued. Bargaining has not produced a first contract, and risks associated with workforce relations have intensified rather than diminished.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe sudden U-turn on labor relations oversight by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 Board is inconsistent with the company\u8217\'20\'19s turnaround strategy and commitments \u8211\'20\'13 and changes have not been explained to shareholders,\u8221\'20\'1D the shareholders wrote in the letter.\par \par \uc2 Two proxy firms have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/starbucks-may-be-neglecting-labor-dispute-risks-shareholder-proxy-firms-warn-2026-03-13/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warned}}}\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  shareholders that the company may be neglecting financial and reputational risks from labor disputes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are ongoing controversies related to labor disputes and it is not clear that there is sufficient board oversight of the company\u8217\'20\'19s management of labor relations,\u8221\'20\'1D stated \uc2 Institutional Shareholder Services\uc2  in their recommendations for cautionary support of the two board members. The proxy firm highlighted a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecity.nyc/2025/12/01/starbucks-worker-settlement-fair-workweek-schedules/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $38.9m settlement}}}\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  agreed to pay over New York City fair workweek laws, the largest in the city\u8217\'20\'19s history.\par \par \uc2 A leading proxy adviser, \uc2 Glass Lewis\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/starbucks-may-be-neglecting-labor-dispute-risks-shareholder-proxy-firms-warn-2026-03-13/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recommended}}}\uc2  voting no against the board members, citing, among its reasons, the dissolution of a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  board committee to oversee labor relations.\par \par \uc2 That committee was formed in response to shareholder concerns in 2023 over the company\u8217\'20\'19s handling of union campaigns.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou do not simply simplify oversight of a risk that\u8217\'20\'19s getting worse. You should be strengthening it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kyle Seeley, deputy director of corporate governance at the New York state common retirement fund during the webinar on the vote no campaign.\par \par \uc2 Daisy Pitkin, director of Starbucks Workers United, said no proposals had been made by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  since April 2025 after workers voted down \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s economic proposals. Pitkin said the union was looking for base wage increases, annual pay increases that don\u8217\'20\'19t lag behind inflation, and a minimum of three baristas staffed in each store.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19d like to see a floor, a starting wage at $17 an hour,\u8221\'20\'1D said Pitkin. \u8220\'20\'1CDespite \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  claiming that baristas make $30 an hour, which they have said publicly, in 34 states, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers start at $15.25 an hour, and in another nine states, they start at $16 or below, so we want to get more money into the pockets of these lowest-paid baristas.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jasmine Leli, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  barista in Buffalo, New York, where the union campaign at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  began in 2021, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe continue to file unfair labor practices due to the ongoing union busting at the store level. We continue to work understaffed, which is extremely stressful. We\u8217\'20\'19re struggling to pay our bills.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it remained committed to bargaining and claimed the shareholder proposal was similar to a previous failed effort.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Starbucks Board has the necessary skills and experience to effectively oversee our strategy, including human capital management, which is vital to our ability to drive growth and deliver for our customers,\u8221\'20\'1D Starbucks spokesperson Jaci Anderson said in an email. \u8220\'20\'1COur ongoing investments in the partner experience have enabled Starbucks to offer the best jobs in retail \u8211\'20\'13 pay and benefits average $30 an hour for hourly partners, turnover is far below the industry average, and more than a million people apply to work here every year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260317em3h002jx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
