\par\par \uc2 Food\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Highly problematic behavior\u8217\'20\'19: Noma residency in LA starts with PR crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrew Gumbel in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2026\par 01:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 After stories of Ren\u233\'00\'E9 Redzepi\u8217\'20\'19s abuse of staff resurfaced, protests and sponsorship cancellations eclipsed the restaurant\u8217\'20\'19s pop-up\par \par \uc2 It was always going to be an indulgence for Ren\u233\'00\'E9 Redzepi, the Danish-Albanian chef of Noma fame, to bring his exacting, innovative vision of haute cuisine to Los Angeles and spend several weeks tickling the palates of well-heeled diners at a hilltop estate once dubbed \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most beautiful home in Hollywood\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The timing has certainly been unfortunate, since the US is now fighting a destabilizing war in the Middle East and food prices are climbing so steeply that many ordinary Americans can no longer afford to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/business/story/2025-11-16/mcdonalds-is-losing-its-low-income-customers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eat at McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2 , much less contemplate the counterintuitive delights of tacinga cactus, bougainvillea petals, mealworms and giant tuna eyes.\par \par \uc2 Now, though, with old stories resurfacing from what {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://madfeed.co/2015/08/19/culture-of-the-kitchen-rene-redzepi/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Redzepi himself}}}\uc2  has acknowledged to be a shameful history of physical and verbal abuse in the kitchen, the Noma residency in southern California is facing a full-blown public relations emergency. Protests and a rash of last-minute sponsorship cancellations overshadowed Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s opening, and with the entire 16-week residency suddenly in peril, Redzepi announced that he was stepping down from Noma after more than two decades at the helm.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn apology is not enough; I take responsibility for my own actions,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote in an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  story with dinner yet to be served.\par \par \uc2 Whether his resignation will be enough to quiet the furor remains to be seen. Earlier in the day, as a succession of a large black and white SUVs with tinted windows drove up to the gates of the fabled Paramour Estate in Silver Lake to deliver guests to the inaugural lunch service, a small circle of protesters held up signs reading: \u8220\'20\'1CNo Michelin stars for violence\u8221\'20\'1D and: \u8220\'20\'1CYour kitchen is a crime scene.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The protest organizers, a vocal former Noma employee named Jason Ignacio White, and Sarumathi Jayaraman, a lawyer for the labor rights organization One Fair Wage, made clear they were interested in more than just a symbolic change at the top. They read out a letter demanding reparations for past actions and changes to Noma\u8217\'20\'19s management and employment policies. And they vowed to keep protesting throughout the residency.\par \par \uc2 White, who was in charge of Noma\u8217\'20\'19s fermentation, a key aspect of Redzepi\u8217\'20\'19s culinary method, has spent several weeks publishing firsthand accounts of what he characterizes as abusive conditions there, earning about 14m views for his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/microbes_vibes/?hl=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posts}}}\uc2  on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . The public relations fiasco did not kick into high gear, though, until the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  published a story last weekend {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/07/dining/rene-redzepi-noma-abuse-allegations.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 detailing}}}\uc2  some of those allegations \u8211\'20\'13 most if not all of which date back a decade or more but include accounts of Redzepi allegedly punching employees, jabbing them with kitchen implements and body-shaming them.\par \par \uc2 In response, both Redzepi and Noma issued statements acknowledging problems in the past \u8211\'20\'13 Redzepi has previously admitted to being a bully prone to explosions of rage and physical abuse \u8211\'20\'13 but said that the claims \u8220\'20\'1Cdo not reflect the workplace Noma is today\u8221\'20\'1D. The company statement went on: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve made meaningful changes to transform our culture and workplace over the last several years \u8211\'20\'13 including a fully paid internship program, improved hours and time off, expanded benefits, a dedicated HR team, leadership training, mentorship programs, and more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In an email to the Guardian, the organization added that it has launched a workplace audit, and believes it has robust policies in place to ensure fair workplace conditions in its LA operation.\par \par \uc2 Still, that was not enough to prevent a handful of sponsors of the LA venture including the online dining reservation platform Resy, the loyalty point platform Blackbird, and \uc2 American Express\uc2  to drop their support at the 11th hour and cancel several nights of bookings that they had reserved at the Paramour. Blackbird said it would resell the $1,500-a-head tickets it held and forward the proceeds to organizations fighting for better working conditions in the restaurant business.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe cannot lean on time elapsed and rehabilitation claims when these things resurface,\u8221\'20\'1D Blackbird\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, \uc2 Ben Leventhal\uc2 , said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CRegardless of context, this is highly problematic behavior.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This was hardly how Redzepi\u8217\'20\'19s team imagined their reception in Los Angeles, the latest stop on a journey of experimentation and reinvention since the original Noma restaurant in Copenhagen closed at the end of 2024. The Paramour was deemed an exciting enough location \u8211\'20\'13 built as the Spanish Mediterranean home of an oil heiress and the silent era movie star Antonio Moreno, complete with rose gardens, citrus orchards, and a swimming pool worthy of an ancient Roman villa \u8211\'20\'13 that it was kept under wraps until close to opening day.\par \par \uc2 Tickets for the residency became available in January and sold out within three minutes. When the Noma team joined forces with Courage Bagels, a popular outlet between Silver Lake and downtown Los Angeles, for a one-day pop-up a few days later, the line for the extra special $25 bagels \u8211\'20\'13 oyster mushroom, pumpkin bush, savory rose fudge, nasturtium and bergamot on sea salt sesame \u8211\'20\'13 snaked around an entire city block.\par \par \uc2 In the run-up to Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s opening, Noma\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/nomacph/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Instagram page}}}\uc2  offered teasing glimpses of the team\u8217\'20\'19s test kitchen and its multiple experiments with southern California flavors to develop a multi-course menu to \u8220\'20\'1Cblow minds\u8221\'20\'1D, as one young Noma chef put it. While the final menu is a secret known only to the staff and the first few diners, some of the experimentation has featured hibiscus flower dumplings, witches\u8217\'20\'19 butter, acorns, crickets, turkey wattle and what Redzepi describes as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe intrigue of a slimy texture\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What exactly the team intends with the giant tuna eyes has not been made clear, although one video sequence on Instagram hinted that they might end up inside sunflowers as a visually arresting amuse-bouche.\par \par \uc2 Many chefs and food enthusiasts in the Silver Lake area were excited about Noma\u8217\'20\'19s arrival and saw the choice of their part of town as an honor. The renewed abuse allegations have since split the community down the middle, with some saying that top restaurants have no excuse not to cultivate safe, supportive work environments and others defending Redzepi as an exacting artist who has made a heartfelt effort in recent years to rein in his excesses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a bummer, man,\u8221\'20\'1D said one diner who prides himself on his relationships with local chefs but did not want to be named for fear of the sort of trial by social media he felt Redzepi was being subjected to. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel bad for Noma\u8217\'20\'19s 130 employees, the 18 kids they brought with them \u8230\'20\'26 and all the Eastside restaurateurs who should be celebrating great food and collaboration with Noma for the next four months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some chefs defended Redzepi by saying they had seen worse in other kitchens. One, cited by chef Erich Eichstetter on his podcast {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/noma-under-fire-when-kitchen-culture-crosses-the-line/id1661421256?i=1000750890037"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pot Luck Stories}}}\uc2 , said his experience at Noma was \u8220\'20\'1Coverall positive\u8221\'20\'1D and he described the abuse as \u8220\'20\'1Clow to medium\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 before going on to describe seeing Redzepi push people and punch them in the stomach.\par \par \uc2 The controversy has also shone a spotlight on the economics of fine dining, with several food websites reminding readers that, as of 2019, the Noma restaurant in Copenhagen relied on unpaid interns for almost half its kitchen staff. Once the bad publicity of that revelation, first reported in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/a62a96b8-2db2-44ec-ac80-67fcf83d86ef"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Financial Times}}}\uc2 , forced Noma to start paying its interns, the additional $50,000 in monthly labor costs soon prompted Redzepi to announce he was closing his doors for good.\par \par \uc2 That, in turn, has prompted some observers to question not only the toxic masculinity that characterizes many fine dining kitchens but also whether such establishments can be anything but exploitative to survive.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe truth is that the kind of high-end dining Noma exemplifies is abusive, disingenuous, and unethical,\u8221\'20\'1D Rob Anderson, a chef in the Massachusetts beach resort of Provincetown, wrote in a much-discussed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2023/01/noma-copenhagen-fine-dining-unsustainable/672738/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 essay in the Atlantic}}}\uc2  in 2023. \u8220\'20\'1CChefs know it but continue to imitate Redzepi. The food media know it but continue to celebrate his kind of food. Wealthy diners know it but continue to book tables en masse \u8211\'20\'13 if not at Noma, then at comparable destination restaurants around the world.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jenn Harris, the Los Angeles Times food critic, announced that she would not be dining at Noma because she believed, unlike Anderson, that high-end restaurants can in fact model better work practices and behavior in the kitchen \u8211\'20\'13 and she pointed to a number in Los Angeles that pride themselves on doing so. \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s not forget that we are talking about restaurants,\u8221\'20\'1D she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/food/story/2026-03-10/noma-jenn-harris-critic-controversy-industry"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThese are not operating rooms or battlefields. Are some chefs so self-important that they believe their role above moral and societal obligations?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260312em3c000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
