\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18IG is a drug\u8217\'20\'19: jury to deliberate as US trial over social media addiction wraps up\b0\par\par\uc2 Dara Kerr \par \uc2 1,687 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2026\par 00:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  accused of creating harmful products in trial seen as a bellwether for attitudes towards social media\par \par \uc2 The first-ever jury trial over the potential harms of social media wrapped up on Thursday. Lawyers for \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  have argued their platforms are safe for the vast majority of young people, while lawyers for a young woman at the center of the case say the tech companies have designed their products to be addictive, leading to mental health issues in children and teens.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow did they become such behemoths?\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Mark Lanier\uc2  , a lawyer for the plaintiffs, said during closing arguments in Los Angeles superior court on Thursday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/social-media/social-media-trial-los-angeles-la-meta-youtube-rcna263063"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to NBC}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the attention economy. They\u8217\'20\'19re making money off capturing your attention.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The six-week trial has seen a parade of high-profile witnesses, including \uc2 Meta\uc2  chief executive \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  head Adam Mosseri and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s vice-president of engineering Cristos Goodrow. Jurors have also{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/26/us-trial-social-media-addiction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  heard testimony from the lead plaintiff}}}\uc2 , a 20-year-old woman who goes by the initials KGM, her therapist, whistleblowers and expert witnesses on social media and addiction.\par \par \uc2 If jurors rule in favor of KGM, the social media companies could face harsh financial penalties, which plaintiffs\u8217\'20\'19 lawyers hope will lead them to change fundamental aspects of how their platforms function. In this case, the burden of proof is on the plaintiffs. The jury would need to find negligence and causation by \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2  before it could impose damages, so the outcome of the trial could take several different forms. Deliberations are set to begin on Friday.\par \par \uc2 KGM said she got hooked on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  starting at six and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  at nine. By the time she was 10, she said, she had become depressed and was engaging in self-harm as a result. The cycle of social media use caused her to have strained relationships with her family and in school, she testified. She said she had suicidal thoughts and began cutting herself as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccoping mechanism to deal with my depression\u8221\'20\'1D. When she was 13, KGM\u8217\'20\'19s therapist diagnosed her with body dysmorphic disorder and social phobia, which KGM attributes to her use of \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 KGM\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers say her experience is emblematic of what tens of thousands of young people have faced on social media and in their offline lives.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  deny wrongdoing. A \uc2 YouTube\uc2  spokesperson, Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Casta\u241\'00\'F1eda, called the allegations in the lawsuits \u8220\'20\'1Csimply not true\u8221\'20\'1D and said that providing young people with a \u8220\'20\'1Csafer, healthier experience has always been core to our work\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesperson said in a statement that KGM\u8217\'20\'19s mental health issues were brought on by a difficult home life, a key argument in the company\u8217\'20\'19s case, saying she \u8220\'20\'1Chas faced profound challenges, and we continue to recognize all she has endured. The jury\u8217\'20\'19s only task, however, is to decide if those struggles would have existed without \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A bellwether for views of social media\par \par \uc2 This trial is the first in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jan/27/social-media-trial-meta-tiktok-youtube"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 consolidated group of cases}}}\uc2  brought against \uc2 Meta\uc2  , \uc2 TikTok\uc2  , \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and Snap on behalf of more than 1,600 plaintiffs, including more than 350 families and 250 school districts. \uc2 TikTok\uc2  and Snap settled the KGM lawsuit just before trial.\par \par \uc2 KGM\u8217\'20\'19s case is also the first of more than 20 \u8220\'20\'1Cbellwether\u8221\'20\'1D trials, which are slated to go to court over the next couple of years and are used to gauge juries\u8217\'20\'19 reactions as well as set legal precedent. The next bellwether case is scheduled to go to trial in July.\par \par \uc2 Online safety advocates, parents and the plaintiff lawyers say no matter how the jury decides they have already won.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFour years ago, when we started suing social media companies, nobody thought that we would ever get to this point,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Matthew Bergman\uc2  , founder of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://socialmediavictims.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Social Media Victims Law Center}}}\uc2  and an attorney representing the plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWin or lose the outcome of this trial, victims in the United States have won, because now we know that social media companies can and will be held accountable before a fair and impartial jury.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What was revealed during trial\par \par \uc2 KGM\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers allege some of the features that social media companies built into their platforms, such as an infinitely scrollable feed and video autoplay, are designed to keep people on the apps and create to their addictive quality. The lawyers also allege that \u8220\'20\'1Clike\u8221\'20\'1D buttons feed into teens\u8217\'20\'19 desire for validation and features such as beauty filters can distort young peoples\u8217\'20\'19 self-image.\par \par \uc2 Troves of previously sealed documents came to light during KGM\u8217\'20\'19s trial that showed some employees inside \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  considered the platforms either addictive or ineffective in their efforts to protect the wellbeing of young people.\par \par \uc2 An internal document from \uc2 YouTube\uc2  in 2021 poses the question, \u8220\'20\'1CHow are we measuring wellbeing?\u8221\'20\'1D and adds the response: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not.\u8221\'20\'1D Another document details how kids under 13 are the fastest growing internet audience in the world and presents the opportunity of YouTube playing the digital babysitter to children as young as eight. One document reads: \u8220\'20\'1C[The] goal is not viewership, it\u8217\'20\'19s viewer addiction.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Documents from Meta show that some employees questioned the company\u8217\'20\'19s leadership over targeting of young audiences. In an email from 2017, an employee writes to a colleague, \u8220\'20\'1Coh good, we\u8217\'20\'19re going after <13 year olds now?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The colleague responds, \u8220\'20\'1Czuck has been talking about that for a while,\u8221\'20\'1D which prompts the first employee to say, \u8220\'20\'1Cyeah it was gross the last time he mentioned it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A separate email conversation between Meta employees in 2020 shows one person saying, \u8220\'20\'1Coh my gosh yall IG is a drug\u8221\'20\'1D. A colleague responds, \u8220\'20\'1CLol, I mean, all social media. We\u8217\'20\'19re basically pushers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The dialog continues with the employees likening social media\u8217\'20\'19s draw to being similar to gambling with \u8220\'20\'1Creward tolerance\u8221\'20\'1D getting so high that people \u8220\'20\'1Ccan\u8217\'20\'19t feel reward anymore\u8221\'20\'1D. The conversation concludes with an employee saying: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s kind of scary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Meta and YouTube say they did nothing wrong\par \par \uc2 During the trial, Meta and YouTube\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers denied that their platforms were addictive. YouTube\u8217\'20\'19s counsel pointed to parental controls and internal statistics that showed the average person\u8217\'20\'19s use of the video streaming platform usually lasts less than 30 minutes a day. During his testimony, Goodrow said YouTube is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot designed to maximize time\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Meta focused its arguments on KGM and testimony from her therapist, saying her problems had little to do with social media. Lawyers cited medical records from when KGM was 13, which contained quotes of her saying her mother fat-shamed and screamed at her. The lawyers alleged abuse led to the young woman\u8217\'20\'19s mental health issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHer records show significant emotional and physical abuse, academic struggles and psychiatric conditions, separate from her social media usage,\u8221\'20\'1D the Meta spokesperson said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe evidence simply doesn\u8217\'20\'19t support reducing a lifetime of hardship to a single factor, and our case will continue to underscore that reality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 KGM still lives with her mother, who was present throughout the trial. During her testimony, KGM took issue with what the lawyers cited from her medical records when she was young, saying her mother \u8220\'20\'1Cwasn\u8217\'20\'19t perfect but she was trying her best \u8230\'20\'26 I don\u8217\'20\'19t think I would call it abuse or neglect or anything like that\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 During closing arguments on Thursday, Meta\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer Paul Schmidt once again pointed out KGM\u8217\'20\'19s contentious relationship with her mother. According to NBC, he played jurors a video appearing to show her mother yelling at her. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s possible to hear those recordings and think: \u8216\'20\'18Gosh, this is normal tension,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Schmidt said. \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18Gosh, she\u8217\'20\'19s dramatizing this.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 YouTube\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer Luis Li said during closing arguments that there \u8220\'20\'1Cisn\u8217\'20\'19t a single mention of an addiction to YouTube\u8221\'20\'1D in KGM\u8217\'20\'19s medical records. He also drew attention to KGM\u8217\'20\'19s previous testimony when she said she lost interest in YouTube as she got older. \u8220\'20\'1CAsk whether anybody suffering from addiction could just say: \u8216\'20\'18Yeah, I kinda lost interest,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Li said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s your common sense tell you about that?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Can social media cause an addiction?\par \par \uc2 During trial, Meta contended with the idea of addiction, saying that KGM never received that official diagnosis. When {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/feb/11/instagram-adam-mosseri-social-media-addiction-trial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mosseri took the stand}}}\uc2 , he took it further by pushing back on the science behind social media addiction, denying that users could be \u8220\'20\'1Cclinically addicted\u8221\'20\'1D. Psychologists do not classify social media addiction as an official diagnosis, but researchers have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2025/jun/18/teenagers-social-media-mobile-phones-video-games-mental-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 documented}}}\uc2  the harmful consequences of compulsive use among young people.\par \par \uc2 During closing arguments, Lanier compared Instagram\u8217\'20\'19s endless scroll and YouTube\u8217\'20\'19s autoplay to getting free tortilla chips at a restaurant and not being able to stop eating them, according to NBC. He said other features, such as notifications and \u8220\'20\'1Clikes\u8221\'20\'1D from friends, adds to the addictive quality of platforms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow do you make a child never put down the phone? That\u8217\'20\'19s called the engineering of addiction. They engineered it, they put these features on the phones,\u8221\'20\'1D Lanier said. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are Trojan horses: they look wonderful and great ... but you invite them in and they take over.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 KGM\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers\u8217\'20\'19 arguments mirror those brought against big tobacco in the 1990s, which focused on cigarettes\u8217\'20\'19 addictive qualities and companies publicly denying that for decades even while knowing their products\u8217\'20\'19 harms. Online safety advocates and parents say the social media companies have refused to look at their effects on young people and instead have used a \u8220\'20\'1Cblame the victim\u8221\'20\'1D tactic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re really truly pulling from big tobacco\u8217\'20\'19s playbook. Blame the victim, blame the parents, blame the child, blame anyone but the products they designed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sacha Haworth, the executive director of the Tech Oversight Project, which has been involved in advocacy work for the plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are the most profitable corporations in the history of the world. And they could make these changes if they wanted to. But instead of doing that, they attack the victims.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260312em3c0073m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
