\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US lost 92,000 jobs in February just before Trump joined Iran conflict\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 627 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2026\par 18:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 42\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate was 4.4% in February, with 130,000 jobs added in JanuaryBusiness live \u8211\'20\'13 latest updates\par \par \uc2 The US lost 92,000 jobs in February, an unexpected major slackening in the labor market that came just before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  threw the global {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/useconomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 economy}}}\uc2  into upheaval with his conflict in Iran.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate edged up to 4.4% in February. In comparison, the US added a revised 126,000 jobs in January, far surpassing expectations of 70,000 jobs but still less than January 2025. Economists {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/05/economy/us-jobs-report-february-preview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 predicted}}}\uc2  an increase of 60,000 jobs added in February and a steady unemployment rate of 4.3%.\par \par \uc2 The report revised down job figures from December and January. Figures in December went down by 65,000, from 48,000 job gains to 17,000 losses. January saw a much smaller drop, revised down by 4,000 jobs, from 130,000 to 126,000.\par \par \uc2 January\u8217\'20\'19s job {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/feb/11/delayed-us-january-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  also included revisions that brought down the total number of jobs added to the economy in 2025 to 181,000 jobs \u8211\'20\'13 the weakest year of job growth since Covid and a substantial decrease from the 2m jobs added to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/useconomy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy}}}\uc2  in 2024. And the job growth in 2025 was concentrated in the first half of the year: from July to December 2025, the US economy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cepr.net/publications/february-2026-jobs-preview/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost 45,000 jobs}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The job losses were broadly based. Healthcare jobs saw a huge swing down in February, going from 77,000 new jobs in January to 28,000 losses in February. Physician offices lost 37,000 jobs primarily due to strike activity. The information sector, which includes media and telecommunications, and transportation and warehousing trended down 11,000 each.\par \par \uc2 Federal government employment also continued to decline, dropping 10,000 last month. After reaching a peak in October 2024, federal government employment is down 11%, or 330,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 And while the unemployment rate has remained stable, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://email.berlinrosen.com/c/eJwkzDFuxSAMANDTwBiBcWwyMHTJ1Hb5JzDB_CClSUX4PX9V9R3glcRl8YG91eSZgXCJC9k9AeKyqIDmynGLokw5wqxYgMIc0LYEDsgFhz4izjRtWcgja2TMAiIGXdZ-tLNft57Tdn3ZI-1jfN8mvBlYDay1a5nucVyvdlct09WfBtZbe9PbwPr--fDo_pDt6aNtu-gxPaSdMi6Dbuz6fEkvTf77kWrGWjBjBN6k8oZIFZmCsMsYorcj-eqJILiZOHAF4BlQsaKqQgxc7E-C3wAAAP__aQRQ1Q"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Black unemployment rate}}}\uc2  has remained high. Last year, the rate jumped from 6.2 %in January 2025 to 8.2% in November, before decreasing to 7.7% in February. In comparison, the White unemployment rate has remained steady and below the national average at 3.7%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe job market is struggling in the face of so many headwinds. Companies are cautious to hire and some industries are doing layoffs. The February jobs report was dismal with heavy job losses, even in healthcare, and a rise in the unemployment rate,\u8221\'20\'1D said Heather Long, chief economist at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tracking.us.nylas.com/l/088ea17df49741efb3fee4f6c25099e2/1/ffc3b955f53efbf03b37bd0817790adbd7bb04059a719aeb1a5cd72958f5e418?cache_buster=1772805322"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Navy federal credit union}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Long noted that the US economy has essentially added no jobs since last April, when Trump announced his slate of tariffs. \u8220\'20\'1CAlmost every major sector of the economy has been shedding jobs. Even healthcare, the one bright spot had a weak February, though that is attributed to strike activity,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Because the data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  released on Friday is solely focused on jobs in February, it does not capture the global shock waves caused by the US-Israel conflict with Iran. But the new jobs data will be influential in shaping the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s upcoming meeting over interest rates on 17 and 18 March.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear where Fed officials will land on interest rates, though some seem cautious about making any changes. Ahead of the meeting, \uc2 Beth M Hammack\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/04/business/fed-hammack-inflation-interest-rates.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called}}}\uc2  for an extended pause of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interest rates}}}\uc2  amid concerns about inflation, even though Trump has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/01/30/business/trump-kevin-warsh-fed-rates.html#:~:text=not%20be%20easy.-,Mr.,percent%20to%203.75%20percent%20range."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pushing}}}\uc2  to lower them.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 6 March 2026. The December job figures went down by 65,000, not 68,000 as stated in an earlier version.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260306em36003mm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
