\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Three Australians were on US submarine that sank Iranian warship, PM says\b0\par\par\uc2 Josh Butler, Ben Doherty and Krishani Dhanji \par \uc2 1,109 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2026\par 05:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese confirms ADF members were on vessel but says \u8216\'20\'18no Australian personnel\u8217\'20\'19 took part in attacks on IranMiddle East crisis \u8211\'20\'13 live updatesFollow our Australia news live blog for latest updatesGet our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese has confirmed that three {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/mar/05/iran-warship-australian-crew-onboard-us-submarine-foreign-minister-refuses-to-say"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australians were on a US submarine that sank an Iranian warship}}}\uc2 , after the Labor government earlier refused to comment on reports that emerged on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister said the Australian defence force personnel were on the submarine as part of an Aukus training program.\par \par \uc2 But he maintained that Australian forces were in compliance with international law, and said: \u8220\'20\'1CNo Australian personnel have participated in any offensive action against Iran.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asked about implications for international law in the US and Israeli strikes on Iran, Albanese said Australia was \u8220\'20\'1Ccomfortable\u8221\'20\'1D with assessing that Tehran posed a threat on three levels.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=copyembed&CMP=emailbutton"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Sign up: AU Breaking News email }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t normally confirm such an issue, but given our NSC [national security committee of cabinet] meetings, in the public interest I can confirm that there were three Australian personnel onboard that vessel,\u8221\'20\'1D Albanese told Sky News Australia on Friday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can confirm also, though, that no Australian personnel have participated in any offensive action against Iran.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are longstanding third-country arrangements that have been in place for a long period of time. And what they do is ensure that Australian defence force personnel, where they\u8217\'20\'19re embedded in third countries\u8217\'20\'19 defence assets, they act in accordance with Australian law, with Australian policy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Australian government previously refused to disclose whether Australian sailors or officers were onboard the US attack submarine that torpedoed and sank an Iranian warship in the Indian Ocean on Wednesday, killing at least 87 people.\par \par \uc2 The Greens senator \uc2 David Shoebridge\uc2  called the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s claim that no Australian personnel were involved in offensive action \u8220\'20\'1Cextraordinary\u8221\'20\'1D and said it was inevitable that Australian personnel would be involved in US action.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis makes Australia obviously, clearly, unambiguously, part of an illegal war, part of a war that is breaking down the norms of international law and making the world a less safe place,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is as sure as day follows night that Australians throughout the US military are actively involved, part of the United States and Israel\u8217\'20\'19s illegal war in Iran.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shoebridge said Australia was a \u8220\'20\'1Chostage\u8221\'20\'1D not an ally in the relationship with the US, with the nation \u8220\'20\'1Cdragged into this war without even a decision being made inside the cabinet room\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Albanese said the three Australians onboard the submarine were there as part of the Aukus defence pact training program.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s one of the big pluses behind the Aukus arrangements, Australian personnel getting experience across a range of assets, including being onboard nuclear-powered submarines, but also the exchange that\u8217\'20\'19s occurring across the board.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Hansard/Hansard_Display?bid=committees/estimate/28998/&sid=0000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 50 Australian sailors and officers}}}\uc2  are serving in the US attack submarine fleet, a training regimen that is part of preparations for Australia to command its own nuclear-powered submarines under the Aukus deal.\par \par \uc2 According to the Royal Australian Navy, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.defence.gov.au/news-events/news/2025-11-27/aussie-torpedoman-takes-us-attack-subs#:~:text=Nuclear%2Dpowered%20attack%20submarines%20are%20part%20of%20the,course**%20*%20**Advanced%20training%20on%20torpedoman%20fundamentals**"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in 10 crew members}}}\uc2  onboard \uc2 US Navy\uc2  attack submarines is Australian.\par \par \uc2 The US submarine strike on the Islamic Republic of Iran Ship Dena was the first time an enemy vessel has been sunk by a US torpedo since the second world war, the US defence secretary, \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2 , told a Pentagon briefing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn American submarine sank an Iranian warship that thought it was safe in international waters. It was sunk by a torpedo, a quiet death.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hegseth has promised \u8220\'20\'1Csheer destruction\u8221\'20\'1D for the Iranian regime.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica is winning decisively, devastatingly and without mercy \u8230\'20\'26 they are toast and they know it,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon has not identified which submarine was involved in the attack on Wednesday but defence trade press reported that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.globaldefensecorp.com/2026/03/05/u-s-navys-hmas-stirling-based-aukus-rotational-virginia-class-nuclear-submarine-uss-minnesota-torpedoed-iranian-frigate-off-sri-lanka/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it was the USS Minnesota}}}\uc2 , a Virginia-class submarine that rotated through the HMAS Stirling base in Western Australia last year.\par \par \uc2 Iran\u8217\'20\'19s foreign minister, Seyed Abbas Araghchi, described the US attack as an \u8220\'20\'1Catrocity at sea, 2,000 miles from Iran\u8217\'20\'19s shores\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMark my words: the US will come to bitterly regret the precedent it has set.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At least 87 Iranian sailors were killed. Thirty-two were pulled from the water by the Sri Lankan navy. Up to 180 personnel were believed onboard.\par \par \uc2 The frigate was sailing in international waters as it returned from a naval exercise organised by India in the Bay of Bengal.\par \par \uc2 A professor of international law at the \uc2 Australian National University\uc2 , Donald Rothwell, said while he believed the initial strikes by the US and Israel on Iran had no legal basis, there was no illegality in the US targeting the Iranian naval vessel as the two countries were now engaged in armed conflict.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CArmed conflict has commenced and the members of the Iranian navy are legitimate combatants for those purposes,\u8221\'20\'1D Rothwell said.\par \par \uc2 He said the presence of the three Australians on the submarine did not make Australia party to the broader conflict.\par \par \uc2 But Shoebridge said the decision to \u8220\'20\'1Cabandon\u8221\'20\'1D the Iranian survivors from the warship, to be picked up later by Sri Lanka, was a contravention of the second Geneva convention \u8220\'20\'1Cwhen there was no credible threat to the US nuclear submarine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A senior lecturer in law at Adelaide University, Juliette McIntyre, agreed with Rothwell that due to the conflict, a military vessel like the war ship is \u8220\'20\'1Cconsidered to be a lawful target\u8221\'20\'1D, adding, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe initial targeting doesn\u8217\'20\'19t appear to be any kind of blatant breach of the laws of war\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But she said the issue of \u8220\'20\'1Cabandoning\u8221\'20\'1D Iranian survivors was less clear cut.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe second Geneva Convention does require aid to be rendered ordinarily \u8230\'20\'26 but because it\u8217\'20\'19s a submarine there are some debates whether it is possible for a submarine to do that because they would have to surface,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut because it was so far away from what you would call the main theatre of war, there wasn\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily any real risk to the submarine surfacing and rendering aid.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The national patron of Labor Against War and a former ALP senator, Doug Cameron, said the presence of the Australians confirmed the concerns of many rank-and-file party members that the Aukus agreement meant Australia could get \u8220\'20\'1Croped into\u8221\'20\'1D any future US conflict.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are grave, distressing and terrible developments, especially for a Labor government,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CLabor have always sought to pursue peace and independence and we have given that away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260306em360008d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
