\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Workers at top 20 US low-wage firms rely on public assistance, report says\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2026\par 14:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Exclusive: study finds workers at 20 S&P 500 firms rely on Medicaid and Snap as CEO pay and buybacks soarSign up for the Breaking News US email to get newsletter alerts in your inbox\par \par \uc2 Many workers at some of the largest US corporations have no choice but to rely on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/healthcare"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 healthcare}}}\uc2  and food assistance because of low wages, even as CEO compensation continues to grow, according to a new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ips-dc.org/report-americas-20-largest-low-wage-employers-and-the-affordability-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  released Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The report, published by the Institute of Policy Studies, focuses on 20 of the S&P 500 corporations that have primarily US-based workforces and report the lowest median wages of the group.\par \par \uc2 Collectively, this \u8220\'20\'1CLow-Wage 20\u8221\'20\'1D employs 6.7 million people in the US. The median pay at a majority (75%) of the companies is lower than the income minimum for a family of three to be eligible for Medicaid in most states. At 13 of the companies, median pay was also lower than the Supplemental Nutrition Assistant Program income threshold for a family of three.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a quarter of \uc2 Walmart\uc2  employees (29.3%) and half of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers (48.4%) in the Nevada \u8211\'20\'13 which collects Medicaid enrollment numbers among employees at large companies \u8211\'20\'13 were on Medicaid in 2024, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 Among the four states that disclose Snap data related to large companies \u8211\'20\'13 Colorado, Massachusetts, Illinois, and Michigan \u8211\'20\'13 10,920 \uc2 Walmart\uc2  workers and 9,633 \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers were enrolled in Snap in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The report noted Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1COne Big Beautiful Bill\u8221\'20\'1D is expected to result in some 7.5 million Americans losing Medicaid and 4 million losing some or all of their Snap benefits after budget cuts.\par \par \uc2 The corporations also spent massive mounts of stock buybacks in 2024, totaling $32.5bn. Lowe\u8217\'20\'19s and \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  top the stock buyback list in the report, with Lowe\u8217\'20\'19s spending $46.6bn on stock buybacks during this period, and \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  spending $37.9bn.\par \par \uc2 Had all the companies spent that money to bolster worker pay, the wage of a million workers could have risen from $29,087 to $59,600 \u8211\'20\'13 the income level needed to afford the average rent for a two-bedroom apartment in the US, noted the report.\par \par \uc2 Instead, average median pay among the \u8220\'20\'1CLow-Wage 20\u8221\'20\'1D declined from 2019 to 2024 when adjusted for inflation by 4.6%, from $30,474 to $29,087.\par \par \uc2 Despite \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  offering a 401K matching program, many workers haven\u8217\'20\'19t been able to afford the benefit: 45% of eligible employees had balances of zero in their plan accounts in 2024. That same year, \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  CEO, had a compensation of $95.8m, compared to the median pay of $14,674, a ratio of $6,666 to $1.\par \par \uc2 Average CEO pay across the 20 corporations reached $18.9m in 2024, with the average CEO to median worker pay ratio of $899 to $1 at these companies. The wealth of sixteen billionaires are tied to these 20 companies, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , eight members of \uc2 Walmart\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Walton family and \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , the former \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  CEO, the report noted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen corporations can get away with shifting their employees\u8217\'20\'19 basic living costs onto taxpayers, this is a form of corporate welfare,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Sarah Anderson\uc2 , director of the Global Economy Project at the Institute for Policy Studies and author of the report, in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CWith the federal government slashing spending on anti-poverty programs, it\u8217\'20\'19s even more important that major corporations in the world\u8217\'20\'19s richest country pay their employees a living wage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  claimed their pay is among the best in the industry, retail. According to a 2024 report, \uc2 Amazon{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nelp.org/app/uploads/2024/04/Amazons-Outsized-Role-5-1-24.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 employs 79%}}}\uc2  of the workforce in US warehouses with over 1,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPointing fingers at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  over Snap and/or Medicaid is a red herring when eligibility is based on total household income and size \u8211\'20\'13 and not individual wages,\u8221\'20\'1D said an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesperson in an email. \u8220\'20\'1CAs we\u8217\'20\'19ve said for years, what really needs to happen is a significant and large increase in the federal minimum wage \u8211\'20\'13 that would be a big boost for American families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  and Lowe\u8217\'20\'19s did not respond to multiple requests for comment\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Walmart\uc2  said in an email \u8220\'20\'1Cwe offer a ladder of opportunity so people can build a career at \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , which includes a small percentage of our workforce that comes to us on public assistance. We hire them, train them and give them the chance to build a better life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  did not comment on the 401k citation but said the company provides benefits like healthcare, equity grants and tuition coverage, noting in an email that employees \u8220\'20\'1Cstay with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  at far higher rates than the retail average\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260304em34003h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
