\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US contractors in Kuwait decry meager bunkers and pay cuts amid Iran war: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re treated as expendable\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Katie McQue \par \uc2 1,067 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2026\par 12:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees say they have heard little from major defense contractor \uc2 V2X Inc\uc2  about safety and evacuation protocols\par \par \uc2 Employees of major defense contractor \uc2 V2X Inc\uc2  on US military bases in Kuwait say they lack adequate bunker facilities and have had their pay reduced amid Iranian missile attacks across the Gulf region, while receiving limited communication from their employer about safety and evacuation procedures.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  interviewed three V2X employees on the US bases Camp Arifjan and Camp Buehring in Kuwait, following Iranian missile strikes on Kuwait, Bahrain, the United Arab Emirates, Qatar and Jordan on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The attacks follow United States and Israeli strikes on Iran earlier on Saturday. Iran has since launched a series of retaliatory attacks targeting US military bases and, according to regional authorities, some civilian infrastructure in the Persian Gulf.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe worst part is, the company has yet to put out anything so we don\u8217\'20\'19t know what\u8217\'20\'19s happening or what would happen to us if our base gets hit or anything. We got zero instructions,\u8221\'20\'1D said one American worker, speaking on condition of anonymity for fear of reprisals. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have nobody and no instructions. We don\u8217\'20\'19t know what will happen or how we would even get out of here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  has requested comment from \uc2 V2X Inc\uc2  and did not hear back by press time.\par \par \uc2 V2X workers in Kuwait and Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, received an alert email from the company at 11.58am local time on the day of the attacks. The message, reviewed by the Guardian, read: \u8220\'20\'1CPER GIANT VOICE! INCOMING, INCOMING, TAKE COVER, TAKE COVER. MOVE TO THE NEAREST BUNKER OR HARDENED BUILDING AT CAMP BUEHRING. SECURE IBA AND HELMET!!! THIS IS NOT A TEST !!!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers interviewed said contract employees are assigned different bunker facilities from military personnel on the bases. Some of the shelters resemble above-ground concrete tunnels with open ends, while others are enclosed structures with metal doors but \u8220\'20\'1Cno light, barely enough air and total darkness\u8221\'20\'1D, they said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are all packed into the bunkers that are closest to wherever you work,\u8221\'20\'1D a second American worker said.\par \par \uc2 The two American contract workers the Guardian spoke to alleged that US military personnel stationed in Kuwait were evacuated in the past few weeks, leaving the bases relatively empty, while contractors remained in the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt almost seemed like [the Pentagon] evacuated the soldiers so if anything would happen safely, for instance, that base was hit it would be less casualties on the military standpoint,\u8221\'20\'1D said the second American worker. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat about us? Are we just considered casualties of war? How did they leave before us?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The civilian contractor workforce at US bases in Kuwait had been scaled back in the past week in anticipation of potential conflict with Iran, according to the workers. Earlier in the week, workers deemed \u8220\'20\'1Cnon-essential\u8221\'20\'1D were told to remain in their accommodation, and not given the option to evacuate, according to the two American workers. To leave Kuwait, they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cfr.org/backgrounders/what-kafala-system"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 require}}}\uc2  an exit permit signed by their employer, one worker said. Under the region\u8217\'20\'19s strict labor regulations, particularly those linked to the kafala sponsorship system, workers who leave their jobs without their employer\u8217\'20\'19s permission have in many cases faced arrest and imprisonment on charges of \u8220\'20\'1Cabsconding\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody talks about the military but nobody talks about us,\u8221\'20\'1D said the first American worker. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are stuck here and treated like we are expendable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The situation is hugely traumatic for workers and their families, both American workers noted.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe overall lack of planning and communication has created an emotional strain for me. Every noise has us on edge,\u8221\'20\'1D said the second American worker. \u8220\'20\'1CThis situation has triggered anxiety, as it has resurfaced memories of my previous deployments to Iraq. It has been difficult to sleep.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 V2X Inc\uc2  was formed in July 2022 following a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://investors.gov2x.com/news/news-details/2022/Vectrus-and-Vertex-Complete-Combination-Establishing-V2X-as-a-Leading-Provider-of-Critical-Mission-Solutions-Globally/default.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $2.1bn merger}}}\uc2  between \uc2 Vectrus\uc2  and the Vertex Company. The company holds a LOGCAP V contract supporting US military operations in Kuwait, providing logistics and base operations services, including roles such as mechanics, warehouse staff, dining facility workers and IT personnel.\par \par \uc2 Workers said that following base closures during the attacks, the company reduced their paid hours. Many typically work 12-hour days and receive overtime pay, all three employees the Guardian spoke to said, adding that they were informed their hours would be reduced to eight per day.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a low salary here. And now I\u8217\'20\'19m being paid less because I\u8217\'20\'19m sitting in a room,\u8221\'20\'1D said a worker from India, employed by V2X via a subcontractor. The worker said their base salary is $493 per month, with total pay typically reaching about $819 based on 72 hours of work per week.\par \par \uc2 The two American workers said the only communication they received from a supervisor during the attacks concerned a new payroll code to enter into their timesheets, which would reduce their pay. The Guardian has reviewed the message.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s the only or the main thing that the company is worried about,\u8221\'20\'1D said the first American worker, who earns about $20 per hour.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, Kuwait\u8217\'20\'19s tap water is not considered suitable for drinking. Workers said they have enough bottled water to last for the next two to three days, but were uncertain how additional supplies would be obtained after that.\par \par \uc2 Missiles struck several locations across the region on Saturday. Authorities in the United Arab Emirates said one person was killed in a ballistic missile strike in Abu Dhabi, and that air defense systems intercepted other projectiles. In the UAE, both the international airports in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/four-injured-after-incident-dubai-international-airport-dubai-media-office-says-2026-02-28/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dubai}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/ad_airports/status/2027889161315750260?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Abu Dhabi}}}\uc2  were struck with missiles, as were the luxury residential and tourist destination Palm Jumeirah in Dubai, and explosions occurred in the skies above residential areas including Jumeirah Lake Towers (JLT) and Discovery Gardens. A residential area in Doha, Qatar, was also hit. The Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps announced the closure of the strait of Hormuz, a strategic shipping route through which about 20% of the world\u8217\'20\'19s oil supply passes.\par \par \uc2 Kuwait\u8217\'20\'19s General Authority of Civil Aviation said an Iranian drone struck terminal 1 of Kuwait international airport, resulting in minor injuries to several workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re definitely stuck,\u8221\'20\'1D said the first American worker. \u8220\'20\'1CThey should\u8217\'20\'19ve got us out of here a week ago.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260301em31001p8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
