\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Two staff stabbed after US contractor used Kia Carnival to transfer Australian immigration detainee: \u8216\'20\'18You might as well hire an \uc2 Uber\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Christopher Knaus and Ariel Bogle \par \uc2 774 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2026\par 14:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Exclusive: Home affairs department intervened about the use of non-modified people movers after 500 detention centre staff flagged safety concernsGet our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 The American private prison operator running Australia\u8217\'20\'19s immigration network used an unsecured and unmodified Kia Carnival to transport a detainee who allegedly stabbed two of its staff during the journey and fled.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia can also reveal that concerns about the vehicles being used by \uc2 Management and Training Corporation\uc2  (MTC) had prompted an intervention by the department of home affairs and warnings from almost 500 detention centre staff.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, the Australian government awarded a $2.3bn contract to Secure Journeys, a local subsidiary of private prison company MTC, to run its onshore immigration detention centres.\par \par \uc2 The contract was awarded despite serious questions about MTC\u8217\'20\'19s history in the US, where it has been accused of defrauding the state of Mississippi, as well as egregious security failures that led to the alleged gang-rape of a female detainee and the murder of two retirees by an escaped prisoner.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=copyembed&CMP=emailbutton"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Sign up: AU Breaking News email }}}\par \par \uc2 Australian staff have already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/feb/06/workers-accuse-new-company-managing-australias-immigration-detention-centres-of-running-them-like-a-prison-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 begun airing concerns}}}\uc2  about the centre\u8217\'20\'19s operations, including claims MTC is severely understaffing detention centres.\par \par \uc2 Now, Guardian Australia can reveal that the company is also facing serious questions about the potentially dangerous method it is using to transport detainees to and from immigration detention centres.\par \par \uc2 The company has at times used standard, unmodified people-movers such as Kia Carnivals and Hyundai Starias, which lack basic safety modifications, including Perspex barriers, to protect staff.\par \par \uc2 In one case in May last year, MTC used a Kia Carnival to transport detainee Paea Teu from Villawood to Sydney airport for deportation.\par \par \uc2 The detainee allegedly stabbed two MTC staff and fled the vehicle, sparking a manhunt. He now faces a string of criminal charges in the NSW local court.\par \par \uc2 Video and images from the scene show the Kia Carnival he was transported in had no protective barriers for staff.\par \par \uc2 Vehicle registration records show the car MTC was using was insured as a \u8220\'20\'1Chire and drive yourself vehicle\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Neither the department nor MTC answered questions on whether MTC was using rental cars to transport detainees.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia understands that the department has raised the suitability of the vehicles being used for transport directly with MTC.\par \par \uc2 Some vehicles have now had barriers added, one source at Yongah Hill Immigration Detention Centre said.\par \par \uc2 The department can\u8217\'20\'19t direct the day-to-day use of individual vehicles but can hold MTC accountable for a failure to meet contractual and work health and safety obligations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 Australian Border Force\uc2  requires that the transportation of immigration detainees is conducted safely, securely and in accordance with contractual and work health and safety requirements,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson for the department said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Department closely monitors the performance of its detention service providers, including the suitability and fit\u8209\'20\'11out of vehicles used for transport and escort activities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere issues are identified, the Department works with the service provider to ensure risks are addressed and appropriate safeguards are in place for staff, detainees and the community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Staff working at the detention centres raised concerns about the suitability of transport and escort vehicles being used by MTC in an open letter to the \uc2 Australian Border Force\uc2  in September last year. The letter, signed by 483 detention centre workers, complained that staff were \u8220\'20\'1Cnot being provided with the proper tools to do our jobs \u8211\'20\'13 including T&E vehicles\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The United Workers Union, which represents MTC staff, said it was aware of concerns about the use of \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople-mover style vehicles to transport detainees\u8221\'20\'1D and said it was \u8220\'20\'1Camong the many safety concerns we have consistently raised with MTC and the department\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 UWU executive director of allied industries, Godfrey Moase, said the union had formally raised those concerns through the open letter which called for the transport and escort fleet to be replaced or modified.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMembers have reported that, compared to previous arrangements under \uc2 Serco\uc2 , some of the vehicles currently in use do not provide the same level of security protections for workers, including the absence of physical separation between detainees and staff,\u8221\'20\'1D Moase said.Concerns have also been raised by the union and staff about the use of labour hire drivers for detainee transport. \u8220\'20\'1CYou might as well hire an \uc2 Uber\uc2  and do an escort,\u8221\'20\'1D said one detention service officer at Brisbane Immigration Detention Centre.\par \par \uc2 MTC declined to comment, referring questions to the department.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260227em2r003pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
