\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California fast food workers, still reeling from ICE raids, demand employers step up\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 760 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2026\par 13:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union-backed pledge urges fast food employers to protect workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights as immigration raids fuel fear and walkouts\par \par \uc2 Fast food workers in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California}}}\uc2  are demanding employers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://californiafastfoodworkersunion.org/wp-content/uploads/FastFoodConstitutionalPledge.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sign}}}\uc2  a pledge reaffirming workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ice-us-immigration-and-customs-enforcement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement}}}\uc2  (ICE) raids at workplaces across the US.\par \par \uc2 The California Fast Food Workers Union, an affiliate of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , drafted a Constitutional Pledge to California Workers\u8217\'20\'19 Rights for workplaces to sign that affirms a commitment to protecting workers and \u8220\'20\'1Ckeep ICE from going where they are not allowed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Sl9Lu9-yk7r6WF3T69LixDpRZBAvamla/view"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pledge}}}\uc2  includes a list of actions that would protect vulnerable workers, such as making sure workers have access to private, protected areas in the workplace and ensuring that enforcement officers aren\u8217\'20\'19t entering these spaces without a judicial warrant.\par \par \uc2 In San Jose, workers at a \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s franchise {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/p/DUcWJFXFJvh/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 walked out}}}\uc2  earlier this month after management declined to affirm they would help employees if ICE showed up, telling workers to instead hide in their cars should enforcement arrive.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMcDonald\u8217\'20\'19s has the legal right to deny [immigration enforcement officers] entry to private areas of the store if they don\u8217\'20\'19t present a warrant signed by a federal judge,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote the California Fast Food Workers Union in response to the strike, adding that other \u8220\'20\'1Cbusinesses have put up signs as well as trained their staff to enforce that right\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Candida Masin, a McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s worker in Los Angeles, told the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  that as immigration enforcement ramped up over last summer, co-workers started calling out of work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey say that they are not going to come to work because ICE is close and they are afraid to leave their home. That impacts us because we have to take on their workload,\u8221\'20\'1D Masin said, adding that it has also affected the number of customers visiting her store. \u8220\'20\'1CICE agents were raiding areas just two blocks from where I was and I saw my co-workers crying, I saw customers that were really scared. I had to take on double duty to help calm their nerves because I could see how this fear really impacts people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Masin said she and other {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/food/fast-food"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fast food}}}\uc2  workers have visited dozens of different franchise locations to push them to sign on to and post the pledge, which outlines how workplaces can keep \u8220\'20\'1CICE and other federal agents from going where they are legally not allowed, protect workers\u8217\'20\'19 privacy and ensure workers\u8217\'20\'19 safety\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Though the immigration enforcement operations in Los Angeles have calmed since last summer, ICE continues to conduct raids and arrests across the city, which is home to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lapublicpress.org/2026/02/la-ice-valley-hot-spot/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated 3.5 million immigrants}}}\uc2 . Of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://californiafastfoodworkersunion.org/wp-content/uploads/Immigration-and-labor-rights-in-fast-food.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 630,000 fast food workers}}}\uc2  in California, over a quarter are immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Fast food workers in Los Angeles and Santa Clarita counties in California have also been pushing for a fast food fair work ordinance, which would provide {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://siliconvalleyrising.org/fast-food-fair-work-ordinance/#:~:text=WHAT%20WORKERS%20WIN%20WITH%20THE,approximately%208%20days%20per%20year."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid trainings}}}\uc2  for workers to learn about their rights on the job.\par \par \uc2 Masin argued it\u8217\'20\'19s important for employers to educate workers and affirm their rights in the workplace to mitigate the anxiety they have when showing up to work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fear is there and if people don\u8217\'20\'19t know their rights, the fear will always be there in their minds,\u8221\'20\'1D added Masin. \u8220\'20\'1CIt helps when you know your rights. If you don\u8217\'20\'19t, then you won\u8217\'20\'19t feel a difference in your workplace.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s and the \uc2 California Restaurant Association\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s ramped up immigration enforcement in California has resulted in a downturn in private sector jobs throughout the state and economic losses for businesses.\par \par \uc2 Following Trump\u8217\'20\'19s deployment of the national guard to Los Angeles last summer, private sector jobs recorded a 5% decline in June and July, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://clc.ucmerced.edu/sites/g/files/ufvvjh626/f/page/documents/effects_of_federal_immigration_enforcement_feb.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent study}}}\uc2  by the University of California Merced\u8217\'20\'19s Community and Labor Center. A Los Angeles county {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2026-02-13/dhs-sweeps-county-businesses-report-losses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  found that 82% of local businesses reported negative impacts from the immigration raids and 44% reported losses of greater than half their normal revenue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorking people across Los Angeles are demanding global fast food employers sign the constitutional pledge to declare they are choosing courage over silence and dignity over fear,\u8221\'20\'1D said David Green, president and executive director of SEIU Local 721 in Los Angeles. \u8220\'20\'1CFast-food companies must stand with cooks and cashiers in this moment, and do their part in building workplaces and communities where all of us can thrive together, stronger and unafraid.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260227em2r003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
