\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democrats in four states seek to bar ICE employees from future civil service jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein \par \uc2 1,161 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2026\par 18:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bills aim to make ICE employees ineligible for jobs in law enforcement, public education and state civil serviceSign up for the Breaking News US email to get newsletter alerts in your inbox\par \par \uc2 Supercharged by billions in dollars from Congress, \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)\uc2  has hired thousands of new officers to carry out \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s mass deportation campaign in an effort it has likened to \u8220\'20\'1C{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/31/ice-recruitment-media-campaign"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wartime recruitment}}}\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. In several states, Democratic lawmakers want applicants to think twice about taking part.\par \par \uc2 Bills introduced in recent weeks in the legislatures of at least four Democratic-led states would impose long-term consequences on new ICE employees by rendering them ineligible for jobs in law enforcement, public education, and, in their most expansive form, the entire state civil service.\par \par \uc2 None of the proposals has been signed into law, and potential legislation may face legal challenges. The bills nonetheless underscore Democratic state lawmaker\u8217\'20\'19s determination to undermine Trump\u8217\'20\'19s hardline immigration policy, even as a similar effort in Congress that has resulted in the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  (DHS) shutting down faces uncertain prospects.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re an ICE agent, you\u8217\'20\'19re signing up to engage in unlawful conduct. You\u8217\'20\'19re signing up to engage in racially profiling Latino communities. You\u8217\'20\'19re signing up to engage in illegal detentions and deportations of people who have legal rights in this country, you\u8217\'20\'19re signing up for the separation of families and children,\u8221\'20\'1D Democratic New Jersey assemblyman Ravi Bhalla told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in February, he introduced legislation that would effectively bar from state and local government employment anyone who joined ICE between September 2025 and the expected final day of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s term in 2029.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you make that decision, there will be consequences if you seek employment in New Jersey,\u8221\'20\'1D Bhalla said.\par \par \uc2 DHS spokesperson \uc2 Tricia McLaughlin\uc2  accused the lawmakers proposing these bills of \u8220\'20\'1Cfalsely casting federal law enforcement as villains while ICE officers are being targeted, threatened, and doxxed simply for doing their jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo most Americans, ICE officers are heroes,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 After Trump returned to the White House last year, Congress\u8217\'20\'19s Republican leaders moved quickly to provide ICE and other federal agencies money to make good on the president\u8217\'20\'19s campaign promise of deporting all undocumented immigrants.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/may/22/what-is-trump-big-beautiful-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The One Big Beautiful Bill Act}}}\uc2  approved by the GOP along party lines allocated nearly $30bn to hire and train new ICE agents, and the agency embarked on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/30/ice-hiring-incentives-signing-bonuses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a hiring spree}}}\uc2  that made {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/03/trump-memes-immigration-deportation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 liberal use}}}\uc2  of patriotic and, at times, xenophobic, slogans as well as incentives like signing bonuses as high as $50,000.\par \par \uc2 In January, the DHS announced the agency\u8217\'20\'19s headcount of officers and agents had more than doubled, reaching 22,000. But ICE\u8217\'20\'19s aggressive tactics appear to have become a political liability for Trump, whose approval ratings on immigration have recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/11/trump-immigration-support-minneapolis-shootings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slumped}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A key driver of that collapse came after an ICE agent shot Renee Good in Minneapolis last month, and two officers from border patrol and Customs and Border Protection opened fire on Alex Pretti weeks later. Democrats in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  responded to their deaths by blocking passage of legislation funding DHS \u8211\'20\'13 which oversees those agencies \u8211\'20\'13 until Congress\u8217\'20\'19s Republican majority agrees to demands including a ban on officers wearing masks, a requirement they seek judicial warrants before entering private property and an end to their practice of stopping people they suspect of being undocumented on the street.\par \par \uc2 GOP leaders have rejected several of their conditions, leading to the DHS {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/13/us-homeland-security-department-shutdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shutting down}}}\uc2  after its spending authorization expired. Talks between the two parties remain ongoing, but the deportation push has continued with funding from the One Big Beautiful Bill act.\par \par \uc2 Though their laws apply only within their borders, states where Democrats control the governor\u8217\'20\'19s office and the legislature have sought to circumvent the congressional deadlock with new measures to address what lawmakers characterize as ICE\u8217\'20\'19s brutal and indiscriminate tactics. The New Jersey governor, \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2 , this month signed an executive order {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/12/new-jersey-immigration-ice-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning the agents}}}\uc2  from some state properties, and California lawmakers last year approved rules against federal agents wearing masks, though the law was recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/09/judge-california-ice-masks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked}}}\uc2  by a federal judge.\par \par \uc2 In Maryland, where Democratic governor \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2  this month signed a law prohibiting local law enforcement from being deputized for federal immigration enforcement operations, delegate Adrian Boafo has introduced the ICE Breaker Act, which would prevent any state police agency from hiring people who took jobs with ICE after 20 January 2025 \u8211\'20\'13 the date Trump began his second term.\par \par \uc2 The measure, which is similar to one proposed in Washington state, is necessary both because of ICE\u8217\'20\'19s tactics in Minneapolis and Trump\u8217\'20\'19s pardoning of January 6 defendants, which led to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jul/02/january-6-rioter-justice-department-jared-l-wise"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least one}}}\uc2  taking a job at the justice department, Boafo said. Adjacent to Washington DC, the state of Maryland is a frequent employer of former federal workers, and the delegate said he wants to state law enforcement is free of people involved in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s deportation push.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are, in the state of Maryland, blessed to have retired federal workers come work for us in the state and local governments,\u8221\'20\'1D Boafo said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re putting a marker in the ground. If you recruited under this administration, under these tactics, we don\u8217\'20\'19t want you to come ever work in Maryland state law enforcement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In California, Democratic assembly member Anamarie \u193\'00\'C1vila Far\u237\'00\'EDas has proposed the Melt Ice act, which would stop people who took jobs with ICE during Trump\u8217\'20\'19s second term from becoming school teachers or police officers in the most-populous state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI would think for any rational person, this should resonate to be a deterrent to join the ranks of ICE in this administration in its current form,\u8221\'20\'1D \u193\'00\'C1vila Far\u237\'00\'EDas said.\par \par \uc2 Tom Lackey, a Republican assembly member and former California highway patrol officer, said the Democratic proposal would amount to employment discrimination.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt punishes lawful employment, not misconduct, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it has any chance of being constitutionally supported,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m actually baffled by it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The lawmakers sponsoring such measures say they believe their bills can stand up in court, but several say they expect them to be amended as they work their way through their legislature.\par \par \uc2 Commenting on the Melt Ice act, Joseph Fishkin, a professor at the University of California, Los Angeles School of Law, said the proposal \u8220\'20\'1Cpresents some novel issues that no court has yet addressed\u8221\'20\'1D, though whether it can overcome federal supremacy over state law remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCalifornia lawmakers certainly know that. They may be proposing or even passing these laws in part to send a message that California as a state finds the actions of ICE abhorrent,\u8221\'20\'1D Fishkin said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPassing such a law does send that message loud and clear even if a court were to ultimately strike it down on federalism grounds.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260226em2q002uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
