\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 How the anxiety over AI could fuel a new workers\u8217\'20\'19 movement\b0\par\par\uc2 Samantha Oltman \par \uc2 1,167 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 February 2026\par 19:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 New technology has workers spooked, but experts say it\u8217\'20\'19s creating an opening for a resurgence in worker power\par \par \uc2 In 2026, it\u8217\'20\'19s a scary time to work for a living.\par \par \uc2 Gone are the days of quiet quitting, the Great Resignation, and the highly visible union-organizing battles that began the decade and signaled that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/recode/22841490/work-remote-wages-labor-force-participation-great-resignation-unions-quits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 perhaps worker power was on the rise}}}\uc2  again in the US. Instead, much of that momentum is being crowded out of our minds by anxieties: a worsening affordability crisis, geopolitical instability and the specter of artificial intelligence looming over the workplace.\par \par \uc2 For the tech CEOs leading the AI race and enriching themselves as they jostle for dominance, AI isn\u8217\'20\'19t a phantasm at all, but a glimmering unicorn. When they predict AI is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/anthropic-ceo-predicts-ai-models-233113047.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just months away}}}\uc2  from being able to do everything a software engineer does, or that it will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/ais-next-challenge-take-the-ceos-job-e9e2fe98?mod=ai_more_article_pos2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one day}}}\uc2  take over CEOs\u8217\'20\'19 jobs, their excitement for the future is palpable. For the rest of us, it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to feel confident in their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9WkGNe27r_Q"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 offhand remarks}}}\uc2  about how \u8220\'20\'1Csome jobs will be obsolete, but many jobs will be created\u8221\'20\'1D. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2025/04/03/how-the-us-public-and-ai-experts-view-artificial-intelligence/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2025 Pew survey}}}\uc2  found that \u8220\'20\'1C64% of the public thinks AI will lead to fewer jobs over the next 20 years\u8221\'20\'1D, which is probably why only 17% of Americans say AI will have a positive effect on the US over the same time period.\par \par \uc2 Uncertain times like these call for scrutiny. Throughout 2026, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  will publish {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/series/reworked"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reworked, a reporting series that centers the human stakes as AI disrupts our workplaces}}}\uc2 , in ways both thrilling and alarming. Like this essay, the stories in this series will focus on workers\u8217\'20\'19 real-world power and plights as well as the realities and exaggerations of the hype surrounding AI\u8217\'20\'19s transformative possibilities.\par \par \uc2 So which version of the future of work awaits us? It is yet to be settled, which means there is still time to shift course.\par \par \uc2 Dissolving divisions\par \par \uc2 Blue-collar workers, who have long grappled with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/10242589221141055"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 algorithmic surveillance and optimization}}}\uc2  at work, are now worrying that technological advancements will only make their jobs more dehumanizing. \u8220\'20\'1C[For] lower-wage workers, there is concern about being replaced by robots. But on the other hand, there\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of concern about being turned into robots,\u8221\'20\'1D Lisa Kresge, a senior researcher at the UC Berkeley Labor Center, told me.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/ng-interactive/2026/feb/17/ai-startups-work-culture-san-francisco"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12-hour days, no weekends: the anxiety driving AI\u8217\'20\'19s brutal work culture is a warning for all of us}}}\par \par \uc2 And white-collar workers are now wondering if their work will begin to resemble blue-collar labor \u8211\'20\'13 either because they will be similarly tracked and managed, or because they will need to switch to more manual work that\u8217\'20\'19s resistant to being taken over by AI.\par \par \uc2 It may seem that workers haven\u8217\'20\'19t been this vulnerable in a long time. In some ways, that\u8217\'20\'19s true. But this is also a pivotal moment, one in which something unexpected is happening: society\u8217\'20\'19s collective anxiety over AI is catalyzing workers to push back.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is creating an opportunity,\u8221\'20\'1D Sarita Gupta, the \uc2 Ford Foundation\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s vice-president of US programs and co-author of The Future We Need: Organizing for a Better Democracy in the Twenty-First Century, told me. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you have a young Silicon Valley software engineer {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://doctorow.medium.com/https-pluralistic-net-2025-03-13-electronic-whipping-youre-next-f9e462ccc141"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 realize}}}\uc2  that their performance is tracked or undermined by the same logic as a working-class warehouse picker, class divisions dissolve, and larger working-class movements for dignity are possible. That is what we\u8217\'20\'19re starting to see.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 People across industries and income brackets are anxious and frustrated, quite like they were when the Covid pandemic placed punishing demands on frontline workers and erased the boundaries between work and life for everyone else. Those struggles prompted power shifts: at the same time that workers led unionization efforts at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouses and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  locations around the US, the Great Resignation saw a record number of employees quit their jobs, and the ones who remained in the workforce began negotiating for and gaining better pay and conditions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was not a pretty time for a lot of workers. And so part of the resurgence of labor organizing from that period of time was in response to a lot of fears,\u8221\'20\'1D Kresge said.\par \par \uc2 She also sees the rise of AI as an opening for the labor movement to regain some of the power it has lost after decades of attacks from employers. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m hopeful about the opportunity for technology to lift up some of the issues that have been under way in our economy for decades \u8230\'20\'26 in terms of how workers are treated and how we are distributing the rewards of productivity,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Perceptions of power\par \par \uc2 Conditions for workers have been rough for a long while now. \u8220\'20\'1COver time, unions have lost collective bargaining power, and a lot of that is due to the lack of laws that we need and enforcement of laws,\u8221\'20\'1D Gupta said. \u8220\'20\'1CFor four decades, productivity soared while wages stayed flat, and unionization hit historic lows.\u8221\'20\'1D In 2025, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/union2.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only 9.9% of US workers were union members}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the same percentage as 2024, but still the lowest numbers in almost 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/ng-interactive/2026/feb/18/ai-four-day-workweek"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The bogus four-day workweek that AI supposedly \u8216\'20\'18frees up\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Today, the advent of AI is drawing the world\u8217\'20\'19s attention to the extreme imbalance of power between employers and their employees \u8211\'20\'13 and people are getting worked up. Even if the outcomes are still undetermined, that\u8217\'20\'19s a glimmer of possibility in bleak times.\par \par \uc2 AI is still a nascent technology. Many of the predictions about what it will be capable of and how it will transform labor and the economy are just that \u8211\'20\'13 predictions. The question of worker power in the age of AI hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been decided yet, even if billionaire CEOs with a vested interest in the unregulated dominance of AI keep implying that it has.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a concerted effort among many tech leaders to basically create mystification around AI as a tactic, to a large extent, to disempower workers, policymakers, and anyone who might be critical of the growing concentration of funding and resources in our society toward this goal,\u8221\'20\'1D Kresge told me.\par \par \uc2 In other words, take what these billionaires say with a grain of salt. The rise of AI is already transforming society, the economy and our relationship to work, but a lot of these shifts are anticipatory, based on our belief in the potential of a technology that\u8217\'20\'19s still being built.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to always remind ourselves that the direction of technology is a choice, right? We can use AI to build a surveillance economy that squeezes every drop of value out of a worker, or we can use it to build an era of shared prosperity,\u8221\'20\'1D Gupta said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know if technology were designed and deployed and governed by the people doing the work, AI wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be such a threat.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260219em2j003ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
