\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s immigration siege is rattling hospitality industry, workers say\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 803 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2026\par 20:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest hospitality workers\u8217\'20\'19 union, says ICE crackdown is harming tourism and costing jobs\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s immigration policies are having a chilling effect on the hospitality industry, where nearly a third of workers are immigrants, according to the largest hospitality union in the US.\par \par \uc2 The number of employed hospitality workers dropped by 98,000 from December 2024 to December 2025, according to a report from \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , which represents 300,000 workers across the hospitality, food and tourism industries in the US and Canada.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders say the Trump administration\u8217\'20\'19s brutal immigration crackdown has not only scared workers but has also discouraged international tourism. The US saw a decline of $1.2bn, or a 5.5% drop, in tourism revenue from September 2024 to September 2025, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need immigrant workers. They\u8217\'20\'19re an important part of our workforce. They\u8217\'20\'19re my members, they\u8217\'20\'19re my neighbors, and the way they\u8217\'20\'19ve been treated in this time is really abhorrent,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wade L\u252\'00\'FCneburg, political director for \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 17 in Minneapolis, which represents 6,000 workers in Minnesota, including workers at the Minneapolis-St Paul international airport.\par \par \uc2 Because of the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s immigration crackdown, \u8220\'20\'1Cmany of our members have been afraid to go to work,\u8221\'20\'1D L\u252\'00\'FCneburg said. Sixteen airport workers were detained by immigration enforcement earlier this year, L\u252\'00\'FCneburg said. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are workers that are authorized to work and passed TSA background checks,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  (DHS) and the White House did not comment on these members being taken by \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even as Minnesota seeks to recover from Operation Metro Surge, Trump\u8217\'20\'19s immigration policies \u8211\'20\'13 from cancelling temporary protected status, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cfr.org/articles/guide-countries-trumps-travel-ban-list"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expanding }}}\uc2 travel visa bans, to having {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/aug/25/tourists-foreign-visitors-trump-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tourists detained}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 have driven a slump in tourism across the US.\par \par \uc2 International visitors to the US declined by 2.5 million in 2025, even as international tourism increased around the world, according to \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s report.\par \par \uc2 L\u252\'00\'FCneburg said regional declines of Canadian tourism to Minnesota drove a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wjon.com/minnesota-travel-decline-2025/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 15% decline}}}\uc2  in overall international air travelers to Minnesota in 2025 and a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcbmag.com/why-canadian-fan-attendance-dropped-at-the-2026-world-juniors-in-minnesota/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 plunge}}}\uc2  in Canadians attending the World Junior Hockey championships. Small businesses in Minneapolis reportedly lost up to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kstp.com/kstp-news/top-news/how-minneapolis-is-tallying-the-cost-of-ice-report-says-small-businesses-lost-up-to-81m-in-january/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $81m}}}\uc2  in revenue in January 2026.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe economic ramifications of what is happening are much further-reaching than just to immigrants and their families, as horrific as that is,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gwen Mills, president of \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , during a panel introducing the report last week. \u8220\'20\'1CThe scenes of violence on the streets in our major cities, the anti-immigrant rhetoric, the fearmongering, it\u8217\'20\'19s discouraging domestic travel, it\u8217\'20\'19s discouraging foreign visitors from coming to the United States.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The slowdown is being felt in other cities. In 2025, a record number of restaurants {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wtop.com/dc/2026/01/the-head-of-the-dc-restaurant-association-has-a-high-level-of-concern-for-city-eateries-in-2026/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 closed}}}\uc2  in Washington DC while restaurant openings slowed by 30%. Meanwhile, tourism in Las Vegas dropped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news3lv.com/news/local/las-vegas-tourism-falls-75-percent-2025-december-visitation-lvcva-convention-visitors-economy-policy-hotels-casinos-gaming"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 7.5%}}}\uc2  in 2025.\par \par \uc2 Shaleah Taylor, a guest room attendant in Las Vegas, said in the report that \u8220\'20\'1Cyou can feel that business has slowed down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are spending less, and some of my co-workers who depend on tips are seeing a real drop,\u8221\'20\'1D Taylor said.\par \par \uc2 Moana Molly, a hotel housekeeper in Atlantic City, New Jersey, said during {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/watch/live/?ref=watch_permalink&v=868277876030329"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the panel}}}\uc2  that several co-workers left due to the immigration crackdown, increasing the workload for herself and the remaining workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t have the staffing that we need and nobody\u8217\'20\'19s applying,\u8221\'20\'1D said Molly.\par \par \uc2 A July 2025 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/trumps-deportation-agenda-will-destroy-millions-of-jobs-both-immigrants-and-u-s-born-workers-would-suffer-job-losses-particularly-in-construction-and-child-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  found Trump\u8217\'20\'19s deportation goals of 4 million people would result in job losses of 3.3 million for immigrants and 2.6 million for US-born workers.\par \par \uc2 But the policies are creating fear among US citizens, too. Greg Barney, a line cook at St Anselm in Washington DC, said there was a significant drop-off in visitors to the restaurant.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI wasn\u8217\'20\'19t afraid about being scooped up myself, but a lot of my close friends were, and everyone, all my co-workers, regardless of whether they were born here or not, could feel that fear and that tension, and it was hard on all of us,\u8221\'20\'1D Barney said during the panel.\par \par \uc2 Tricia McLaughlin\uc2 , the DHS spokesperson, told the Guardian that \u8220\'20\'1Cif there was any correlation between rampant illegal immigration and a good economy, Biden would have had a booming economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRemoving these criminals from the streets makes communities safer and more welcoming for business owners, customers, and for tourists,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 In an email to the Guardian, Abigail Jackson, a White House spokesperson, cited an article that pointed to a 8.9% youth unemployment rate in 2024, saying \u8220\'20\'1Cthere is no shortage of American minds and hands to grow our labor force.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260218em2i0060p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
