\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US union membership soared to 16-year high in 2025 despite Trump assault\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 438 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 February 2026\par 16:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union coverage slightly increased last year even as White House tried to eliminate contracts for thousands of workers\par \par \uc2 The number of workers covered under union contracts increased to a 16-year high in 2025, despite ongoing attempts by the Trump administration to wipe out collective bargaining agreements for tens of thousands of federal workers, according to new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/union2.htm#:~:text=Highlights%20from%20the%202024%20data,(See%20table%205.)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data}}}\uc2  from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 About {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/union2.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 16.5 million}}}\uc2  workers were covered by a union contract in 2025, up from{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seafarers.org/bls-issues-annual-union-density-report/#:~:text=The%20union%20membership%20rate%20in,related%20occupations%20(2.7%20percent)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  16 million}}}\uc2  in 2024 and the highest level since 2009. The increase stems from workers joining unions as members \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/union2.htm#:~:text=Highlights%20from%20the%202024%20data,(See%20table%205.)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14.7 million }}}\uc2 US workers were union members in 2025, up from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seafarers.org/bls-issues-annual-union-density-report/#:~:text=The%20union%20membership%20rate%20in,related%20occupations%20(2.7%20percent)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 14.2 million}}}\uc2  workers in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The percentage of all workers in the US covered by a union contracts ticked up to 11.2% in 2025, compared with{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seafarers.org/bls-issues-annual-union-density-report/#:~:text=The%20union%20membership%20rate%20in,related%20occupations%20(2.7%20percent)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  11.1%}}}\uc2  in 2024. Union membership increased from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seafarers.org/bls-issues-annual-union-density-report/#:~:text=The%20union%20membership%20rate%20in,related%20occupations%20(2.7%20percent)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.9%}}}\uc2  in 2024 to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/union2.htm#:~:text=Highlights%20from%20the%202024%20data,(See%20table%205.)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10% }}}\uc2 in 2025.\par \par \uc2 Union density in the US has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R47596#:~:text=Figure%202%20shows%20the%20total,also%20generally%20increased%20until%201979."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined }}}\uc2 drastically in recent decades from above 30% in the late 1940s and 50s. Despite the decline, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/694472/labor-union-approval-relatively-steady.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public approval }}}\uc2 of labor unions has increased in recent years. Approval of unions now ranges between 67% and 71%, according to Gallup, levels last reached in the late 1950s and early 1960s.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe second Trump administration launched the most vicious attack on public sector union workers since the Reagan era. While the assault\u8217\'20\'19s full effect remains to be seen, the numbers from 2025 leave room for cautious optimism,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hayley Brown, a researcher at the Center for Economic and Policy Research (CEPR), in a statement on the union density increase.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is pushing to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.govexec.com/workforce/2026/02/opm-instructs-agencies-terminate-union-contracts-violation-court-orders/411441/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cancel}}}\uc2  collective bargaining agreements for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americanprogress.org/article/the-trump-administration-ended-collective-bargaining-for-1-million-federal-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 1 million }}}\uc2 federal workers, which would offset these slight gains.\par \par \uc2 Heidi Shierholz\uc2 , president of the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  and former chief economist off the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2 , noted the increase of 463,000 workers represented by a union is the highest number in the US in 16 years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an extremely welcome departure from prior years\u8217\'20\'19 declines,\u8221\'20\'1D Shierholz {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bsky.app/profile/hshierholz.bsky.social/post/3mf5ea5bw6s25"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted }}}\uc2 on Bluesky. \u8220\'20\'1CIn a time of fear, uncertainty, and hardship, workers are realizing they are better off in a union. Workers are exerting control over their jobs and their lives through unionization. This is especially clear in the federal government, where unionization surged despite unprecedented attacks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She cited the increases in 2025 to \u8220\'20\'1Cyears of sustained effort. Organizing and winning union elections is a lengthy process, and the groundwork laid over many years definitely contributed to this year\u8217\'20\'19s gains.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260218em2i004bl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
