\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 More than 600 people were fired or punished for posting about Charlie Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s death. They want justice\b0\par\par\uc2 John Semley \par \uc2 3,101 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2026\par 14:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Acolytes of the far-right activist urged employers to fire his critics. Now those who were terminated are suing and claiming their right to free speech\par \par \uc2 Julie Strebe, a 55-year-old sheriff\u8217\'20\'19s deputy in the small Bible belt town of Salem, Missouri, was on a date with her husband at a \uc2 Buffalo Wild Wings\uc2  when her husband slid his phone across the table. On \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , people were demanding Strebe\u8217\'20\'19s immediate termination, calling her a \u8220\'20\'1Cwacko\u8221\'20\'1D with \u8220\'20\'1Cextreme mental health issues\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It was the afternoon of 13 September 2025, just a few days after Charlie Kirk had been killed by a sniper\u8217\'20\'19s bullet on a college campus. Shortly after his assassination, Strebe had posted on her personal \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page: \u8220\'20\'1CEmpathy is not owed to oppressors.\u8221\'20\'1D In comments underneath, she did not mince words. She called Kirk a racist, a sexist, an antisemite and the kind of person who wants to see gay people, like her own son, stoned to death. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t feel bad,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, months later, speaking from her home. \u8220\'20\'1CI refuse to feel bad for this man, and the hateful things he stood for.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2025/sep/27/trump-free-speech-charlie-kirk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Free speech for me, not for thee\u8217\'20\'19: how Trump\u8217\'20\'19s censorship blitz is splitting the right}}}\par \par \uc2 When she heard people were calling for her to be fired, Strebe told her superiors that she would take her offending posts down. But it was too late. Her posts had escaped containment. On \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and in phone calls to her workplace, she was called a lunatic with a badge and gun or a \u8220\'20\'1Ccorrupt cop\u8221\'20\'1D, who couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be trusted to execute her duties as law enforcement. Some locals apparently worried that if Strebe pulled them over for a routine traffic stop, she might fire her weapon at them if they were wearing a Maga hat.\par \par \uc2 People from her home county of Dent, which voted overwhelmingly to re-elect Donald Trump in the 2024 election, used homophobic slurs against her son online. Her husband\u8217\'20\'19s woodworking business was targeted too, as was the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page for his charitable side gig, where he dresses up as the Grinch and visits children\u8217\'20\'19s hospitals over the holidays.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been a cop for 19 years,\u8221\'20\'1D Strebe says. \u8220\'20\'1CI believe that everybody should be treated fairly. And that\u8217\'20\'19s what I\u8217\'20\'19ve done my entire career. And this one statement was completely just twisted. It\u8217\'20\'19s very frustrating.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Strebe is one of a group of Americans who endured similar ordeals after Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s death. Some were called out for their unkind, even giddy, social media reactions to news of the far-right activist\u8217\'20\'19s assassination. Others were harangued for merely quoting Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s own words \u8211\'20\'13 particularly his comments about how a certain number of gun deaths are an acceptable cost to maintain the gun rights vouchsafed by the second amendment. A website, titled Charlie Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s Murderers, collated the names and personal information of such alleged offenders. The site has since been decommissioned \u8211\'20\'13 but it aided Kirk acolytes in mounting complaints to employers against those they deemed to be not sufficiently reverential.\par \par \uc2 By November of 2025, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/investigations/charlie-kirk-purge-how-600-americans-were-punished-pro-trump-crackdown-2025-11-19/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Reuters investigation}}}\uc2  estimated that 600 people had been terminated, disciplined, investigated, suspended or otherwise admonished for their Kirk posts, likening the reaction to an ideological purge.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s how the Strebes felt when they started to notice people lurking outside their house. They set up security cameras on the perimeter. A large truck parked on their block with a dinky, Sharpie-on-cardboard taped to its side that read: \u8220\'20\'1CJulie Strebe Supports the Assassination of Charles Kirk.\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CHe could have done a much better sign,\u8221\'20\'1D laughs Strebe, as she prepares to pack up and move out of the town where she has made a life for 20 years. \u8220\'20\'1CIt looks like a third-grader wrote it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Strebe was suspended, then fired. According to Strebe, superiors claimed that because she made her posts on the clock and did not disclaim they were her personal opinions, there were grounds for termination. She also says that her bosses argued she had a history of making controversial social media posts while serving the public, but the only previous example they were able to cite was a 10-year-old post about shoddy local roadwork causing flat tires. When approached for comment, the Dent county sheriff\u8217\'20\'19s office told the Guardian that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe are prohibited by law from disclosing any information regarding that former deputy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBasically they screwed me because of the mob mentality,\u8221\'20\'1D Strebe says. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is everything I\u8217\'20\'19ve ever done in my adult life. And they took it from me \u8230\'20\'26 I could have retired in six and a half years. Full pension. Can\u8217\'20\'19t do that now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Demands for the termination and professional castigation of Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s critics, people like Strebe, were amplified by some of the biggest megaphones in the United States. When JD Vance guest-hosted an episode of Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s podcast in the week after his death, the vice-president told listeners: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you see someone celebrating Charlie\u8217\'20\'19s murder, call them out,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/shorts/zguCSuxaA0k"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vance said}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd, hell, call their employer \u8230\'20\'26 Get involved! It\u8217\'20\'19s the best way to honor Charlie\u8217\'20\'19s legacy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2025/sep/20/charlie-kirk-debate-style"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The students who debated with Charlie Kirk: \u8216\'20\'18His goal was to verbally defeat us\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 For years, Kirk had cast himself as a roving tribune of free expression, touring college campuses to provoke arguments with liberal students and faculty, and warning that political correctness was strangling the first amendment. To civil liberties lawyers, the wave of firings and suspensions following his death became an opportunity to test those principles. Many believed they could help some of the fired get their jobs back.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The Philadelphia-based lawyer Greg Greubel was one of those people who felt Charlie Kirk was upholding the principle of free speech. As a senior attorney working with the Foundation for Individual Rights and Expression (Fire), Greubel had helped Turning Point USA, the conservative, Christian non-profit co-founded by Kirk, set up some of their college chapters. When he heard about Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s shooting, he was devastated. Kirk, to his mind, was an advocate of free expression who had been violently gunned down while exercising his first amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 But it didn\u8217\'20\'19t take long for Greubel to spot retribution for those that didn\u8217\'20\'19t share his view. \u8220\'20\'1CThe fallout from this was easy to predict, unfortunately,\u8221\'20\'1D Greubel recalls, speaking from Fire\u8217\'20\'19s Philadelphia office. \u8220\'20\'1CWithin 24 hours we started seeing calls for terminations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Greubel and other Fire members immediately sprang into action. Greubel\u8217\'20\'19s expertise is the free speech of public employees. He set out to find a clear, obvious example that would demonstrate how many of these employees were unfairly targeted for expressing themselves. He and his colleagues pored over cases of people who had been targeted, and even fired, for social media reactions to Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s death, scanning social media and following tips submitted to Fire\u8217\'20\'19s website. Before long, they had reviewed more than 200 cases. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that number just kept going up,\u8221\'20\'1D Greubel says.\par \par \uc2 He thought Monica Meeks of Clarksville, Tennessee, might prove a compelling test case. She was fired from the Tennessee department of commerce and insurance on 12 September for calling Kirk a \u8220\'20\'1CWhite Supremacist\u8221\'20\'1D in a comment responding to a friend\u8217\'20\'19s Facebook post. In a statement published online, her employer claimed that Meeks \u8220\'20\'1Crevealed bias and disregard toward the very people she was tasked with serving\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A disabled 20-year veteran who served honorably in the United States army and longtime public employee, Meeks struck Greubel as especially sympathetic. \u8220\'20\'1CShe has an impeccable record,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CShe is really dedicated to public service. And for someone to get fired that quickly, it was such a disgrace.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In December, Greubel filed a lawsuit for wrongful termination on behalf of Meeks, seeking reinstatement and damages. (Kevin Walters, communications director at the Tennessee department of commerce and insurance, told the Guardian that the office does not comment about ongoing litigation.) Greubel is optimistic. He points to a settlement from January, when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/08/tennessee-university-professor-reinstated-charlie-kirk-post"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s Austin Peay State University reinstated a professor}}}\uc2 , Darren Michael, who was fired for a social media post, and paid him a $500,000 settlement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was heartened to see the outcome of that case,\u8221\'20\'1D says Jack Cohoon, a South Carolina attorney defending another client fired in the post-Kirk purge. \u8220\'20\'1CIt demonstrates that there is a price to be paid for depriving someone of their first amendment rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cohoon is representing Lauren Vaughn, a South Carolina teachers\u8217\'20\'19 aide at an elementary school who was sacked for social media posts about Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s death. Vaughn\u8217\'20\'19s case is unusual in that she offered precious little actual critical commentary about Kirk, instead repeating Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s own words about gun violence on her personal Facebook account. According to the lawsuit, her post read, in part:\par \par \uc2 In her termination letter, River Ridge elementary school claimed that Vaughan had violated an internal social media policy, which held that all employees \u8220\'20\'1Cmust be respectful and professional in all communications\u8221\'20\'1D. Her legal team disagrees. They are suing on the basis that the termination and the social media guidelines are unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 In response to a Freedom of Information Act (Foia) request from Vaughan\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers, asking the district to produce \u8220\'20\'1CAll complaints, received from any source, regarding Lauren Vaughn from September 10, 2025 to the present\u8221\'20\'1D, the school district revealed that it did not actually possess \u8220\'20\'1Cany written complaints that would be responsive to this request\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey were anticipating harassment in advance,\u8221\'20\'1D says Cohoon of his client\u8217\'20\'19s school district. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a sacrifice to the wolves.\u8221\'20\'1D The district did not respond to the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Fire lawyer Greubel says that often employers were responding to anonymous social media complaints. He says that one of the people that commented on the Monica Meeks case, in a tweet to her employer, had the screen name \u8220\'20\'1CBonerville Asskicker\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Bonerville Asskickers of the world are not there with her at work. They\u8217\'20\'19re not her clients. They\u8217\'20\'19re no one.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite this anonymity of complainants, or no complainants at all, the number of people fired in similar circumstances is legion. A staffer at the University of Mississippi was terminated for calling Kirk a \u8220\'20\'1Creimagined Klan member\u8221\'20\'1D. A University of California at Los Angeles faculty member {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2025-09-15/director-of-dei-at-ucla-on-leave-after-charlie-kirk-posts#:~:text=In%20what%20looked%20to%20be,mourn%20someone%20who%20hated%20us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was suspended}}}\uc2  for a social media post stating, in part, \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t force people to mourn someone who hated us.\u8221\'20\'1D The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/sep/11/msnbc-fires-matthew-dowd-charlie-kirk-shooting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 MSNBC political analyst Matthew Dowd lost his job}}}\uc2  after commenting on air: \u8220\'20\'1CHateful thoughts lead to hateful words, which then lead to hateful actions.\u8221\'20\'1D A Las Vegas realtor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nevadacurrent.com/2025/09/16/nevadans-dismissed-disciplined-amid-political-firestorm-following-kirks-death/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was fired by his company}}}\uc2  for saying Kirk was \u8220\'20\'1Can evil POS\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In October, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/trump-charlie-kirk-visas-revoked-455f43467c0c50e84d3857c1e9c81458"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Associated Press reported}}}\uc2  that half a dozen unidentified foreigners had US visas revoked for their post-Kirk assassination remarks. \u8220\'20\'1CAliens who take advantage of America\u8217\'20\'19s hospitality while celebrating the assassination of our citizens will be removed,\u8221\'20\'1D read a statement from the US state department.\par \par \uc2 Many who found themselves in these situations seem less inclined to muscle their way back to their previous employers. One person, who was fired from a full-time job in the private sector after posting a joke about Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s assassination on a personal Instagram account, told the Guardian they had no intention of pursuing litigation. \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to resubject myself or my family to that trauma,\u8221\'20\'1D they explained, speaking on the condition of anonymity. \u8220\'20\'1CI also want to work in my industry once again and feel that a statement would do more harm than justice at this point.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a path back for employees affected by this,\u8221\'20\'1D says the lawyer Cohoon. \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s essential that some of these individuals fight it out in court. That\u8217\'20\'19s the only way the first amendment can be vindicated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Strebe, Vaughan and Meeks were full-time public employees, and thus subject to certain employment standards. Others who have lost work, or opportunity, for their comments can\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily rely on the same rights.\par \par \uc2 Writer Gretchen Felker-Martin proudly identifies as a \u8220\'20\'1Cdirty communist transexual\u8221\'20\'1D. The day of Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s shooting, she fired off a number of posts from her Bluesky account. She called Kirk a \u8220\'20\'1CNazi bitch\u8221\'20\'1D, and expressed mock sympathy for the assassin\u8217\'20\'19s fatal bullet.\par \par \uc2 She was soon barred from her Bluesky account. And she had a contract terminated with DC Comics, where she had been hired to write a series following the adventures of Red Hood, a lesser-known character in the Batman comics universe. \u8220\'20\'1CI believe their wording was that what I said was indefensible according to their moral code,\u8221\'20\'1D Felker-Martin says.\par \par \uc2 She is no stranger to this kind of online controversy. Her 2022 debut novel Manhunt is set in an apocalyptic future where any person with too much testosterone is transformed into a cannibalistic beast, leaving cisgender women, non-binary people and trans people to fend for themselves. Some of the survivors are hunted by a militia of trans-exclusionary radical feminists (Terfs) and there\u8217\'20\'19s a scene where the gender-critical author JK Rowling is killed. The novel drew plenty of heat online, along with critical plaudits.\par \par \uc2 When Felker-Martin landed the Red Hood gig, she cautioned DC Comics brass that she risked bringing unwanted attention to the company due to her beliefs, identity and general predilection towards being outspoken about both. \u8220\'20\'1CI said I\u8217\'20\'19ve been a controversial figure online in the past, and probably will be again.\u8221\'20\'1D She says her editors didn\u8217\'20\'19t seem bothered \u8211\'20\'13 at first. When the Israeli advocacy group StopAntisemitism subsequently named Felker-Martin their \u8220\'20\'1Canti-Semite of the Week\u8221\'20\'1D, for her criticism of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s war on Gaza, she says her editors asked her to keep a low profile.\par \par \uc2 While she expressed sympathy for colleagues who may have lost work on the Red Hood comic as a result of her social media posts, Felker-Martin stands by her original remarks. \u8220\'20\'1CA very organized rightwing junta is currently killing and disappearing people en masse,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd for whatever reason, the liberal elite of America is still under the impression that they can just keep their heads down and be polite, and they can probably get through it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement made at the time of Felker-Martin\u8217\'20\'19s firing, DC Comics said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe tone of Gretchen\u8217\'20\'19s posts, not her personal views, was of concern for DC and that was clearly communicated to her on August 13. DC does not moderate the personal opinions of its talent, however, when personal statements are directly tied to DC stories or characters, or uses language that can be seen as non-peaceful, we review and act if necessary. Gretchen\u8217\'20\'19s choice to continue her rhetoric despite this feedback was her own. Our decision to cease publication was a result.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As a contractor with a private publishing company, Felker-Martin\u8217\'20\'19s first amendment privileges are much trickier to enforce, but she still accuses DC Comics of being gutless. \u8220\'20\'1CFrankly, I do think it\u8217\'20\'19s cowardice,\u8221\'20\'1D she explains.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Fire lawyer Greubel identifies a distinctive tit-for-tat culture at play in these firings. He remembers when conservatives and rightwingers came under fire for opinions expressed after the 2020 murder of George Floyd by the Minneapolis police officer Derek Chauvin. For many, he believes, Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s killing and the subsequent social media outcry offered an opportunity for these same people to exact a form of revenge. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a race-to-the-bottom element to all of this,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a real problem, writ large, in America.\u8221\'20\'1DMany people interviewed for this story express a fundamental exasperation with the perceived hypocrisy at play. In life, Charlie Kirk and his allies at Turning Point USA advocated for the sanctity of freedom of speech. At the same time, Turning Point curated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.professorwatchlist.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a public watchlist }}}\uc2 of so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cradical professors\u8221\'20\'1D who were accused of \u8220\'20\'1Cadvanc[ing] a radical agenda in our lecture halls\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe far right screams free-speech absolutism,\u8221\'20\'1D says historian Mark Bray, one of the alleged radical professors identified by Turning Point\u8217\'20\'19s watchlist. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd yet they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to shut down all kinds of speech.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/30/antifa-unmasking-ice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Antifa used to unmask neo-Nazis, now it\u8217\'20\'19s exposing ICE: \u8216\'20\'18Predators don\u8217\'20\'19t get anonymity\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Bray is a Rutgers University historian and author of the bestselling Antifa: The Anti-Fascist Handbook. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat happened to me had everything to do with Charlie Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s killing and the response to it,\u8221\'20\'1D Bray says. \u8220\'20\'1CAfter Kirk was killed, the boogeyman term du jour became \u8216\'20\'18antifa\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After Kirk\u8217\'20\'19s death,Trump issued an executive order declaring antifa, a decentralized leftwing political ideology, a \u8220\'20\'1Cdomestic terrorist organization\u8221\'20\'1D. Bray felt the pressure from rightwing activists including the Rutgers chapter of Turning Point USA. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.change.org/p/remove-antifa-financier-professor-mark-bray-from-rutgers-university"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A petition circulated}}}\uc2  calling for Bray\u8217\'20\'19s firing. (Neither Turning Point USA\u8217\'20\'19s national office nor its Rutgers University chapter responded to requests from the Guardian.)\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, 4 October, while watching a Yankees-Blue Jays game, he received one of many death threats. This one singled out his home address. Twenty-four hours later, he was making plans to relocate his family to Spain, where he now teaches remotely. As he says, \u8220\'20\'1Cliving in fear is not how I want to live\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Speaking over a cup of tea at a cafe on the edge of Madrid\u8217\'20\'19s El Retiro Park, Bray says that even though Kirk was a supposed free-speech advocate, one consistent feature of far-right political movements is that they rarely take their own hypocrisies seriously. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the hallmarks of fascism, from the beginning, has been denouncing rational consistency as bourgeois, or effeminate,\u8221\'20\'1D Bray says.\par \par \uc2 Despite these reprisals, even those who don\u8217\'20\'19t have a direct legal path back to employment are finding ways to make the best of their ordeals. Despite losing the DC Comics gig, Felker-Martin is optimistic about placing an original horror comic with another publisher who is more sympathetic to her political beliefs. Bray admits that exile has boosted his professional profile. His Anti-Fascist Handbook shot up bestseller lists.\par \par \uc2 Julie Strebe of Missouri is interviewing for new jobs in blue counties. She, too, is considering legal action. Not because she wants to be reinstated, or be paid damages. But because she believes she\u8217\'20\'19s right. \u8220\'20\'1CI was not even a deputy for the money,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CI made $20 an hour. It\u8217\'20\'19s the principle for me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260217em2h002pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
