\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Markets brace for US jobs report, with White House telling investors \u8216\'20\'18they shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t panic\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 business live\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Kollewe \par \uc2 695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2026\par 08:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump adviser \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  says \u8216\'20\'18we have to revise our expectations down\u8217\'20\'19 because of US deportation programme\par \par \uc2 8.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 London Stock Exchange Group\uc2  shares jump on reports of Elliott stake\par \par \uc2 Shares in the \uc2 London Stock Exchange Group\uc2  jumped 7% after it emerged that the activist investor \uc2 Elliott Management\uc2  has built a stake in the company and is engaging with the board to improve performance, according to a source cited by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 LSEG\uc2  shares are now up 1.5%, after losing more than a third of its value in the past 12 months, including a sell-off in global software stocks last week that wiped out nearly $1tn in combined value.\par \par \uc2 The data and analytics group, which also runs the \uc2 London Stock Exchange\uc2 , has been hit by concerns that rising competition and artificial intelligence tools will squeeze its income.\par \par \uc2 The source said Elliott, a New York-based hedge fund known for its attempts to shake up listed companies, has been in talks with \uc2 LSEG\uc2  to help engineer an improvement and urge it to consider a fresh share buyback, confirming an initial report by the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This marks a fresh campaign by the US investor after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/feb/10/bp-shares-jump-after-feared-us-hedge-fund-elliott-takes-stake"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it pressured BP }}}\uc2 to overhaul its strategy, tighten its operations and boost cashflow.\par \par \uc2 8.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 Heineken to cut up to 6,000 jobs\par \par \uc2 Heineken said it will cut up to 6,000 jobs globally and issued a lower estimate for profit growth in 2026 than last year, as the Dutch brewer and other companies grapple with weak demand for beer.\par \par \uc2 The world\u8217\'20\'19s second-biggest brewer by market value, which makes Heineken, Tiger and Amstel, outlined plans to reduce its global workforce by 5,000 to 6,000 over the next two years \u8211\'20\'13 almost 7% of its 87,000 headcount.\par \par \uc2 Beer sales across the industry have struggled amid stretched consumer finances, geopolitical turmoil and bad weather. In some countries, people are drinking less because of concerns over the health impact of alcohol, and the popularity of weight loss drugs like Wegovy and Mounjaro, which have led to changing diets and lifestyles.\par \par \uc2 Heineken\u8217\'20\'19s finance chief Harold van den Broek said:\par \par \uc2 Some of the job cuts will be in Europe and other non-priority markets that offer fewer growth prospects, he said, and some will come from previously announced measures targeting Heineken\u8217\'20\'19s supply network, head office and regional business divisions.\par \par \uc2 The company is looking for a new chief executive after the surprise resignation of Dolf van den Brink in January.\par \par \uc2 It is expecting slower profit growth of 2% to 6% this year, compared with the 4% to 8% it predicted in 2025. The brewer reported a better-than-expected rise of 4.4% in organic operating profits last year.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Markets brace for US jobs report, with White House telling investors \u8216\'20\'18they shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t panic\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payrolls day! The eagerly-awaited US jobs report is out today, and the White House has been trying to moderate expectations.\par \par \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 , senior counselor for trade and manufacturing to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , was speaking on Fox News last night.\par \par \uc2 When asked whether the number would be weak, he rowed back and said no, but stressed that investors need to expect smaller numbers in future.\par \par \uc2 [A person can\u8217\'20\'19t be illegal \u8211\'20\'13 see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2014/aug/24/open-door-should-we-use-term-illegal-immigrant"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .]\par \par \uc2 This comes after a warning from National Economic Council director Kevin Hassett on Monday. \u8220\'20\'1COne shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t panic,\u8221\'20\'1D he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/video/2026/02/09/watch-cnbcs-full-interview-with-white-house-national-economic-council-director-kevin-hassett.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told CNBC on Monday}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CYou should expect slightly smaller job numbers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The data release, delayed from last week, is expected to show the economy created 70,000 jobs in January, after 50,000 in December.\par \par \uc2 Derren Nathan, head of equity research at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 Economists at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 The Agenda\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm GMT: US non-farm payrolls for January (previous: 50,000; forecast: 70,000)\par \par \uc2 * 5.30pm GMT: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  policymaker James Talbot gives speech\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260211em2b0012x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
