\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 State-sponsored hackers targeting defence sector employees, \uc2 Google\uc2  says\b0\par\par\uc2 Aisha Down \par \uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 February 2026\par 07:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 26\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cyber-espionage campaigns are targeting employees directly, including through hiring processes, report claims\par \par \uc2 Defence companies, their hiring processes and their employees have become a key target of state-sponsored cyber-espionage campaigns, according to a report from \uc2 Google\uc2  released before the Munich Security Conference.\par \par \uc2 The report catalogues a \u8220\'20\'1Crelentless barrage of cyber operations\u8221\'20\'1D, most by state-sponsored groups, against EU and US industrial supply chains. It suggests the range of targets for these hackers has grown to encompass the broader industrial base of the US and Europe \u8211\'20\'13from German aerospace firms to UK carmakers.\par \par \uc2 State-linked hackers have long targeted the global defence industry, but Luke McNamara, an analyst for \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s threat intelligence group, said they had seen more \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonalised\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cdirect to individual\u8221\'20\'1D targeting of employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s harder to detect these threats when it\u8217\'20\'19s happening on an employee\u8217\'20\'19s personal system, right? It\u8217\'20\'19s outside a corporate network,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe whole personnel piece has become one of the major themes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  had also noticed more extortion attacks targeting smaller players not directly in the defence supply chain, he said, such as companies making cars or ball bearings.\par \par \uc2 A recent attack by a group linked to Russian intelligence indicates how broad the net has become. Hackers appeared to try to steal information by spoofing the websites of hundreds of leading defence contractors from the UK, the US, Germany, France, Sweden, Norway, Ukraine, Turkey, and South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Russia has also developed specific hacks to compromise the Signal and \uc2 Telegram\uc2  accounts of Ukrainian military personnel, as well as journalists and public officials, using methods and vulnerabilities that \uc2 Google\uc2  says other attackers could adopt.\par \par \uc2 Hackers have also mounted extremely targeted attacks against Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s frontline drone units by impersonating Ukrainian drone builders or drone training courses.\par \par \uc2 Dr Ilona Khmeleva, the secretary of the Economic Security Council of Ukraine, said that many cyber-attacks against Ukrainian military personnel were individualised, with some potential targets monitored for weeks before an attack.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian authorities have recorded a 37% increase in cyber incidents from 2024 to 2025, she said.\par \par \uc2 Beyond Europe, other groups are using similar tactics to target defence suppliers. More and more, these efforts focus on people who are trying to get jobs in defence, or vulnerabilities in the hiring processes of large companies.\par \par \uc2 North Korean hackers have impersonated corporate recruiters in campaigns against leading defence contractors, using AI to extensively profile employees, their roles and their potential salaries to \u8220\'20\'1Cidentify potential targets for initial compromise\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Many of these campaigns have been extremely successful. Last summer, the US justice department found that North Koreans had managed to obtain jobs as \u8220\'20\'1Cremote IT workers\u8221\'20\'1D for more than 100 US companies. US authorities alleged they were doing this to fund the North Korean government by collecting salaries and, in some cases, stealing cryptocurrency.\par \par \uc2 Iranian state-sponsored groups have created spoof job portals and sent out fake job offers to obtain the credentials of defence firms and drone companies.\par \par \uc2 A group called APT5, linked to China, has targeted employees of aerospace and defence companies with emails and messaging tailored to their geographical location, personal life and professional roles.\par \par \uc2 For example, parents of young children received fake communications from the \uc2 Boy Scouts of America\uc2 , or from a nearby secondary school; residents of certain US states received fake information about the 2024 election. Employees of important companies were also sent fake invitations to events including Red Cross training courses and a national security conference in Canada.\par \par \uc2 Khmeleva said: \u8220\'20\'1CAs western technologies and investments are integrated into Ukraine \u8211\'20\'13 including through military aid and joint industrial projects \u8211\'20\'13 the pool of potential victims expands beyond Ukrainian citizens.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmployees of foreign companies, contractors, engineers, and consultants involved in Ukraine-related projects may also become targets, making this a transnational security issue, not a purely national one.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260210em2a001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
