\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 San Francisco teachers begin first strike in nearly 50 years\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agency \par \uc2 589 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 February 2026\par 17:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2026.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands walk out after talks with district failed to reach agreement on wages, healthcare and resources for students with special needs\par \par \uc2 About 6,000 public schoolteachers in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/san-francisco"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 San Francisco}}}\uc2  went on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/feb/02/california-teacher-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strike}}}\uc2  on Monday, the first public schoolteachers strike in the city in nearly 50 years.\par \par \uc2 The strike comes after teachers and the district failed to reach an agreement over higher wages, health benefits and more resources for special needs students. The San Francisco Unified School District closed all its 120 schools and said it would offer independent study to some of the district\u8217\'20\'19s 50,000 students.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will continue to stand together until we win the schools our students deserve and the contracts our members deserve,\u8221\'20\'1D Cassondra Curiel, president of the United Educators of San Francisco, said at a Monday morning news conference.\par \par \uc2 Teachers with the union were joining the picket line after last-ditch negotiations over the weekend failed to reach a new contract. \uc2 Daniel Lurie\uc2 , the city\u8217\'20\'19s mayor, and Nancy Pelosi,a US representative of California, had urged the two sides to keep talking rather than shut down schools.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders planned to hold a news conference on Monday morning about the strike and an afternoon rally was planned at San Francisco City Hall. Negotiations were scheduled to resume at midday on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The union and the district have been negotiating for nearly a year, with teachers demanding fully funded family healthcare, salary raises and the filling of vacant positions impacting special education and services.\par \par \uc2 The teachers also want the district to enact policies to support homeless and immigrant students and families.\par \par \uc2 Teachers and the district have already reached an agreement on stronger sanctuary policies, but they remain far apart on wages.\par \par \uc2 The union is asking for a 9% raise over two years. SFUSD, which faces a $100m deficit and is under state oversight because of a longstanding financial crisis, said it could not afford those hikes. The teachers say the money could come from reserve funds that could be directed back to classrooms and school sites.\par \par \uc2 Officials have countered with a 6% wage increase paid over three years. On Friday, Maria Su, superintendent of the San Francisco Unified School District, said the offer also includes bonuses for all employees if there is a surplus by the 2027-28 school year.\par \par \uc2 A report by a neutral fact-finding panel released last week recommended a compromise of a 6% increase over two years, largely siding with the district\u8217\'20\'19s arguments that it is financially constrained.\par \par \uc2 The union said San Francisco teachers receive some of the lowest contributions to their healthcare costs in the Bay Area, pushing many to leave. Su said the district offered two options: the district paying 75% of family health coverage at Kaiser or offering an annual allowance of $24,000 for teachers to choose their healthcare plan.\par \par \uc2 Lurie, who helped broker an agreement that ended a hotel workers union strike after he was elected and before taking office, said that the city agencies were coordinating with the district on how to offer support to children and their families.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know everyone participating in these negotiations is committed to schools where students thrive and our educators feel truly supported, and I will continue working to ensure that,\u8221\'20\'1D Lurie said in a social media post on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The last time teachers walked out, in 1979, it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/1979/10/24/archives/san-francisco-teachers-end-walkout-in-7th-week-agreement-to-rehire.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than six weeks}}}\uc2  before classes resumed, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://missionlocal.org/2026/02/sfusd-teachers-strike-school-district-explained/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mission Local reported}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020260209em290043a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
